window.GUIDE_SEARCH=[{"id": 1, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What is RAG, and where does Ragnor fit in the complete journey?", "text": "What is RAG, and where does Ragnor fit in the complete journey? Retrieval-Augmented Generation combines externally retrieved evidence with a language model's generation. A user question becomes a search request; relevant passages become context; a generator writes an answer conditioned on that context. Ragnor implements reusable ingestion, retrieval, evidence, and operational pieces. A host still supplies the conversational application and its answer-generation policy. Separate the offline path, which builds a searchable corpus, from the online path, which retrieves evidence for a question. This lets a source change trigger indexing without retraining the language model. The original RAG paper combines parametric model memory with a retrieved non-parametric index; this repository is an engineering subsystem, not a reproduction of that training architecture. Retrieval can miss the right passage, and a generator can ignore a correct passage. The evidence assembler exposes explicit failure states and citations, but cannot prove every generated claim is true. External knowledge improves freshness and traceability at the cost of ingestion, search latency, access control, and evaluation work. Small stable tasks may need only a prompt or structured database query. Trace one document through ingestion and one question through search. Draw the boundary where your own generator must be called."}, {"id": 2, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "Why can an LLM sound confident while being wrong?", "text": "Why can an LLM sound confident while being wrong? A Large Language Model predicts token sequences using learned patterns. Fluent continuation and factual verification are different capabilities. If the prompt lacks the relevant fact, or includes ambiguous evidence, a plausible continuation can still be false. Confidence in the prose is not a calibrated probability that the claim is correct. In this project, the retrieval and evidence layers supply inspectable material before a host invokes generation. EvidenceStatus distinguishes missing evidence from a failed backend, giving the application a basis for abstaining or asking a follow-up question. Presenting a citation does not establish that the citation supports the attached sentence. Ragnor preserves evidence identity, while claim-to-evidence checking and appropriate abstention remain host responsibilities. Restricting answers to supplied evidence improves auditability but can reduce coverage. Allowing general model knowledge expands coverage while weakening the promise that every answer reflects the indexed corpus. Ask one answerable, one ambiguous, and one unanswerable question. Write separate expected behaviors before evaluating any generated output."}, {"id": 3, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "How are search, retrieval, and generation different responsibilities?", "text": "How are search, retrieval, and generation different responsibilities? Search ranks records against a query. Retrieval includes query preparation, backend selection, constraint enforcement, candidate fetching, and optional reranking. Generation turns the resulting evidence and instructions into a new answer. Treating these as one function hides whether an error came from missing data, ranking, or answer synthesis. HybridRetriever and the strategy classes orchestrate search; EvidenceAssembler builds a bounded evidence bundle. Their return types are useful without a chat model. This separation also supports source verification tools and ordinary search interfaces. A fallback that silently generates when search fails can convert an infrastructure outage into misinformation. Use strict retrieval and propagate backend failure into the host's response state. Explicit stages require more wiring and more status handling. They also let you benchmark retrieval independently and switch generation providers without rebuilding the corpus. Design three response payloads: successful evidence, successful empty search, and backend failure. Explain which may safely reach generation."}, {"id": 4, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "When should I use RAG instead of fine-tuning or a SQL query?", "text": "When should I use RAG instead of fine-tuning or a SQL query? Use retrieval when answers depend on a changing collection and need source attribution. Use a structured query when the task is exact aggregation over authoritative fields. Consider fine-tuning when the persistent problem is behavior, style, or task adaptation rather than missing up-to-date facts. These approaches can be combined. Ragnor's document and evidence abstractions suit passage retrieval. A question such as total approved invoices this month should usually reach a database computation; nearest passages cannot guarantee an exact total. Fine-tuning is not implemented by this subsystem. A semantic similarity score can make an approximate answer look exact. The host must route tasks appropriately and preserve authorization on both document search and structured tools. Task routing adds complexity but avoids forcing every question through a vector index. A single retrieval path is easier to prototype and easier to misuse. Classify ten realistic product questions into passage retrieval, database computation, general explanation, or behavior adaptation. Defend two borderline cases."}, {"id": 5, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What is the difference between a document, a chunk, a vector, and evidence?", "text": "What is the difference between a document, a chunk, a vector, and evidence? A document is a source-level unit of text and metadata. A chunk is a searchable subdivision. An embedding vector is a numerical representation of that subdivision. Evidence is selected, attributed text prepared for a consuming model or user. These objects have different lifetimes and should not share one overloaded identity field. The canonical Chunk, Document, and SearchResult dataclasses separate ingestion and search shapes; evidence types add revision, source, span, and citation information. A chunk hit becomes useful evidence only after selection and attribution. Confusing a chunk ID with a document ID breaks deletion, citation lookup, and version switching. Ragnor carries metadata through the pipeline, but hosts must supply stable source identities and meaningful provenance. More explicit types require conversion at boundaries. They reduce accidental field loss and make changes to storage independent of the user-facing citation contract. Annotate a sample result with its source ID, document ID, revision, chunk ID, text, vector dimension, and evidence label. Identify which fields can change after reindexing."}, {"id": 6, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What do dense and sparse retrieval mean?", "text": "What do dense and sparse retrieval mean? Dense retrieval compares learned vectors, making semantic paraphrases discoverable. Sparse or lexical retrieval scores token-based signals, making exact names, identifiers, and unusual terms discoverable. They capture different notions of relevance. Neither should be described as understanding the user's authorization or guaranteeing truth. Ragnor separates VectorIndex and LexicalIndex capabilities and composes them in HybridRetrieval. PostgreSQL offers native full-text search; some other adapters approximate lexical ranking with token overlap. Those are different implementations, not equivalent language models. Dense retrieval can confuse related concepts; lexical retrieval can miss synonyms. Hybrid fusion reduces dependence on one signal but adds its own ranking choices and cannot recover absent source material. Two signals increase implementation and evaluation cost. A keyword-only strategy can be preferable for exact support codes or a prototype with no embedding service. Create queries containing a product code, a paraphrase, and a misspelling. Predict which retrieval stage should help, then inspect both ranked lists."}, {"id": 7, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What does grounding actually guarantee?", "text": "What does grounding actually guarantee? Grounding means giving an answer process explicit evidence it can use. It is a relationship between a claim and supporting material, not a property conferred by vector search alone. A grounded workflow must preserve provenance, show the relevant revision, and handle insufficient evidence honestly. EvidenceAssembler deduplicates, merges eligible spans, budgets rendered context, and creates citation labels. SourceLookup can retrieve a bounded span from a retained revision. These mechanisms make verification possible rather than automatically performing every verification step. A wrong retrieved passage, stale document, or unsupported inference can survive into the answer. Host-side citation checks and evaluation are still necessary; delimiter escaping is only one mitigation against untrusted source instructions. Strong attribution needs snapshot retention and richer metadata. Minimal text-only search is cheaper but cannot support reliable historical verification. Write an answer with three factual claims. Map each claim to an exact evidence span; flag any sentence whose support requires an unstated inference."}, {"id": 8, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "How do relevance, correctness, faithfulness, and freshness differ?", "text": "How do relevance, correctness, faithfulness, and freshness differ? Relevance asks whether material addresses the question. Correctness asks whether the claim is true. Faithfulness asks whether the answer follows the provided evidence. Freshness asks whether the evidence reflects an appropriate time or revision. An answer can be faithful to an obsolete policy and therefore be incorrect for today's user. The project separates retrieval evaluation from answer evaluation and carries freshness and revision fields in evidence. These signals allow a host to assess different failure modes rather than collapse quality into a single score. High retrieval recall does not establish answer correctness. A heuristic overlap score also cannot establish that a reasoning step is valid or that the source itself is authoritative. Multiple metrics complicate release decisions but expose regressions that one average hides. The right acceptance criteria depend on the product's questions and corpus. Construct four answers that each fail a different dimension. Explain which measurement and which code boundary would reveal each failure."}, {"id": 9, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What are the main cost and latency stages in a RAG request?", "text": "What are the main cost and latency stages in a RAG request? Typical stages are query transformation, query embedding, candidate search, fusion, reranking, evidence assembly, and host generation. Ingestion has separate extraction, chunking, embedding, and storage costs. The relative expense depends on providers, data size, network placement, and configuration; this repository does not establish universal timings. Separating candidate_k from final_k permits a larger search pool without necessarily sending every hit to generation. Telemetry records stage events and durations, and the embedding executor bounds concurrency and provider call duration when configured. Increasing query variants, candidate counts, and retries together can multiply load. No single semaphore controls an entire fleet or gives an end-to-end request deadline. Higher candidate recall often costs more latency and money. Optimize against a quality target using measured stage distributions, not guessed millisecond claims. Create a latency budget spreadsheet with p50 and p95 per stage and an overall deadline. Mark every number as a hypothesis until you measure it."}, {"id": 10, "chapter": "RAG foundations", "slug": "foundations", "title": "What does domain-agnostic mean, and what does it not solve?", "text": "What does domain-agnostic mean, and what does it not solve? Domain-agnostic means the core can work with different application schemas, sources, settings, and storage implementations through explicit interfaces. It does not mean every domain has identical chunking needs, permissions, legal retention rules, or quality thresholds. Those policies remain part of the host's product design. Ragnor receives settings, sessions, version storage, embedders, and retrieval strategies rather than importing a host's models. The same core can serve a policy corpus or a support corpus while their adapters and evaluation datasets differ. Generic abstractions can hide important domain constraints if the host never models them. A tenant filter is useful only when trusted identity selects the correct scope and every storage path respects it. Reusability reduces coupling but requires deliberate integration work. A specialized application may be quicker initially and harder to reuse safely later. Compare an HR policy assistant and a public FAQ assistant. List five policies that differ even if they use identical Ragnor components."}, {"id": 11, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "Why is this project a library subsystem rather than a complete application?", "text": "Why is this project a library subsystem rather than a complete application? The package supplies composable RAG infrastructure while the host owns application models, authentication, routing, deployment, and generation. That boundary allows an existing backend to adopt retrieval without letting a library control its domain schema or request lifecycle. The package declares no mandatory runtime dependencies and exposes protocols and optional adapters. Builder defaults support local experimentation; production integration can inject explicit stores and sessions. This is evidence of a dependency boundary, not evidence that competing frameworks are inherently unsuitable. Integration errors move to the seams: missing scope policy, incorrect transaction ownership, or a fake embedder accidentally used for real search. Examples and contract tests help, but the host must review its wiring. You gain control and portability while doing more assembly. A packaged application would offer faster initial setup with more predetermined choices. Draw a ports-and-adapters diagram showing which side owns users, SQL tables, provider credentials, evidence, and chat responses."}, {"id": 12, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "How does dependency inversion appear in the repository?", "text": "How does dependency inversion appear in the repository? High-level orchestration depends on contracts for chunking, embeddings, storage, sessions, and version records. Concrete adapters fulfill those contracts. This reverses the dependency that would otherwise make ingestion import an application's ORM models and global settings. IngestionPipeline accepts its collaborators in the constructor. VersionStore receives the active session for mutations, and SQLFactory abstracts database operations. You can replace a collaborator with a controlled test implementation without rewriting orchestration. An adapter can satisfy a Python method signature while violating atomicity or isolation semantics. Structural compatibility is weaker than behavioral compatibility, so tests need to exercise the promised guarantees. Interfaces add indirection and documentation work. They pay off when deployment stacks differ or when failure paths need deterministic testing. Replace version storage with a tiny recording fake. Verify the order of find, close, create, delete, and upsert calls without assuming that call order alone proves rollback safety."}, {"id": 13, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "Why use Protocol instead of requiring every adapter to inherit a base class?", "text": "Why use Protocol instead of requiring every adapter to inherit a base class? Python Protocol supports structural typing: an object can satisfy a contract by providing the required members. This is useful when a host already has settings or session providers and should not need to inherit library-specific classes. RagSettings and several storage seams express the attributes or methods consumers need. Some interfaces also use runtime_checkable, but runtime checks do not fully validate annotations, returned values, or transactional behavior. Duck typing can postpone integration errors until execution. Validate configuration at startup and run adapter contract tests; do not interpret isinstance against a protocol as a complete certification. Protocols reduce inheritance coupling while offering less shared implementation. Abstract base classes remain useful when construction rules or common behavior must be enforced. Supply a dataclass as settings, then remove a required attribute. Compare static type feedback with the eventual runtime failure."}, {"id": 14, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "What does the Fluent Builder pattern contribute here?", "text": "What does the Fluent Builder pattern contribute here? RagnorBuilder assembles collaborators through readable configuration methods and an asynchronous build step. The local constructor chooses in-memory defaults while still requiring an embedding function. The result is a convenient facade over the lower-level components. This keeps a small experiment short while allowing chunkers, stores, version stores, and settings to be replaced. The builder is composition convenience; it does not make an in-memory setup durable or create a learned local embedding model. Hidden defaults can be carried into production. Review the resolved store, embedding dimension, scope policy, and state retention before deploying an instance built for local use. A builder is easier to discover than a large constructor but adds another assembly surface to maintain. Direct constructors are clearer for unusual production wiring. Run the offline quickstart, then rewrite its setup with explicit collaborators. Describe which defaults you would replace for a multi-worker service."}, {"id": 15, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "How are Factory, Registry, Strategy, and Adapter different patterns?", "text": "How are Factory, Registry, Strategy, and Adapter different patterns? A factory constructs a collaborator. A registry maps names to implementations. A strategy selects interchangeable behavior such as dense, lexical, or hybrid retrieval. An adapter translates an external API into the project's contract. The patterns solve different problems and often appear together. Store factories and registries choose backend implementations; retrieval strategies compose search capabilities; backend classes translate vendor operations. This allows configuration-driven construction without forcing every search behavior into a storage class. A name-based registry can defer typos or missing optional dependency failures until runtime. Validate names and dependencies during startup, and avoid loading plugins based on arbitrary untrusted input. Configurable assembly improves extension but increases possible combinations. Support a documented matrix rather than assuming every registered backend supports every strategy and filter. Explain how adding a new vector backend differs from adding a query reranker. Identify the construction, contract, and behavior changes separately."}, {"id": 16, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "Why split VectorStore into smaller capability protocols?", "text": "Why split VectorStore into smaller capability protocols? A fused store interface is convenient when one backend does everything. Smaller VectorIndex, LexicalIndex, fetch, and write capabilities allow strategies to use only the operations they need. A keyword-only path should not require an embedding provider merely to satisfy a broad constructor. Ragnor's retrieval strategies compose vector and lexical capabilities, permitting different backends for different signals. Capability declarations describe supported filters and guarantees before the strategy executes. A backend may advertise a capability it implements incompletely. Mandatory constraints should fail closed, and contract tests should verify semantics rather than only checking that methods exist. More interfaces create more concepts, but avoid fake implementations of unsupported methods. A single-store facade remains helpful for simple applications. Design a lexical-only adapter. List the interfaces it needs and the vector-specific methods it should never have to emulate."}, {"id": 17, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "What does SQLFactory abstract, and what remains PostgreSQL-specific?", "text": "What does SQLFactory abstract, and what remains PostgreSQL-specific? SQLFactory abstracts session construction, execution, fetching, and advisory locking. It lets orchestration avoid direct dependence on one SQL library. It does not make PostgreSQL full-text functions or pgvector operators portable to an arbitrary database engine. PgVectorStore still owns database-specific SQL. An alternative driver can implement the factory contract while preserving PostgreSQL semantics; a different database usually needs a different storage adapter and capability declaration. Replacing the driver without matching transaction and lock behavior can invalidate ingestion guarantees. A no-op advisory-lock implementation is not an equivalent production replacement. The extra seam improves driver flexibility and testability but carries semantic obligations. Direct driver calls would be simpler and more tightly coupled. Write down the minimum behavior an async database driver adapter must preserve for a locked ingestion transaction. Include failure and rollback cases."}, {"id": 18, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "Why use dataclasses and explicit converters at API boundaries?", "text": "Why use dataclasses and explicit converters at API boundaries? Dataclasses make fields, defaults, and meanings discoverable without requiring a validation framework. Explicit converters keep dictionary-based adapters compatible while typed consumers work with Document, SearchResult, RetrievalRequest, and RetrievalResponse. as_dicts and typed retrieval bridges separate legacy shapes from newer APIs. This supports gradual adoption without forcing every host and backend to migrate simultaneously. Type annotations alone do not validate hostile input, and conversions can lose fields such as reranker scores if an older result type does not represent them. Validate transport input and choose the typed response when you need its richer score information. Compatibility layers cost code and can preserve ambiguity. Removing them simplifies internals but may break downstream callers; a migration policy must be explicit. Convert one typed hit to a dictionary and back. Check identity, metadata, distance, RRF score, and reranker score separately."}, {"id": 19, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "How does the Null Object pattern appear in telemetry?", "text": "How does the Null Object pattern appear in telemetry? A NullEventSink implements the event-sink contract while doing nothing. Consumers can emit an event without checking whether telemetry is configured at every call site. This keeps optional observability out of the main control flow. Constructors select a null sink when no sink is supplied. A real sink can later export sanitized events, and the business path can retain the same call structure. A null sink can make missing production instrumentation invisible. Also inspect whether a supplied sink can raise or block; optional observability should not accidentally become an uncontrolled availability dependency. Fewer branches improve readability, while silent default behavior requires explicit operational verification. An application may choose mandatory instrumentation at its composition root. Run the same retrieval with a recording sink and a null sink. Verify equal results and inspect which dimensions the recording sink receives."}, {"id": 20, "chapter": "Architecture and design patterns", "slug": "architecture", "title": "How should I defend the architecture in an interview without inventing author intent?", "text": "How should I defend the architecture in an interview without inventing author intent? Describe observed boundaries, explain the problems they address, and distinguish inference from documented intent. For example, injected sessions support host transaction ownership; you can infer a portability benefit without claiming the author measured it against every ORM or framework. Code-backed explanations are strongest when tied to a call site, invariant, and failure scenario. The original guide is useful as a topic map, but fixed timing claims and universal concurrency claims need measurement or narrower wording. Repeating comments as guarantees can mislead. Some comments simplify current behavior: the generic hybrid strategy awaits stages sequentially, and sliding-window chunks use UUIDs rather than universally deterministic IDs. Qualified claims sound less absolute but survive technical scrutiny. A good design defense acknowledges obligations pushed to host adapters and conditions under which another design would win. Prepare a five-minute defense with three source references, two explicit limitations, and one alternative architecture you would choose under different requirements."}, {"id": 21, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "Why distinguish discovery, fetching, extraction, and ingestion?", "text": "Why distinguish discovery, fetching, extraction, and ingestion? Discovery enumerates source items. Fetching obtains bytes. Extraction converts those bytes into usable text and provenance. Ingestion chunks, embeds, and indexes the result. Keeping these boundaries separate lets the same extraction or indexing logic serve several source systems. Connector records carry discovery and load status, while RawDocument carries content and source metadata into the pipeline. A PDF extraction failure is therefore different from an empty bucket or an embedding provider outage. Flattening every failure into an empty list can cause a later reconciliation to delete healthy knowledge. The connector contract represents errors and whether a result is authoritative; the pipeline refuses non-authoritative LoadResult mutation. Rich status handling takes more code than returning strings. It supports safe retries, deletion decisions, and actionable diagnostics. Trace a PDF in an object bucket through all four stages. Specify the returned status when each stage fails and which stages can be retried independently."}, {"id": 22, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "What makes a source scan authoritative?", "text": "What makes a source scan authoritative? An authoritative scan represents the complete intended snapshot under its configured scope and traversal rules. A successful first page is not a complete snapshot. Authority also depends on whether errors, limits, or missing pages could have hidden existing documents. LoadResult distinguishes completeness and status; sync uses that authority to infer removals. The ordinary ingestion pipeline skips a non-authoritative LoadResult rather than replacing its current indexed content with a partial view. Treating a timeout or permission error as authoritative emptiness can erase knowledge. Conversely, never trusting a complete empty scan leaves deleted content searchable. Both require explicit semantics. Conservative authority rules may delay deletion after a source outage. That delay is preferable to interpreting an incomplete traversal as proof of absence, but access revocation needs a separate immediate control. Simulate a three-page source where page two fails. Explain why the successfully fetched documents do not prove that all missing documents were deleted."}, {"id": 23, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "How should pagination and checkpoints work for incremental ingestion?", "text": "How should pagination and checkpoints work for incremental ingestion? A cursor identifies where enumeration should continue; a checkpoint records safely processed progress. They are not interchangeable. Saving a cursor before the corresponding data is activated can skip work after a crash, while saving too late can repeat work. Connectors expose page and full-load methods, and sync checkpoints are guarded by the current fencing token. Idempotency and complete-snapshot reconciliation make repeated pages safer than silently losing them. Sources may mutate between pages, causing duplicates or omissions even if every request succeeds. Stable ordering, source-side snapshot tokens where available, deduplication, and periodic full reconciliation are integration concerns. Full scans simplify reconciliation but cost more source I/O. Incremental feeds are faster but require careful resume and deletion semantics. Draw crashes immediately before activation, after activation, and after checkpoint saving. State which records will repeat and why no record should be skipped."}, {"id": 24, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "How does the directory connector reduce filesystem risks?", "text": "How does the directory connector reduce filesystem risks? Directory ingestion must constrain traversal to an intended root, apply inclusion policies, bound resource use, and avoid unsafe path or symlink behavior. The connector implements explicit open, target, and read helpers rather than blindly opening every discovered string. The directory adapter also records intentional skips separately from scan errors. That distinction preserves the meaning of completeness: a deliberately excluded file is not the same as an inaccessible file that should have been included. A local filesystem is not automatically trusted. Permission changes and concurrent file replacement can occur during scanning, and host deployment permissions still determine what the process can access. Strict traversal and file policies can exclude legitimate sources and need configuration. Broad scanning is easier to start and harder to secure or make reproducible. Read the directory connector's _open and _target helpers. Build a small temporary corpus containing ignored files, nested directories, and a symlink, and predict the reported skips."}, {"id": 25, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "Why does an HTTP connector need SSRF protection?", "text": "Why does an HTTP connector need SSRF protection? Server-Side Request Forgery occurs when a server fetches a destination chosen through untrusted input and reaches internal services or metadata endpoints. A document URL can be an attack input just as an API parameter can. The HTTP connector validates destinations, pins requests to an approved public IP, disables automatic redirects, and checks redirects explicitly. It rejects attempts to turn off its network protections. Website crawling requires explicit allowed domains. Domain checking alone cannot stop DNS rebinding or redirect-based bypasses. Egress policy, deployment DNS behavior, and the actual client transport also matter. Treat source fetching as a separate trust boundary. Public-only fetching limits access to legitimate private knowledge services. Supporting private services safely requires a deliberately trusted integration rather than a blanket bypass of URL validation. Inspect the checks before and after a redirect. Explain why allowing example.com does not authorize a redirect to a loopback address."}, {"id": 26, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "How should HTTP rate limits, redirects, and partial crawls affect indexing?", "text": "How should HTTP rate limits, redirects, and partial crawls affect indexing? Rate limits and timeouts indicate unavailable or incomplete source access, not document deletion. Redirects may change origin and therefore change what credentials may safely be forwarded. Crawl limits bound work but can also prevent a complete snapshot. The connector tracks statuses such as rate-limited or incomplete traversal, checks redirect destinations, and rejects authenticated cross-origin redirects. Sync can use result authority to decide whether removal inference is permissible. Retrying an incomplete scan without bounds can overload the source; accepting it as complete can remove data. A bounded source request still needs a host scheduling and retry policy for repeated jobs. Tight crawl limits improve predictability while reducing corpus coverage. Completeness must reflect the limit rather than presenting the bounded subset as the whole source. Design a crawl that reaches its configured limit exactly. Determine from the connector's terminal state whether it is authoritative, rather than guessing from the number of returned pages."}, {"id": 27, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "What identity and consistency issues arise with object storage?", "text": "What identity and consistency issues arise with object storage? Object keys identify source items, but content can change under the same key. ETags, version identifiers, metadata, and content hashes can describe different aspects of revision identity. An ETag should not automatically be treated as a universal content checksum. ObjectStorageConnector separates listing and reading and returns provenance through RawDocument. Its full-load path must account for continuation tokens and errors before a scan can authorize removal of absent objects. Listing and fetching can observe different moments. A host needing exact historical verification should retain source versions or snapshots rather than assuming the latest object body matches a previous citation. Versioned reads and snapshot retention cost storage and integration work. Reading only current objects is simpler but weakens reproducibility. Change one object between listing and download. Describe which metadata lets you detect or explain the observed revision and which guarantees the source must provide."}, {"id": 28, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "Why is text extraction part of the retrieval quality problem?", "text": "Why is text extraction part of the retrieval quality problem? Search cannot recover information that extraction discarded or scrambled. PDF reading order, tables, headings, repeated headers, and image-only pages can distort the indexed text. Extraction quality therefore sets an upper bound on downstream retrieval quality. Extractors are separate adapters and extractor identity participates in ingestion fingerprints. Changing extraction behavior can require reindexing even if the underlying file bytes did not change. A successful parse may still yield semantically broken text. The project does not supply a universal OCR or table-understanding guarantee; inspect output and use host-selected extractors where needed. Rich document parsing improves structure at the cost of dependencies, compute, and more failure modes. Plain text extraction is easier to operate but can lose relationships that answers depend on. Extract a two-column page and a table. Compare the text to the source visually, then write a retrieval question that fails if cell relationships are lost."}, {"id": 29, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "Which source metadata must survive into evidence?", "text": "Which source metadata must survive into evidence? Preserve stable source and document identity, revision, title or section, location or offsets where available, and the relevant isolation scope. These fields support deletion, attribution, source opening, and debugging. They are not decorative labels added after search. IngestionPipeline merges document and chunk metadata and stamps the actual source name. It also forwards external_id, provenance, location, and extractor information. Evidence types then normalize attribution fields for consumers. Metadata may contain secrets or untrusted URLs. Sanitizing a displayed URI is not the same as safely fetching it, and arbitrary metadata should not automatically be logged or rendered as HTML. Rich metadata adds storage and schema complexity. Omitting it makes early indexing easy but later provenance reconstruction unreliable. Trace the metadata of two documents with different sources through one ingestion run. Verify that every chunk retains the correct source instead of inheriting one global source label."}, {"id": 30, "chapter": "Sources, connectors, and extraction", "slug": "sources", "title": "Why are watchers insufficient without reconciliation?", "text": "Why are watchers insufficient without reconciliation? A watcher provides low-latency change notifications, but notifications can be missed during downtime, buffer overflow, or process failure. A periodic reconciliation compares the source's current authoritative state against indexed state and repairs divergence. The directory module includes watcher and periodic reconciler helpers. Sync plans classify additions, changes, removals, metadata-only updates, and unchanged items so a full comparison need not re-embed everything. Assuming an event stream is complete leaves permanently stale knowledge after a missed event. Deletion events also need an explicit target; absence of an event is not proof that an item still exists. Combining watchers with scans spends extra source I/O but improves eventual correctness. Full scans alone are simpler when freshness requirements are modest. Stop the watcher, edit and delete files, then resume it. Describe how a scheduled reconciliation repairs changes that the live event stream never delivered."}, {"id": 31, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "Why chunk documents at all?", "text": "Why chunk documents at all? Chunking creates retrieval units small enough to match a question and fit a context budget. A whole manual can contain many unrelated topics; embedding it as one vector blends signals and returning all of it wastes context. A tiny fragment can lose the conditions that make its meaning correct. Ragnor injects a Chunker into ingestion. This makes chunking a replaceable strategy whose version and configuration participate in snapshot comparison, rather than an untracked preprocessing script. A boundary can separate a rule from its exception or a table heading from its values. Overlap and section-aware parsing mitigate some boundary loss, but only corpus-specific evaluation reveals whether the chosen units preserve meaning. Smaller chunks improve targeting and increase index size; larger chunks preserve context and can dilute relevance. There is no universally best chunk size. Split the same policy into small and large chunks. Use questions about exceptions and compare whether each retrieved unit contains enough information to answer safely."}, {"id": 32, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "Does SlidingWindowChunker count tokens, words, or characters?", "text": "Does SlidingWindowChunker count tokens, words, or characters? It counts whitespace-separated words using text.split(). chunk_size and overlap therefore measure words. They do not define a model tokenizer's context size, and they do not behave identically for every language or code-heavy input. The implementation steps through words by chunk_size minus overlap and rejoins each slice. This simple algorithm has no model dependency and is suitable as a transparent baseline. Treating 600 words as 600 tokens can overflow a downstream budget. Languages without whitespace and punctuation-heavy text can also produce surprising retrieval units. The evidence layer has a separate tokenizer abstraction for context accounting. Word splitting is fast and dependency-free, while token-aware or structure-aware splitting can better match actual model and document constraints. Chunk an English paragraph, a Python file, and Arabic text with identical settings. Count actual model tokens separately and compare the ratios."}, {"id": 33, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "How does overlap affect recall and ingestion cost?", "text": "How does overlap affect recall and ingestion cost? Overlap repeats material near neighboring boundaries so that a question spanning a split still has a chance to retrieve a coherent chunk. The stride is chunk_size minus overlap. With long text, the repetition factor is roughly chunk_size divided by stride, ignoring the final partial window. For size 600 and overlap 100, the stride is 500 and the approximate repeated-word factor is 1.2. This is a mathematical estimate of chunked input, not a measured provider billing factor or a quality guarantee. Excessive overlap produces near-duplicate hits and increases embedding work. Evidence deduplication and eligible span merging reduce redundant context, but they do not undo ingestion cost. More overlap can improve boundary coverage while reducing diversity among top hits. Tune against a labeled question set rather than maximizing repetition. Calculate chunk counts for 1,100 words at several overlaps, then verify them with sliding_window_chunk. Explain why the loop stops after the final covered window."}, {"id": 34, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "Why validate that overlap is smaller than chunk_size?", "text": "Why validate that overlap is smaller than chunk_size? The stride must be positive for the window to advance. An overlap equal to the chunk size creates a zero stride; a larger overlap creates an invalid or backward progression. These are algorithm validity constraints, not optional quality preferences. Both the function and the chunker constructor validate positive size and overlap in the interval from zero inclusive to size exclusive. Invalid configuration fails early before ingestion mutates versions or spends embedding calls. A malformed chunking configuration can block all ingestion or unexpectedly produce no chunks. Startup validation and focused boundary tests make such failures easier to diagnose than late failures inside a scheduled job. Strict validation rejects configurations that a permissive implementation might silently repair. Silent repair would conceal the actual indexing configuration and make snapshot identity less meaningful. Exercise zero size, negative overlap, overlap equal to size, and empty text. Distinguish invalid configuration from a valid empty result."}, {"id": 35, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "How does MarkdownSectionChunker preserve semantic structure?", "text": "How does MarkdownSectionChunker preserve semantic structure? It splits Markdown on H2 headings, reconstructs a section with its title, and delegates that section to an inner chunker. Section titles therefore participate in embedded text rather than existing only as metadata. The inner strategy still controls subdivision of a long section. Composition reuses the sliding-window algorithm while adding section, chunk index, and sub-chunk index metadata. File reading remains in the source adapter; the chunker operates on text. A regular expression for H2 headings is not a complete Markdown parser. Unusual markup and headings inside fenced code can create misleading boundaries. Evaluate actual corpus syntax before relying on section semantics. This wrapper is lightweight and understandable, while a full syntax-tree parser handles richer structure at higher implementation and dependency cost. Use a document with a preamble, H2 sections, H3 headings, and a fenced code sample containing a heading-like line. Inspect the exact emitted chunk text."}, {"id": 36, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "Are chunk IDs deterministic in this implementation?", "text": "Are chunk IDs deterministic in this implementation? Not universally. SlidingWindowChunker assigns UUIDs to Chunk.id. MarkdownSectionChunker preserves the inner ID and separately records position-based metadata such as chunk_id and chunk_index. The sync coordinator constructs generation-specific identities during preparation. Distinguishing physical IDs from logical or positional metadata prevents assumptions that every reindex will reuse the same vector-store row key. Different paths serve different versioning and visibility models. Conflating position metadata with the actual store ID can break deduplication, deletion, and citation mapping. Comments about position-based chunk identifiers should be interpreted in the relevant adapter context, not generalized to every chunker. Random physical IDs avoid some collisions but need lifecycle tracking. Deterministic IDs simplify repeat writes but must include enough scope and revision information to avoid accidental replacement. Chunk identical text twice and compare Chunk.id with metadata.chunk_id. Then inspect how sync adds scope and generation to its prepared records."}, {"id": 37, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "Why must chunker configuration be part of an ingestion fingerprint?", "text": "Why must chunker configuration be part of an ingestion fingerprint? The same source text produces a different index when chunk size, overlap, section handling, or algorithm version changes. Hashing only file content would incorrectly classify that index as current and skip the required rebuild. The snapshot builder records chunker identity, version, and sanitized configuration alongside source and embedding information. snapshot_matches therefore represents compatibility of the indexing process rather than only equality of input bytes. A custom chunker that hides meaningful configuration can keep stale vectors active after a behavior change. A version property is useful only if maintainers update it when output semantics change. Detailed fingerprints trigger more rebuilds and improve reproducibility. Minimal fingerprints save work while making silent configuration drift more likely. Ingest unchanged text, change overlap, and ingest again. Verify that the snapshot changes even though the source hash remains equal."}, {"id": 38, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "What would parent-child or hierarchical retrieval add?", "text": "What would parent-child or hierarchical retrieval add? Parent-child retrieval searches fine-grained child chunks, then expands selected hits into a larger parent section or document for context. Hierarchical retrieval can first choose a topic or summary and then search within it. These are design extensions, not a default pipeline implemented by this project. Ragnor's metadata, fetch capabilities, and evidence assembly seams provide useful building blocks, but a host must implement expansion, identity mapping, authorization, and evaluation. Expansion should occur before final budgeting so its actual rendered size is counted. Parent expansion can expose unauthorized neighboring text or overwhelm context. A child being authorized does not automatically authorize every parent span or sibling chunk. More context may resolve ambiguity while increasing latency and irrelevant material. A simple section-aware chunker may be sufficient for a small structured corpus. Design an extension that expands a hit to its section. State which metadata is required and where scope checks and budget checks must run."}, {"id": 39, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "How should chunking change for code, tables, and multilingual documents?", "text": "How should chunking change for code, tables, and multilingual documents? Use boundaries that preserve the relationships questions depend on: functions and classes for code, row and column context for tables, and language-appropriate segmentation for text. A word window is a baseline, not proof that all these formats retain their semantics. The Chunker protocol lets a host substitute a parser-based strategy without changing the entire ingestion pipeline. Metadata can preserve symbols, table identifiers, or locations for evidence presentation. Combining unrelated rows or truncating a function's preconditions can generate a misleading answer from faithfully quoted text. Arbitrary structure metadata also needs stable versioning and source lookup support. Specialized chunkers improve some domains while increasing maintenance and creating less comparable retrieval units. Maintain a shared evaluation interface across them. Build three questions that require a function's error branch, a table's unit header, and a multilingual synonym. Compare a structural chunker with the word-window baseline."}, {"id": 40, "chapter": "Chunking and document structure", "slug": "chunking", "title": "How do I tune chunking scientifically?", "text": "How do I tune chunking scientifically? Hold the corpus, query set, embedding profile, backend settings, and evaluation procedure fixed while varying chunking. Measure retrieval recall and rank, evidence fit, answer faithfulness, index size, and embedding volume. Record configurations so the result is reproducible. Ragnor separates chunking from embedding and evaluation, making controlled experiments possible. Its snapshots help identify configuration changes, but they are not an experiment tracker by themselves. Optimizing on a handful of visible questions overfits the benchmark. Split tuning and held-out questions and include boundary cases, exact identifiers, long sections, and unanswerable queries. A full grid search costs indexing time. Start with a small set of plausible configurations and investigate failure examples before expanding the search. Compare three sizes with two overlaps and publish a table containing quality, indexed chunk count, and estimated embedding input. Explain the winning configuration's remaining failure cases."}, {"id": 41, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "What is an embedding, and why is its dimension not enough to identify it?", "text": "What is an embedding, and why is its dimension not enough to identify it? An embedding maps input text to a vector whose geometry supports a similarity task. Dimension is the number of coordinates, not an identity for the learned space. Two providers can both return 1,536 numbers while placing meanings in incompatible coordinate systems. EmbeddingProfile records provider, endpoint, model, revision, dimension, normalization, metric, task mode, and preprocessing configuration. Its canonical hash gives the indexing configuration a stable identity that can be carried into snapshots and caches. Mixing incompatible document and query vectors can produce plausible but meaningless nearest neighbors without a shape error. Dimension validation catches only one class of incompatibility. Rich profile identity makes model migrations safer but requires reindexing discipline and additional metadata. Reusing an index based only on equal dimension is cheaper and unreliable. Define two profiles with the same dimension and different models. Explain why a successful database insert would not prove they can share one search space."}, {"id": 42, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "Why distinguish document and query embedding modes?", "text": "Why distinguish document and query embedding modes? Some embedding models are trained asymmetrically: a short question and a longer passage use different task instructions or modes but remain in a compatible retrieval space. Symmetric models use the same treatment for both. The correct mode is part of the model's intended retrieval contract. TaskMode represents DOCUMENT, QUERY, and SYMMETRIC. Query-vector helpers can call embed_query, while document processing can use document embedding methods. Profile compatibility deliberately allows different task modes for an otherwise matching pair. A generic callable may erase mode distinctions unless the adapter implements them correctly. Also, the compatibility helper does not compare preprocessing_config, so custom instruction compatibility needs additional validation. Task-aware interfaces improve provider fidelity but add API surface. A plain callable remains convenient for simple symmetric embedders and offline demonstrations. Inspect a custom adapter's query and document paths. Verify the instructions each sends and explain whether profile equality or query compatibility is the appropriate check."}, {"id": 43, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "How do cosine, dot product, and L2 differ?", "text": "How do cosine, dot product, and L2 differ? Cosine compares direction, dot product combines alignment and magnitude, and L2 measures Euclidean separation. For unit-normalized vectors, squared L2 equals two minus twice the dot product, and dot product equals cosine similarity. Without normalization, those ranking relationships need not hold. Profile enums describe metric and normalization. Stores translate the chosen metric into their backend semantics, while result scores distinguish lower-is-better distance from higher-is-better relevance. A threshold calibrated for one metric cannot safely be reused for another. Unknown dimensions and unnormalized vectors can also make low-level helpers behave unexpectedly if callers bypass validation. Cosine often fits directional semantic comparisons; dot product may intentionally use norm information. The right choice follows the embedding model and index configuration, then empirical evaluation. Compare vectors [1,0], [2,0], and [0,1] under all three measures. Predict which pairs tie and why normalization changes the answer."}, {"id": 44, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "What validation should happen before storing embedding responses?", "text": "What validation should happen before storing embedding responses? Check one vector per input, the expected dimension, and finite numeric values. Preserve input-output order. A response with fewer vectors must fail rather than silently pair only a prefix of the chunks with embeddings. EmbeddingExecutor validates response count and geometry; IngestionPipeline uses strict zip alignment and validates each chunk vector before mutation. Preparation failures therefore occur before the version-switching critical section. Valid shape does not prove semantic quality or correct provider task mode. A constant vector can pass shape validation and still destroy retrieval usefulness, so quality evaluation remains separate. Validation costs a pass over returned coordinates but prevents durable index corruption that is expensive to diagnose later. Skipping it is only a superficial throughput optimization. Use fake providers returning too few vectors, wrong dimensions, NaN, and identical finite vectors. Identify which failures validation catches and which require a retrieval test."}, {"id": 45, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "How do retries and exponential backoff help without fixing every outage?", "text": "How do retries and exponential backoff help without fixing every outage? Retries can recover transient network, rate-limit, and service failures. Exponential backoff delays repeated attempts; jitter reduces synchronized retry bursts. Permanent authentication, configuration, and dimension errors should fail promptly because identical retries are unlikely to help. EmbeddingExecutor classifies exceptions, honors exposed Retry-After hints, applies a retry ceiling, and validates responses. Cancellation is propagated rather than treated as another retryable failure. Classification includes message-pattern heuristics and treats unknown errors as transient. Misclassification can waste calls, and Retry-After can extend operation duration. The host still needs an overall deadline and provider-specific error handling where necessary. More retries improve recovery probability while increasing tail latency, cost, and load during an outage. Bound them against the request's purpose and budget. Simulate two transient failures followed by success, then a permanent failure. Record attempts and delays using injected clock and sleep functions."}, {"id": 46, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "What do concurrency limits, queue bounds, and backpressure actually mean here?", "text": "What do concurrency limits, queue bounds, and backpressure actually mean here? A semaphore limits simultaneous in-flight provider operations for one executor instance. Backpressure makes callers wait or reject work when capacity is saturated. A bounded waiting queue additionally caps how much pending work can accumulate; it is a separate property. The inspected executor constructs an asyncio.Semaphore and batches calls. Its docstring mentions a bounded input queue, but the constructor does not expose a separate queue-capacity setting. Do not infer a durable or fleet-wide queue from that comment. Many waiting tasks or many process-local executors can still overwhelm memory or a shared provider quota. The host needs admission control and, when appropriate, a bounded job queue and shared quota policy. Small concurrency limits protect providers and increase waiting latency. Larger values improve throughput until the actual service or connection pool saturates. Launch many calls through one executor and then through several instances. Measure in-flight operations and explain why a process-local semaphore is not a global rate limiter."}, {"id": 47, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "How does embedding caching preserve isolation and compatibility?", "text": "How does embedding caching preserve isolation and compatibility? Cache identity combines isolation scope, content hash, embedding profile hash, and task mode. Reusing a vector is safe only when these semantic inputs match the deployment's policy. A content hash alone is insufficient for model changes or tenant isolation. EmbeddingCache uses scoped keys by default, copies vectors on storage and retrieval, supports LRU capacity and optional TTL, and requires explicit opt-in for cross-tenant sharing. It is an in-process component, not a distributed cache. Cross-tenant reuse can leak equality signals or complicate retention obligations. The class is not thread-safe, and TTL expiry alone is not immediate revocation. The host must align cache scope and purge behavior with its privacy policy. More isolation reduces hit rate while keeping independent data lifecycles manageable. Shared caching saves calls but needs a justified policy. Cache identical content in two scopes with different profiles and task modes. Verify the allowed hits and test scope invalidation."}, {"id": 48, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "How should an embedding model migration be rolled out?", "text": "How should an embedding model migration be rolled out? Treat a vector-space change as a new index generation. Build and validate new vectors without mixing them into the old query space, evaluate quality, activate the new generation, and retain a rollback plan. Query embeddings must switch compatibly with the active documents. profiles_require_reindex compares canonical hashes, while ingestion snapshots and sync generation mechanisms support detecting configuration changes and controlling visibility. They provide building blocks, not a complete deployment orchestrator for every backend. Switching only the query model can silently degrade retrieval. Reusing caches or thresholds calibrated for the old profile can also invalidate conclusions even if dimensions remain equal. Parallel generations need extra storage and embedding cost but permit verification before cutover. In-place replacement is cheaper and harder to roll back safely. Write a migration checklist with a shadow evaluation, profile hash check, activation condition, and rollback trigger. State how queries choose the active profile."}, {"id": 49, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "Why can a hashing embedder demonstrate plumbing but not semantic intelligence?", "text": "Why can a hashing embedder demonstrate plumbing but not semantic intelligence? The quickstart hashes tokens into a small vector and normalizes it. Shared words produce shared coordinates, making an offline demonstration deterministic and cheap. It has no learned semantic representation and cannot reliably associate paraphrases that share little vocabulary. RagnorBuilder.local requires an injected embedder; core does not ship pretrained neural weights. The example deliberately supplies a fake so build, ingest, and search can run without API keys or external services. Treating success on that example as proof of production retrieval quality confuses system integration with representation learning. Hash collisions can also make unrelated text appear close in a tiny space. A fake is excellent for controlled tests and onboarding, while a learned provider is needed to evaluate realistic semantic behavior. Keep those purposes distinct. Search with a synonym-only paraphrase and compare it to an exact-word query. Explain the difference using the hashing algorithm rather than attributing it to an LLM."}, {"id": 50, "chapter": "Embeddings and resilient execution", "slug": "embeddings", "title": "How should I measure embedding throughput and cost responsibly?", "text": "How should I measure embedding throughput and cost responsibly? Track documents and tokens processed, batches, retries, cache hits, provider latency, and validation failures. Estimate total spend using actual provider usage where available; heuristic token estimates are useful for scheduling but are not billing records. EmbeddingMetrics and telemetry support operational accounting, while the executor splits batches and can enforce a soft estimated-input cap. Separating preparation from transactions prevents slow provider calls from unnecessarily occupying ingestion locks. Average latency hides retries and tail behavior. A low per-batch cost can become expensive when overlap, query expansion, and repeated full indexing multiply input volume. Detailed metrics improve tuning while high-cardinality labels and raw text logging create overhead and privacy risks. Prefer aggregated, sanitized dimensions. Compare a cold ingestion with an unchanged-source rerun and a model-change rerun. Attribute work saved by snapshot skipping separately from cache hits."}, {"id": 51, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "What does a vector database store besides vectors?", "text": "What does a vector database store besides vectors? A useful retrieval record contains identity, text or a text reference, a vector, and filterable metadata. Production systems also need revision and scope information, deletion semantics, and an explicit persistence model. A fast nearest-neighbor API alone does not provide those guarantees. Ragnor's store contracts combine or separate writing, vector search, lexical search, fetch, and deletion. Backend capability declarations describe which operations and constraints the adapter actually supports. An index that returns similar vectors but cannot enforce a required tenant filter is unsuitable for that request. Capability validation rejects unsupported mandatory constraints instead of silently ignoring them. A general-purpose database can unify transactional metadata and vectors; a specialist engine can offer different scaling and operational features. Compare the adapter's concrete guarantees rather than category labels. Create a decision matrix for persistence, filters, lexical search, transactions, generation visibility, and deletion latency for two candidate backends."}, {"id": 52, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "Why use PostgreSQL with pgvector and full-text search?", "text": "Why use PostgreSQL with pgvector and full-text search? PostgreSQL can combine relational filters, vector distance, lexical ranking, and host-managed transactions in one database. This is attractive when the application already depends on PostgreSQL and wants retrieval to participate in existing data governance. PgVectorStore builds table-agnostic SQL from validated identifiers and uses database-specific search operations. The host supplies schema and sessions; the adapter does not turn every database into PostgreSQL or own every application migration. Database extension setup, indexes, pool sizing, and query plans still need operational care. The presence of pgvector does not prove that an approximate index is configured or used for a particular query. One database reduces cross-system coordination but couples workloads and capacity. A separate vector service isolates scaling while introducing another consistency and authorization boundary. Explain what an EXPLAIN plan would need to show before you claim a vector query uses an ANN index. Keep that distinct from the Python adapter's API."}, {"id": 53, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "What is ANN, and how is it different from exact nearest-neighbor search?", "text": "What is ANN, and how is it different from exact nearest-neighbor search? Approximate Nearest Neighbor search trades guaranteed exact ranking for faster search over large vector collections. Exact search compares against all eligible vectors and provides a useful correctness baseline. Algorithms such as HNSW and IVF have different memory, tuning, and recall behavior. Ragnor delegates vector operations to backends. An in-memory scan is useful for small controlled examples; a production backend may use an approximate index depending on its configuration and query plan. ANN is not universally forced by this library. ANN can miss relevant candidates before reranking ever sees them. Metadata filters and small candidate pools can interact with index behavior, so recall must be measured under actual constraints. Higher search effort usually improves recall and costs latency or memory. Exact search may be sufficient for small corpora or useful as an offline oracle. Compare approximate results with exact results on the same authorized subset. Plot recall versus latency while varying backend search effort."}, {"id": 54, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "Why are native lexical search and token-overlap fallback not equivalent?", "text": "Why are native lexical search and token-overlap fallback not equivalent? PostgreSQL full-text search provides language configuration, query parsing, and ranking facilities. The shared fallback tokenizes lowercase ASCII alphanumeric sequences and scores query-token overlap. It is a lightweight approximation, not BM25 and not equivalent multilingual search. The scoring helper makes non-PostgreSQL backends usable in hybrid examples while capability declarations distinguish native and approximate lexical support. This preserves a common result shape without pretending the underlying ranking models are identical. Non-ASCII text and linguistic variation may behave poorly in the fallback. An evaluation that covers only English exact-word examples can miss this limitation. A portable fallback avoids another search service, while native full-text infrastructure generally offers richer lexical behavior. Choose according to corpus language and quality requirements. Tokenize an Arabic query and an English product code using the fallback. Inspect the actual tokens before interpreting any hybrid score."}, {"id": 55, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "How does the project prevent SQL injection in dynamic queries?", "text": "How does the project prevent SQL injection in dynamic queries? SQL values use bound parameters, while table and column names require separate identifier validation because SQL drivers cannot bind identifiers as ordinary values. Ragnor validates dynamic names before interpolation into backend SQL. The validation helper restricts identifiers to a simple allowlisted syntax. PgVectorStore applies validation when constructing operations and keeps values in parameter dictionaries. This is safer than escaping arbitrary user-provided SQL fragments. A valid identifier is not proof of authorization to access that table. Hosts must select trusted schema configuration and limit database permissions. Application transport validation also remains necessary. Strict names exclude qualified or unusual identifiers unless explicitly supported. That constraint simplifies safe SQL construction at the cost of some schema flexibility. Compare a malicious value, a malicious table name, and a syntactically valid but unauthorized table name. Explain which layer must stop each one."}, {"id": 56, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "What does session injection buy, and when does atomicity fail?", "text": "What does session injection buy, and when does atomicity fail? Session injection lets several operations join a transaction owned by the host or pipeline. When version mutations and vector writes truly use the same transactional database session, rollback can preserve the previous active state if a write fails. IngestionPipeline forwards the critical session into version storage and store upsert. Its guarantee depends on both adapters honoring that session. A remote vector service or in-memory store may not participate in the SQL transaction. Passing a session argument that an adapter ignores does not create cross-system atomicity. The sync path instead uses prepared generations and manifest visibility, with durable adapter obligations. Shared transactions simplify consistency within one database. Cross-service storage can scale independently but needs explicit visibility, retries, reconciliation, and failure recovery. Draw a rollback scenario for PostgreSQL-only storage and another for SQL versions plus a remote vector engine. Identify the write that cannot be rolled back in the second design."}, {"id": 57, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "How do Qdrant, Chroma, FAISS, and in-memory adapters differ operationally?", "text": "How do Qdrant, Chroma, FAISS, and in-memory adapters differ operationally? They expose a common retrieval surface but use different storage and execution models. In-memory state is process-local. FAISS is an indexing library whose surrounding metadata and persistence need attention. Service-backed engines introduce network and server configuration. Adapter support, not brand identity, determines available guarantees. Each backend has its own implementation and optional dependency. The factory and capability layer let callers choose a backend while rejecting unsupported constraints instead of claiming complete interchangeability. Assuming identical durability, filter semantics, or transaction participation across these adapters can violate correctness. Inspect persistence and metadata behavior in the selected implementation and deployment. Lightweight local execution reduces setup; service-backed deployment supports different scale and operations needs. Portability often means accepting a common subset or explicitly branching on capabilities. Inspect the three adapter source pages in the module atlas. Record where vectors and metadata live and what survives a process restart in your proposed configuration."}, {"id": 58, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "Why are strict error semantics important for storage operations?", "text": "Why are strict error semantics important for storage operations? A failed query and a successful query with zero matches mean different things. Lenient APIs may log an error and return an empty list or zero count; that is convenient for some callers but dangerous when the host interprets emptiness as authoritative. Retrieval supports strict mode and a RetrievalError type. Mandatory capability and scope failures are designed to propagate rather than become silent fallbacks. Evidence assembly can represent backend failure distinctly when the host preserves that information. Forgetting strict mode can make an outage look like missing knowledge or an empty index. Health and deletion decisions should not infer success solely from an empty return value. Strict propagation requires explicit application error handling. Lenient fallback improves continuity only when the product can tolerate ambiguity and communicates it honestly. Make the backend raise on search and count. Compare strict and lenient behavior and design the corresponding UI states."}, {"id": 59, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "How should deletion differ from physical garbage collection?", "text": "How should deletion differ from physical garbage collection? Logical deletion removes permission or visibility immediately; physical cleanup later reclaims index storage. Separating the two prevents asynchronous vector deletion from remaining an access-control dependency. Sync manifests can tombstone documents, and SyncRetriever validates status, generation, scope, and chunk membership before returning hits. Garbage collection removes retired or uncommitted vectors after visibility decisions. Calling the raw vector store bypasses manifest checks. A delayed physical deletion is safe only if every reader uses the required visibility boundary and source lookup also respects revocation. Manifest gating adds reads and coordination but permits conservative cleanup and safer recovery. Immediate physical deletion alone is simpler but can fail or lag. Leave a tombstoned document's vectors physically present. Verify that the supported sync retrieval path returns no evidence and explain why raw nearest-neighbor access must remain internal."}, {"id": 60, "chapter": "Vector storage and database design", "slug": "storage", "title": "What does a trustworthy backend capability contract look like?", "text": "What does a trustworthy backend capability contract look like? It states supported filter operators and features such as document fetch, deletion, scope enforcement, generation visibility, and transaction guarantees. Unsupported mandatory requirements produce an error before returning potentially unsafe results. StoreCapabilities validates both requested constraints and filter shapes. This makes backend selection a correctness decision rather than a best-effort call to methods that may silently ignore arguments. Declarations are assertions by adapter code. A falsely advertised capability is still a bug, and permissive host defaults can avoid requesting a guarantee that the product actually needs. Fail-closed behavior reduces surprising leakage but can reject configurations that previously appeared to work. That incompatibility is useful when the earlier behavior was unsafe or incomplete. Build an adapter that rejects range filters and transactions. Send requests requiring each feature and verify rejection occurs before any evidence is returned."}, {"id": 61, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "What happens inside a typed retrieval request?", "text": "What happens inside a typed retrieval request? RetrievalRequest carries the query, candidate and final counts, threshold, filters, isolation scope, expected generation, metadata projection, session, and mandatory capabilities. It makes execution constraints explicit instead of hiding them in loosely interpreted keyword arguments. HybridRetriever coerces legacy calls into this form and validates supported constraints before delegating to a store or strategy. Effective filters and required metadata help preserve scope checks across the execution path. A well-typed request can still contain an unauthorized scope if the host trusts the user's input. Authentication must establish which scope the caller is allowed to request. A richer request object improves composability and inspection but requires a stable public contract and deliberate defaults. A small positional API is easier for toy usage. Construct one request for tenant A with a larger candidate pool and a smaller final result set. Explain every field that affects authorization, ranking, or visibility."}, {"id": 62, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "Why separate candidate_k from final_k?", "text": "Why separate candidate_k from final_k? candidate_k determines how many initial hits later stages can inspect; final_k determines how many results the caller receives. A reranker cannot promote a relevant passage that the candidate stage never retrieved. Fetching only five candidates and returning five wastes much of the value of reranking. The strategy API carries both counts through retrieval and applies final slicing after optional reranking and scope validation. Evidence budgeting is yet another limit and should not be confused with either count. Very large candidate pools increase backend work, reranker cost, and exposure of material to external services. Candidates sent to a reranker must already respect applicable permissions. More candidates can improve recall with diminishing returns. Tune using recall, final ranking quality, and latency rather than assuming larger is always better. Compare candidate pools of 10, 30, and 100 while holding final_k at five. Track whether missing answers were absent from the pool or simply ranked too low."}, {"id": 63, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "How does Reciprocal Rank Fusion work here?", "text": "How does Reciprocal Rank Fusion work here? RRF sums reciprocal rank contributions from independent result lists. This implementation uses 1/(60 + rank) with one-based ranks, then sorts by descending sum and breaks ties by document ID. It avoids directly adding incomparable raw distance and keyword scores. The shared rrf_merge helper constructs rank maps for vector and lexical lists. A document appearing in both gets two contributions; a document missing from a stage receives no contribution from that stage. RRF discards score magnitudes, so a near-tie and a decisive first-place win contribute identically at the same rank. Candidate truncation also limits which documents can benefit from fusion. Rank fusion is simple and robust across scoring scales, while weighted or learned fusion may capture more signal at the cost of calibration and training data. Calculate scores for a document ranked first in one stage and another ranked tenth in both. Verify the ordering with rrf_merge and inspect deterministic ties."}, {"id": 64, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "Are dense and lexical stages executed concurrently?", "text": "Are dense and lexical stages executed concurrently? In the inspected generic HybridRetrieval strategy, the vector search is awaited and then keyword search is awaited inside each query variant. The code does not use asyncio.gather for these stages. PostgreSQL's combined SQL path is a different execution arrangement and does not imply Python-level concurrency. Async syntax allows nonblocking waits but does not automatically parallelize consecutive awaits. Reading call sites is necessary before claiming an overlap or a particular latency model. Assuming stage concurrency understates latency. Conversely, parallelizing blindly can violate a shared database session's rules or increase load beyond intended limits. Sequential execution is easier to reason about with one session. Parallel execution may reduce latency for independent backends but requires separate safe resources, cancellation, and error policy. Instrument start and finish events for both stages. Propose a parallel variant only after specifying which sessions and connections may be used concurrently."}, {"id": 65, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "When does a reranker help, and what can it not fix?", "text": "When does a reranker help, and what can it not fix? A reranker reorders a candidate set using a more expensive relevance model, often evaluating the query and passage together. It helps distinguish closely related passages after a fast first-stage search. It cannot recover a document that never entered the candidate pool. HybridRetrieval accepts an optional Reranker protocol. LiteLLMReranker is one adapter, while a host can supply another implementation. Typed responses preserve richer score information where required. External reranking may transmit document content to another provider, add latency, or fail. Authorization and data-handling policy must apply before transmission, and timeout or failure behavior should be explicit. Reranking spends more per candidate to improve final ordering. It may be unnecessary for exact identifier queries or small corpora with strong lexical matches. Inspect ten candidates with similar vocabulary but different policy conditions. Compare fused ordering with reranked ordering and classify whether improvement justifies added cost."}, {"id": 66, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "How should query rewriting and multi-query retrieval be bounded?", "text": "How should query rewriting and multi-query retrieval be bounded? Query rewriting can make a conversational or ambiguous question more searchable; multi-query retrieval explores alternative phrasings. Both add work and can drift away from the original intent. Bounds should cover variant count, text length, time, and downstream calls. BoundedQueryTransformer wraps a host-provided transformer with limits and a timeout. The default project does not secretly call a generator to rewrite every query; the host injects that behavior. Generated variants may invent identifiers or broaden the scope of meaning. Scope filters must remain fixed across variants, and deduplication does not by itself calibrate rankings across all variants. Expansion can improve recall for difficult language while multiplying embedding and search cost. Start with a baseline and add it only for demonstrated failures. Supply a transformer returning duplicates, blank strings, too many variants, and a slow response. Explain which limits protect cost and which protect meaning."}, {"id": 67, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "How does multi-query merging differ from a fully calibrated global ranker?", "text": "How does multi-query merging differ from a fully calibrated global ranker? The generic hybrid strategy fuses each query variant's dense and lexical results, extends a candidate list, and deduplicates by ID in first-seen order before optional reranking. That is not a learned global calibration of all variant scores. The implementation is a bounded composition mechanism. Its optional reranker is the natural place to compare the combined candidates against the original question, but absent that stage the ordering reflects implementation choices. The first query variant can dominate which duplicate representation and ordering survive. More variants therefore need not improve final ranking and can increase noise. First-seen deduplication is simple and deterministic. A second fusion across variants or a global reranker adds complexity and should be justified by evaluation. Reverse the order of two transformer variants and compare final results without a reranker. Explain any changed ordering using the code path."}, {"id": 68, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "Why must filters be applied during each retrieval stage?", "text": "Why must filters be applied during each retrieval stage? If unauthorized documents consume the initial top-k pool, post-filtering can leave too few permitted results and may send forbidden text to later components. Applying constraints inside dense and lexical stages improves both isolation and eligible-candidate recall. Strategies pass effective filters to each backend, validate filter support, and run scope defense checks before returning results. These checks complement authorization at the transport or host boundary. A backend that ignores a filter can still leak data. Defense in depth detects some invalid results, but tests must also ensure a reranker or external processor does not receive unauthorized candidates before that final check. Filter-aware search can affect query plans and approximate recall. Security requirements take precedence; tune backend indexes rather than dropping constraints for speed. Place many high-similarity forbidden records ahead of a permitted record. Verify eligible recall and inspect exactly which records reach the reranker."}, {"id": 69, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "How should I interpret distance thresholds and relevance scores?", "text": "How should I interpret distance thresholds and relevance scores? Distance is a geometric measurement, not a truth probability. RRF is a rank-fusion score, not a calibrated confidence. Reranker scores have provider-specific meaning. A distance threshold must match the selected metric and embedding profile. Result types preserve distance, keyword score, and fusion or reranker scores separately rather than pretending they share a universal numerical scale. The retriever can apply a configured threshold and explicit final result limit. Displaying 0.92 as 92 percent correct misleads users. A low distance can retrieve an obsolete, unauthorized, or merely related passage; those require separate checks. Thresholding removes weak candidates but can reduce coverage and varies across corpus changes. Product confidence should be evaluated empirically using multiple signals and abstention policy. Collect score distributions for answerable and unanswerable questions. Evaluate a threshold's false acceptance and false rejection rates before choosing a UI label."}, {"id": 70, "chapter": "Retrieval, fusion, and reranking", "slug": "retrieval", "title": "How would you debug a relevant document missing from the final answer?", "text": "How would you debug a relevant document missing from the final answer? Walk the chain: was the source discovered, extracted, indexed under the active profile and generation, eligible under filters, retrieved into either candidate list, retained by fusion, ranked high enough, selected into evidence, and actually used by generation? Each step can independently remove useful information. Ragnor exposes distinct modules and result shapes for these stages. That separation supports inspection of intermediate artifacts rather than guessing that every wrong answer is an embedding problem. Debug logging can expose queries or private passages. Prefer controlled fixtures and sanitized IDs, and do not bypass scope checks simply to make a document appear during investigation. Stage-by-stage diagnosis takes longer than tweaking a single constant but targets the actual cause and prevents compensating errors. Create a failure report containing the first stage where an expected passage disappears, the supporting observation, and one focused change to test."}, {"id": 71, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "Why does evidence assembly deserve a separate module?", "text": "Why does evidence assembly deserve a separate module? Search ranking and prompt construction optimize different things. Ranking selects likely relevant hits; assembly must remove redundancy, preserve source identity, fit a budget, and communicate whether usable evidence exists. A list of raw top-k records does not meet all those needs. EvidenceAssembler coerces supported hit shapes, deduplicates, merges eligible spans, balances source diversity, selects items, and renders stable citation labels. This deterministic layer can be tested independently of an unpredictable generator. Assuming all retrieved text is safe prompt instruction invites source-driven manipulation. The bundle treats text as evidence and escapes selected delimiters, while host prompt policy and tool permissions remain necessary. An extra layer adds types and decisions but centralizes budgeting and attribution. Ad hoc prompt concatenation is shorter and easier to get subtly wrong. Feed duplicate and overlapping hits from two sources into the assembler. Inspect both selected text and the counters explaining what was removed or merged."}, {"id": 72, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "How is the context budget calculated?", "text": "How is the context budget calculated? Available evidence capacity equals total budget minus reserved instructions, user input, and answer tokens, floored at zero. The assembler must account for rendered framing and citation text, not only passage body tokens. A positive capacity does not imply that even one candidate will fit. ContextBudget exposes reserved_total, available_evidence_tokens, and is_valid. Assembly checks the rendered form as items are added and returns insufficient_budget when no candidate fits. Reserving too little for generation can cause provider truncation; counting with an approximate tokenizer cannot guarantee an actual model limit. The host must include its complete message framing and any tool schema costs. Larger answer reservations reduce evidence capacity. Larger evidence budgets increase cost and can include irrelevant or distracting material. For a 16,000-token window, reserve 2,000 instructions, 1,000 user input, and 3,000 answer tokens. Compute evidence room, then explain what additional host framing must be counted."}, {"id": 73, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "Why is ApproximateTokenizer not a strict context guarantee?", "text": "Why is ApproximateTokenizer not a strict context guarantee? It estimates from characters and whitespace-separated words. Actual model tokenization depends on its vocabulary and encoding, so a heuristic can undercount some inputs even when intended to be conservative. The class explicitly marks is_exact as false. Ragnor offers ExactTokenizerAdapter for a model-specific counter. That adapter is only as exact as the supplied counting function and the host's representation of the final serialized request. A function labeled exact but counting the wrong model or omitting message wrappers still gives unsafe limits. Multilingual text, code, and unusual characters deserve explicit verification. Approximation keeps core dependency-free and supports offline use. Exact counting adds a tokenizer dependency or integration step and stronger budget fidelity. Compare heuristic and actual counts for prose, code, Arabic, and long identifiers. Explain why agreement on one example does not establish a global upper bound."}, {"id": 74, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "How do deduplication and span merging differ?", "text": "How do deduplication and span merging differ? Deduplication removes repeated hits representing the same chunk. Span merging combines compatible overlapping or contiguous material from the same source context, preserving a larger coherent excerpt. They solve redundancy at different identity and text-boundary levels. EvidenceAssembler has separate _deduplicate and _merge_spans stages and records counts. Proper merging depends on source and revision information plus usable offsets; arbitrary neighboring ranked hits are not automatically one passage. Merging across revisions or unrelated documents can fabricate a source excerpt that never existed. Missing or inconsistent offsets should not be guessed into a false provenance claim. Merging can restore context and save repeated framing, but may create a large item that fails the budget. Keeping chunks separate provides finer selection at the cost of repetition. Construct overlapping spans from the same document revision and one from a different revision. Predict which may merge and verify the emitted provenance."}, {"id": 75, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "Why balance evidence across sources?", "text": "Why balance evidence across sources? A single document can occupy the candidate pool with many similar chunks. Source diversity can expose corroborating or contrasting material and reduce redundant context. It is a selection heuristic, not a guarantee that independent sources agree or are trustworthy. The assembler ranks within sources and interleaves them before budget selection. This can preserve diversity even when a long source produces many high-scoring chunks. Diversity may demote the only authoritative source in favor of weaker material. Source quality and authority are product policies that may need explicit weighting or filtering outside the default heuristic. Pure relevance order maximizes one ranking signal; diversity spreads context coverage. Evaluate questions requiring one detailed source separately from questions requiring synthesis across sources. Give one source eight high-ranked chunks and another one relevant chunk. Compare evidence selection with the product's expected authority policy."}, {"id": 76, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "What failure states should an evidence consumer handle?", "text": "What failure states should an evidence consumer handle? EvidenceStatus includes success, empty_search, backend_failure, insufficient_budget, stale_index, and revision_unavailable. These represent different next actions: answer, clarify, retry, change capacity, refresh indexing, or explain that a historical source cannot be retrieved. The assembler explicitly returns backend and stale-index states rather than assembling ungrounded material. Revision-aware lookup raises a distinct error instead of silently substituting the current document. Flattening every state into no answer hides outages and can trigger unsafe generation fallbacks. Success also means usable selected evidence, not proof of a correct final answer. Rich states require more UI and API branches but let users respond appropriately. A single boolean is simpler and loses important operational meaning. Write user-facing messages for each status and specify which states may be retried automatically without changing the user's question."}, {"id": 77, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "What makes a citation reproducible?", "text": "What makes a citation reproducible? A reproducible citation identifies a stable source, document, revision, and bounded location or span. A URL pointing only to the latest page may no longer support an old answer after an update. Display labels such as [1] are local presentation, not durable source identities. SourceLookup retrieves retained snapshots and rejects unavailable revisions. Evidence items carry revision and span fields, while source-reference helpers encode identifiers for later verification. Historical snapshots can contain sensitive material even after current content changes. Retention and revocation policies must apply to lookup as well as search, and a host must provide durable snapshot storage when needed. Retention improves auditability and costs storage and policy management. Current-only lookup is lighter but must admit when a historical claim cannot be verified. Cite revision A, update the source to B, and attempt lookup. Verify that A is returned if retained and that missing A never silently becomes B."}, {"id": 78, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "What does delimiter escaping mitigate, and what does it leave open?", "text": "What does delimiter escaping mitigate, and what does it leave open? Escaping selected instruction-like tags and code fences reduces the chance that source text visually or syntactically breaks out of an evidence wrapper. It does not recognize every malicious natural-language instruction or make a model incapable of following one. escape_untrusted_delimiters replaces known markers in evidence rendering, and URI sanitization removes embedded credentials or disallowed active schemes. These are narrow input-handling mechanisms within a larger trust model. A passage can say ignore previous instructions without using any special delimiter. Host instructions must classify evidence as untrusted data, and tool permissions must prevent source text from gaining authority. Escaping preserves useful text with modest complexity but can alter literal code examples. More aggressive sanitization may damage legitimate content and still cannot solve all prompt injection. Compare a tag-based injection and a plain-language injection. Explain why both should remain untrusted even if only one changes during escaping."}, {"id": 79, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "How can evidence compression preserve usefulness without fabricating support?", "text": "How can evidence compression preserve usefulness without fabricating support? Compression shortens selected material to fit a budget. Extractive compression selects original spans; abstractive compression generates a shorter restatement. The latter can change meaning or omit conditions, so provenance and faithfulness require explicit checks. EvidenceAssembler accepts an optional compressor, passes a copy of the item, checks selected provenance fields, recounts the rendered result, and falls back when compression fails. No universal learned compressor is installed by default. Preserving source ID and revision does not prove the compressed words accurately represent the source. The host must evaluate the compressor and decide whether the compressed text is quoteable or should be labeled as a summary. Compression increases context coverage but adds cost and potential distortion. Dropping a low-value item is often safer than aggressively rewriting essential evidence. Compress a rule containing an exception. Verify both metadata invariance and semantic preservation; show a failure that passes metadata checks but changes the rule."}, {"id": 80, "chapter": "Evidence, context, and citations", "slug": "evidence", "title": "How should the host turn evidence into a safe answer policy?", "text": "How should the host turn evidence into a safe answer policy? The host should specify when to answer, how to cite, when to ask for clarification, and when to abstain. It should pass authorized evidence as data, preserve status signals, and check that important claims are supported. This policy is part of the application, not an automatic property of Ragnor. The bridge returns a structured EvidenceBundle that a generator or source-viewing UI can consume. Its explicit status and attribution fields make a disciplined policy feasible without binding the subsystem to one chat model. A host that discards bundle status, regenerates citation identifiers, or allows source text to choose tools can undermine the safeguards upstream. Streaming claims before evidence selection is settled creates another verification gap. Strict evidence-only answering improves traceability but can feel less helpful for general questions. Clearly separated modes can preserve user expectations. Draft a generation policy for answerable, ambiguous, empty, stale, and failed retrieval cases. Create one acceptance example for each branch."}, {"id": 81, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "What are the phases of IngestionPipeline.run?", "text": "What are the phases of IngestionPipeline.run? The pipeline loads source documents, verifies load authority, computes a snapshot, checks whether it is unchanged, chunks, embeds, and validates. It then enters a critical session to recheck the snapshot, retire the old version, create a new version, and upsert chunks. Slow provider work precedes the mutation-critical section. This reduces the time an internally owned session and advisory lock are held for version changes. The read-only initial snapshot check uses a short session when the caller did not supply one. A caller-supplied session may already have an open transaction throughout preparation. The library cannot undo that host decision, so do not claim remote calls are outside every possible transaction in all integrations. Preparation can be duplicated by concurrent workers, but database contention is lower. Serializing all work under a lock saves duplicate embeddings while holding scarce resources longer. Instrument session entry, embedding start, lock acquisition, upsert, and commit. Compare internally owned and caller-owned session paths."}, {"id": 82, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "Why is there a second snapshot check under the lock?", "text": "Why is there a second snapshot check under the lock? Two workers can both read an old snapshot, prepare identical new vectors, and then reach the write phase. The second check detects that another worker already activated the intended snapshot and avoids duplicate version mutation. _commit_ingestion reads the active version again before retiring anything. This is double-checked coordination: the early check avoids unnecessary work in the common case; the protected check handles races during preparation. The recheck is only serialized when the relevant locking and transaction semantics are actually enforced. Without a supplied lock key or equivalent host coordination, concurrent writes may still race. A second read costs a small amount of database work and does not prevent duplicate provider calls. It protects the authoritative mutation rather than promising exactly-once preparation. Pause two workers after their initial checks, let one commit, then release the second. Verify only one new active version appears when the lock contract is honored."}, {"id": 83, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "What belongs in an ingestion snapshot?", "text": "What belongs in an ingestion snapshot? The snapshot should represent the inputs that determine indexed content: source manifest hash, extractor behavior, chunker identity and configuration, and embedding configuration or profile. A change to any of these can make existing vectors stale despite unchanged source text. versioning.py builds and compares structured snapshots using stable serialized data and sanitized configuration. This turns unchanged detection into an explicit compatibility decision. Omitting hidden preprocessing or a model revision can cause false unchanged matches. Including unstable or secret-bearing fields can trigger needless rebuilds or expose credentials. Comprehensive snapshots improve repeatability while requiring disciplined adapter metadata. A simple content hash is easier but cannot detect pipeline changes. List every transformation between source bytes and stored vectors in your host. Identify which are represented in the snapshot and add a documented version for any custom transformation."}, {"id": 84, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "What is idempotency, and what is actually idempotent here?", "text": "What is idempotency, and what is actually idempotent here? An idempotent operation can be repeated without producing additional logical effects after the intended state is reached. Ingestion uses snapshot comparison to skip unchanged work and rechecks before mutation. That does not mean every low-level call executes once or always reuses identical physical chunk IDs. Repeated preparation may call an embedding provider more than once, while activation should preserve one intended current state when coordination is correct. This distinction is essential for crash recovery and job retries. Billing side effects, external writes, or custom version-store behavior may not be idempotent. The host must use stable operation identity and ensure repeated processing does not create duplicate application effects. Logical idempotency enables robust retries at the cost of hashes, state checks, and identity tracking. Exactly-once execution across external services is a much stronger claim. Run unchanged ingestion twice and count provider calls, active versions, and stored chunks. Then repeat after a forced failure immediately before commit."}, {"id": 85, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "Who commits a caller-supplied database session?", "text": "Who commits a caller-supplied database session? The caller owns a supplied session and its transaction. The pipeline yields it into the critical section but does not commit it. When the pipeline opens its own session, it commits after successful critical work. This ownership rule prevents a library from unexpectedly committing unrelated host changes. _transacting_session implements the distinction and can acquire the advisory lock on a caller session when a lock key is supplied. The lock lifetime still follows the actual database and transaction behavior. A host that forgets to commit loses intended changes; a host that keeps the transaction open across slow preparation can retain connections and locks too long. Session ownership must be clear at integration boundaries. Caller ownership supports composing RAG writes with business operations, while library ownership simplifies isolated ingestion. Mixing the models implicitly is error-prone. Add an unrelated host row before calling ingestion with the same session. Roll back and verify whether both mutations obey the host's intended transaction."}, {"id": 86, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "What does an advisory lock protect, and what does it not protect?", "text": "What does an advisory lock protect, and what does it not protect? An advisory lock coordinates cooperating processes using the same lock key and database locking mechanism. It can serialize the mutation phase for one source. It does not force unrelated code paths to honor the lock or create a distributed transaction with an external vector service. The pipeline obtains the lock through SQLFactory when lock_key is supplied. Lock-key derivation and consistent source scope are therefore part of the host's correctness contract. Inconsistent keys allow concurrent writers to bypass each other; overly broad keys serialize unrelated sources. A lock also does not guarantee that the shortest possible transaction duration has been measured. Fine-grained keys improve throughput while increasing key-management complexity. A global ingestion lock is simple but can become a bottleneck. Define lock keys for two tenants sharing a source name. Explain why including or excluding tenant scope changes both correctness and contention."}, {"id": 87, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "How should authoritative emptiness differ from extraction failure?", "text": "How should authoritative emptiness differ from extraction failure? An authoritative empty dataset can mean that previously indexed content has been removed. A failed or incomplete read means the source state is unknown. The pipeline defaults empty input to a no-op and requires purge_on_empty to retire current content deliberately. Non-authoritative LoadResult is rejected before mutation. For a valid empty source, the purge path closes the active version and deletes its chunks inside the transacting session. Enabling purge for an adapter that reports failures as an ordinary empty list can destroy visibility of healthy content. Returning zero chunks is not enough information to decide deletion safely. Conservative defaults retain old knowledge during uncertainty but need a separate deletion and revocation policy. Aggressive replacement is simpler and more dangerous during source outages. Test a genuine empty source, an inaccessible source, and a parser returning no chunks from nonempty input. Explain why each path should have different effects."}, {"id": 88, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "Why abort when nonempty documents produce zero chunks?", "text": "Why abort when nonempty documents produce zero chunks? Recording a new active snapshot with no useful chunks could make later runs classify the same source as already indexed and skip it indefinitely. Aborting before version mutation preserves the previous active content and allows a later retry after the chunker or extraction issue is fixed. The pipeline explicitly checks for no chunks before embedding and before entering the critical section. This is a small guard protecting a large lifecycle invariant: an active snapshot should correspond to successfully prepared content. A host may misinterpret the returned zero as successful deletion or successful replacement. Diagnostics and status handling should distinguish aborted preparation from an authoritative empty purge. Retaining old content favors availability while freshness is unresolved. Some products should additionally mark it stale instead of silently treating it as current. Inject a chunker that returns an empty list for a nonempty source. Verify the old version remains active and that a subsequent valid chunker can ingest it."}, {"id": 89, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "How does rollback interact with old-version retirement and new-vector upsert?", "text": "How does rollback interact with old-version retirement and new-vector upsert? The critical sequence retires the active version, deletes its chunks, creates the new version, stamps metadata, and upserts new chunks. With a shared transactional backend, a failure should roll all of that back. With nontransactional collaborators, this sequence alone cannot guarantee atomic replacement. Passing one session is the mechanism for joining transactional writes. In-memory or remote adapters require their own consistency design; the manifest-based sync system addresses a different visibility model. Deleting old content before a remote upsert fails can leave an unavailable or inconsistent index. Do not assume a Python context manager can roll back writes in an external service. In-place transactional replacement is compact in one database. Generation preparation and activation use more storage and state but better fit cross-system operations. Inject an upsert failure after retirement. Test actual visibility and active-version state with your production adapter combination, not only a recording fake."}, {"id": 90, "chapter": "Ingestion and transaction ownership", "slug": "ingestion", "title": "Why are fixed claims such as microsecond swaps or sub-5-ms commits unreliable?", "text": "Why are fixed claims such as microsecond swaps or sub-5-ms commits unreliable? The critical section includes reads, version mutations, deletion, and upsert, not merely a pointer assignment. Its duration depends on chunk count, database load, indexes, network latency, and adapter behavior. No timing benchmark in the inspected implementation proves a universal duration. Moving remote embedding out of internally owned mutation sessions is a sound architectural mechanism. That mechanism supports a narrower claim about resource occupancy, not an invented service-level objective. Designing connection pools or latency budgets around fictional timings can cause overload. Measure lock wait, transaction duration, upsert volume, and retry behavior under representative workloads. Shorter transactions reduce contention but preparation may still dominate end-to-end ingestion time. Optimization should target measured bottlenecks without weakening atomicity. Benchmark the critical section across increasing chunk counts. Report distributions and test conditions, and keep provider time separate from database time."}, {"id": 91, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "Why does sync need more than the basic ingestion pipeline?", "text": "Why does sync need more than the basic ingestion pipeline? Sync reconciles a changing collection over repeated scans and distributed workers. It must classify document changes, track active generations, prevent stale activation, manage removals, checkpoint progress, and clean abandoned writes. A one-shot replace operation does not cover all of those concerns. DefaultSyncCoordinator composes source scans, manifests, fencing state, embeddings, vector storage, optional reconciliation jobs, and garbage collection. It prepares generations before committing visibility through manifests. The coordinator's logic depends on durable adapters implementing atomic fencing and batch compare-and-swap. The provided in-memory implementations demonstrate semantics within a process, not a production multi-process coordination service. The richer protocol improves recoverability while adding state and adapter obligations. Small single-process applications may reasonably use the simpler pipeline. Compare the state required to replace one file with the state required to sync a million-object collection across two workers and intermittent source failures."}, {"id": 92, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "What are leases and fencing tokens, and why are both needed?", "text": "What are leases and fencing tokens, and why are both needed? A lease grants temporary ownership; a fencing token is a monotonically increasing epoch that lets authoritative storage reject obsolete owners. A worker can pause beyond its lease and later resume, so merely checking lease time before a write is insufficient. SyncStateStore provides lease acquisition, renewal, token validation, and a fenced commit context. The coordinator validates tokens after preparation and performs activation inside the fenced context. A stale worker may already have written prepared vectors, but it must not activate them or overwrite checkpoints. Correctness depends on checking authority at the authoritative mutation boundary, not only in worker memory. Fencing requires shared durable state and atomic enforcement. A simple expiring lock is easier to implement but vulnerable to resumed zombie workers. Pause worker A, expire its lease, let B acquire a newer epoch, then resume A. Identify every operation A may still perform and every authoritative write that must reject it."}, {"id": 93, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "Why does a separate token check followed by a write still race?", "text": "Why does a separate token check followed by a write still race? Another worker can take ownership after the check and before the write. This is a time-of-check to time-of-use race. The authority check and authoritative mutation need one protected context or equivalent atomic database condition. The SyncStateStore protocol explicitly requires fenced() to exclude takeover while committing. A production adapter can use transactional row locking, advisory locking, or equivalent compare-and-swap semantics; a no-op context is not sufficient. Implementing validate_token correctly but fenced incorrectly creates false confidence. Unit tests that never interleave takeover with commit will miss the race. Holding a guard during activation can increase contention. Keep expensive embedding outside it and protect only the necessary visibility and checkpoint operations. Write an interleaving with a successful token check, a takeover, and an obsolete write. Then show which atomic guard makes that history impossible."}, {"id": 94, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "What does compare-and-swap do for manifest activation?", "text": "What does compare-and-swap do for manifest activation? Compare-and-swap updates state only if its current version matches the expected version. It detects that another operation changed the document while new content was being prepared. Batch compare-and-swap applies the intended set without a partially updated manifest snapshot. The coordinator records expected fences, prepares vectors, and calls compare_and_swap_many during activation. Readers use a manifest snapshot to validate which generation and chunk IDs are visible. A loop of individually successful per-document swaps is not equivalent to an atomic batch when collection consistency is required. Adapter contracts must make the promised visibility semantics real. Batch atomicity simplifies reader consistency while placing stronger demands on durable storage. Per-document activation permits partial progress with weaker whole-collection simultaneity. Simulate two prepared updates where one expected fence is stale. Verify that a batch failure does not leave only the other document activated."}, {"id": 95, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "How do document and collection activation modes differ?", "text": "How do document and collection activation modes differ? Document mode can activate successful document updates even when another document fails preparation. Collection mode aims to avoid publishing a partially prepared collection and advances collection-generation state after activation. They express different consistency and availability choices. The coordinator's preparation loop records errors; collection mode raises on preparation failure, while document mode can continue. The activation path updates manifests and collection state under the supplied coordination contracts. Cross-store atomicity is not created automatically by selecting collection mode. Durable adapters must preserve the fenced and batch visibility obligations, including failure between manifest commit and generation advancement. Collection consistency can delay fresh data because one bad document blocks a run. Document mode improves partial availability while exposing mixed document update times. Make one of three documents fail embedding. Compare reports and visible generations in both modes and state which behavior your product requires."}, {"id": 96, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "How do metadata-only changes avoid unnecessary re-embedding?", "text": "How do metadata-only changes avoid unnecessary re-embedding? If content and embedding-relevant processing remain unchanged, a metadata update can reuse stored vectors while writing records with new metadata. This saves provider work without pretending that all changes are harmless to vector identity. Sync plans distinguish metadata_only updates. The coordinator fetches existing embeddings by active chunk IDs and passes them into preparation instead of calling the embedder again for that case. Misclassifying a semantic preprocessing change as metadata-only reuses invalid vectors. Missing stored vectors also need an explicit failure path rather than silently substituting arbitrary values. This optimization reduces cost but requires accurate fingerprints and a fetch-capable backend. Re-embedding everything is simpler and more expensive. Change a display title, then change chunker configuration, then change an access field. Explain which changes can reuse vectors and which still require immediate authorization updates."}, {"id": 97, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "How does SyncRetriever protect against stale and tombstoned vectors?", "text": "How does SyncRetriever protect against stale and tombstoned vectors? It asks the backend for scoped active candidates, oversamples, reads a manifest snapshot, and rejects results whose document is absent or non-active, whose generation differs, or whose chunk ID is not in the active manifest. Physical presence is not sufficient for visibility. This read-time gate decouples authorization and active-state checks from eventual vector cleanup. It also checks returned metadata rather than trusting that every backend filter was honored perfectly. Its finite oversampling can still return fewer than the requested number of valid hits. Also, callers using the raw store bypass this protection, so integration must preserve the intended reader path. Additional manifest reads and filtering improve consistency while adding latency and possible underfilling. A stronger backend-native visibility index could reduce that overhead but needs equivalent guarantees. Insert old-generation, tombstoned, wrong-scope, and valid records. Verify which survive and show a case where oversampling still cannot fill final_k."}, {"id": 98, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "What is the transactional outbox pattern, and what must the host implement?", "text": "What is the transactional outbox pattern, and what must the host implement? The outbox writes a job in the same transaction as the application change it represents. A separate worker later processes that job. This prevents a committed business change from losing its intended background work because a process crashed before sending an external message. EmbeddingJobStore and JobProcessor are host-implemented contracts. enqueue behavior with a supplied session must join the caller's transaction; protocol documentation describes safe claiming, including SKIP LOCKED semantics for a SQL implementation. A separate job insert after commit is not a transactional outbox. Likewise, the sync coordinator's optional reconciliation enqueue should not automatically be described as atomic with every remote vector write. An outbox adds polling, retries, and retention but avoids fragile dual writes. It still requires idempotent processing because a crash can cause a job to run again. Draw crashes before business commit, after commit, and after processing but before completion marking. Explain how an outbox avoids lost work while permitting duplicate processing."}, {"id": 99, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "Does SKIP LOCKED provide exactly-once job execution?", "text": "Does SKIP LOCKED provide exactly-once job execution? No. It helps concurrent SQL workers claim different available rows without waiting on already locked rows. A worker can still finish external work and crash before recording completion, causing a later retry to repeat that work. The job system tracks status, attempts, lock ownership, and stale-job reclamation. These mechanisms support recovery, while the processor must ensure repeated execution has safe logical effects. Reclaiming too early can overlap a still-running worker; never reclaiming can strand jobs forever. Production adapters must enforce ownership on completion and failure transitions, not just during initial selection. At-least-once processing is practical with idempotent writes and fencing. Claiming exactly-once behavior across unrelated external services would require a much stronger design. Make the processor succeed, then simulate failure in completion marking. Run reclamation and show that the second execution does not duplicate the application's intended effect."}, {"id": 100, "chapter": "Distributed sync, jobs, and recovery", "slug": "coordination", "title": "How do shutdown, retries, checkpoints, and garbage collection work together?", "text": "How do shutdown, retries, checkpoints, and garbage collection work together? The drainer checks for stale jobs, processes pending work, survives failed passes, and uses a stop event for shutdown. Sync cancels heartbeats, releases leases, and cleans retired or uncommitted vectors in a finally path. Checkpoints record committed progress under the current token. These are recovery mechanisms for different lifetimes: process shutdown, task execution, authority ownership, and storage cleanup. A completion report alone cannot replace all of them. Cleanup can itself fail, and process termination may prevent finally blocks from running. Durable reconciliation must discover leftovers on a later run; a successful logical activation should not be undone by deleting committed IDs as abandoned work. Conservative cleanup leaves temporary storage overhead while protecting visible data. Aggressive deletion reduces space and can make recovery unsafe. Build a crash matrix for preparation, vector write, manifest activation, checkpoint, and cleanup. For each point identify visible state and the next repair action."}, {"id": 101, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "How are authentication, authorization, and retrieval filtering different?", "text": "How are authentication, authorization, and retrieval filtering different? Authentication establishes who is calling. Authorization decides what that identity may access or do. Retrieval filtering constrains the records searched and returned. A tenant_id filter supplied by an untrusted caller is not authentication or authorization. The tool bridge composes scope authorization with retrieval and source lookup. MCP identity can come from trusted transport context or a host callback rather than a model-supplied client identifier. Allowing a request to choose both its identity and scope creates an impersonation path. The host must establish trusted identity and map it to allowed collections before issuing constrained retrieval. Central authorization adds integration work but prevents duplicated, inconsistent policy across tools. Filters remain necessary for efficient authorized search even after permission is granted. Draw an API request with identity, requested collection, and requested scope. Mark which values are trusted and where each becomes constrained."}, {"id": 102, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "Are the remote transport defaults production-secure by themselves?", "text": "Are the remote transport defaults production-secure by themselves? No universal claim is justified. RemoteTransportConfig accepts tokens when no validator is configured, uses a permissive default scope authorizer, and has wildcard CORS defaults. These are integration defaults that a production host must review and tighten deliberately. The security module exposes callbacks and mappings so a host can enforce its actual identity system. Loopback binding reduces default exposure, but it is not a substitute for authorization once the service is reachable remotely. Deploying with permissive defaults can make private knowledge broadly accessible. CORS also does not authenticate non-browser callers; restricting it alone is insufficient. Permissive local defaults simplify experimentation. Explicit validators, deny-by-default scope mappings, bounded origins, TLS, and deployment policy require more setup and give a meaningful remote trust boundary. Construct a configuration that rejects missing tokens and unknown clients. Test an authenticated user requesting another tenant's collection as well as an unauthenticated caller."}, {"id": 103, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "What should the KnowledgeBridge centralize?", "text": "What should the KnowledgeBridge centralize? It provides one framework-neutral boundary for search, bounded evidence, source lookup, collection selection, and health or management operations. Transport adapters should translate requests into that boundary rather than each rebuilding retrieval and permission logic. CollectionConfig supplies retriever and source-lookup configuration; KnowledgeBridge composes authorizer, assembler, and health checker. This resembles a facade or application-service boundary over the lower-level components. A central facade is only protective if all exposed routes use it correctly. Direct backend endpoints or source lookup shortcuts can bypass the common policy. Centralization reduces duplicated integration logic but can become overly broad if every domain behavior is added to it. Keep application-specific decisions in the host. Compare a chat endpoint and an agent tool endpoint. Identify which validation and authorization behavior should be shared through the bridge and which presentation behavior should differ."}, {"id": 104, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "What is MCP, and what does this adapter expose?", "text": "What is MCP, and what does this adapter expose? Model Context Protocol is a protocol for exposing tools and resources to model-enabled clients. Ragnor's MCP adapter wraps the bridge with read tools such as search_knowledge, get_source, and knowledge_status. Management operations require explicit enabling conditions. The adapter keeps protocol concerns outside retrieval logic and obtains identity through trusted integration hooks. It supports SDK variation through an adapter layer rather than making the core depend on MCP. A protocol does not make a tool inherently authorized or safe. A model can request an operation, but the server must enforce permissions, budgets, and management policy independently of the model's wording. Standardized tool access broadens integration while introducing another exposed surface and dependency. A simple host API may be sufficient for one application. Inspect the registered tools and their input schemas. Explain why a tool description is useful for model behavior but cannot enforce a security rule."}, {"id": 105, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "Why are read and management tools separate opt-ins?", "text": "Why are read and management tools separate opt-ins? Searching knowledge and changing or revoking knowledge have different consequences. A client that may read a collection should not automatically be able to ingest arbitrary sources, trigger expensive sync, or remove access for other users. The MCP server defaults to read tools, while management registration and the bridge's allow_management setting provide separate opt-in controls. Those controls complement, rather than replace, identity-based authorization. Enabling a management tool globally without per-caller policy can give an agent excessive authority. Source text must never grant that authority by instructing the model to call a privileged tool. Separate permissions and configuration increase setup but support least privilege. A single unrestricted tool surface is simpler and broadens both accidental and malicious impact. Define reader, operator, and administrator roles. Map each to search, source lookup, sync, and revocation, then state where the host enforces each permission."}, {"id": 106, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "Why is prompt injection an authorization problem as well as a prompting problem?", "text": "Why is prompt injection an authorization problem as well as a prompting problem? Retrieved documents may contain instructions aimed at the model rather than information for the user. If the model can turn those instructions into privileged actions, untrusted content has crossed an authority boundary. Prompt wording alone should not determine which actions are permitted. Evidence rendering escapes some markers, bridge operations apply authorization, and management tools are opt-in. These controls address different layers: presentation, data access, and action authority. Natural-language attacks can survive escaping, and a model can misinterpret legitimate text. Server-side tool validation and least privilege must remain effective even when the model requests the wrong action. Tighter permissions can limit autonomous workflows. Grant narrow capabilities for a task rather than broad access based on assumed model compliance. Put a malicious instruction in a test document asking for another tenant's source. Verify the server refuses the operation even if a simulated model follows the instruction exactly."}, {"id": 107, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "Why must source lookup enforce authorization separately from search?", "text": "Why must source lookup enforce authorization separately from search? A citation reference can be copied, guessed, or reused after access changes. Possession of an identifier is not proof that the caller may read the referenced document. Historical snapshots can also retain text no longer visible in current search. SourceLookup supports scoped access, revocation, bounded spans, and explicit revision availability. The bridge authorizes source retrieval rather than treating a citation label as an unrestricted download token. Protecting search while exposing an unguarded get_source endpoint leaks the same underlying corpus. Retention policy must distinguish audit storage from permission to disclose that storage. Rechecking permissions adds work to every citation open, but preserves access changes over time. Caching source responses also needs scope and revocation-aware keys. Obtain a valid citation, revoke access, then try opening it again. Verify both search and retained-source lookup reject disclosure."}, {"id": 108, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "What does the built-in rate limiter control, and what does it not control?", "text": "What does the built-in rate limiter control, and what does it not control? It tracks request timestamps and active counts per client within one process, enforcing a sliding-window request ceiling and concurrency limit. It is not a shared quota service across many replicas and does not automatically price embedding or generation tokens. RateLimiter uses in-memory dictionaries and an asyncio lock. Remote transport configuration can inject it as one guard around tool work, while embedding execution has a different semaphore for provider calls. Multiple replicas multiply an apparent per-client limit unless they coordinate. Failed release logic can also strand active slots, so acquire and release must be paired even on exceptions and cancellation. In-process limits are fast and simple. Fleet-wide limits need shared state or an API gateway and introduce another availability dependency. Send requests for one identity through two limiter instances. Explain the combined effective quota and design a shared policy for a multi-replica deployment."}, {"id": 109, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "How should telemetry avoid becoming a data leak?", "text": "How should telemetry avoid becoming a data leak? Prefer counts, durations, sanitized error categories, and controlled identifiers. Raw questions, passages, credentials, and user identifiers can expose private knowledge through logs, traces, and analytics systems even when retrieval itself is authorized. RedactionConfig defaults raw query, document text, user identifiers, and unmasked errors to disabled. Event construction sanitizes errors and supports host-controlled redaction. This does not imply that every ordinary logger in the repository or host is automatically covered. Exception strings, URLs, and custom metadata can carry secrets. A redacted event sink cannot sanitize unrelated logs or a provider's own diagnostics. Less raw data makes debugging harder and reduces exposure. Use temporary, controlled debugging fixtures rather than permanently recording production passages. Raise an error containing an API key, source URL, and query text. Inspect events and ordinary logs separately and document remaining exposure paths."}, {"id": 110, "chapter": "Security, tools, and trust boundaries", "slug": "security", "title": "What security acceptance tests matter most for this integration?", "text": "What security acceptance tests matter most for this integration? Prioritize cross-tenant access, unsupported mandatory filters, source lookup after revocation, management permissions, malicious source instructions, SSRF redirects, and backend failures. These test authority boundaries rather than merely validating that a happy-path search returns text. The repository has focused tests around retrieval constraints, connectors, tool bridges, and sync. A host must extend those checks to its own identity provider, deployment routes, and durable adapters. A mock that always applies filters can conceal an actual backend mismatch. Include tests against the deployed adapter combination and inspect what leaves the process for reranking or telemetry. Boundary tests take setup but prevent high-impact failures. Duplicating every implementation branch with brittle tests is less valuable than exercising the invariants users depend on. Write five adversarial scenarios with expected denial and one authorized control case for each. Specify whether denial must occur before any external provider receives document text."}, {"id": 111, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How are Recall@K, Precision@K, and MRR calculated?", "text": "How are Recall@K, Precision@K, and MRR calculated? Recall@K is the fraction of labeled relevant items found in the first K results. Precision@K is the fraction of those K positions that are relevant under the chosen denominator convention. Mean Reciprocal Rank averages one divided by the rank of the first relevant hit, using zero when none is found. RagEvaluationHarness evaluates an injected retrieval function against labeled chunk IDs and records latency and errors. Always inspect denominator and short-result handling in the implementation before comparing scores with another tool. Labels can be incomplete, and chunk IDs can change after reindexing. A correct passage may be marked wrong if labels are tied to obsolete physical chunks. Retrieval metrics are cheap and diagnostic but do not evaluate final answer faithfulness. Use them alongside source-span or document labels and answer-level evaluation. For relevant IDs A and B and ranking C,A,D,B, calculate metrics at K=3. Then compare with the harness's exact conventions."}, {"id": 112, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How should I create a useful evaluation dataset?", "text": "How should I create a useful evaluation dataset? Include realistic questions, expected sources or spans, answerability, permissions, and failure cases. Cover exact identifiers, paraphrases, ambiguous requests, stale policies, multi-document synthesis, multilingual inputs, and unanswerable questions. Keep a held-out set separate from tuning examples. The evaluation modules accept datasets and injected retrieval or answer functions, so the same cases can compare chunkers, profiles, strategies, and host generation policies. Record the corpus revision and configuration alongside each run. Synthetic questions can mirror the source wording too closely and overstate quality. Data leakage from tuning into evaluation also produces misleading improvements. Human-labeled cases cost effort but reveal product relevance. Synthetic expansion is useful when reviewed and balanced against real user questions. Author 30 questions with source-level gold evidence and five deliberate non-answerable cases. Have another reviewer check labels without seeing your retrieval output."}, {"id": 113, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "Why is retrieval-only evaluation useful before generation exists?", "text": "Why is retrieval-only evaluation useful before generation exists? It isolates whether the system can locate supporting material. If relevant passages never reach the candidate or evidence set, a better generator cannot reliably repair that failure. Retrieval-only runs are also cheaper and more deterministic than end-to-end model evaluation. The runner supports distinct evaluation modes and accepts host callbacks. Ragnor can be evaluated as a subsystem without pretending it ships a complete production chat application. Strong retrieval metrics can hide poor evidence budgeting, unsupported claims, and an unhelpful UI. They are a stage gate, not the final product score. Early isolation speeds diagnosis and reduces API cost, while end-to-end testing is still needed to assess user outcomes. Use both at different stages of development. Establish a retrieval baseline before adding a generator. Record cases where the correct source is retrieved but later excluded from the evidence budget."}, {"id": 114, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How do heuristic answer scoring and LLM-as-judge differ?", "text": "How do heuristic answer scoring and LLM-as-judge differ? Heuristic scoring uses deterministic text-overlap signals and is cheap to run offline. LLM-as-judge asks a configured model to assess qualities such as faithfulness, relevance, completeness, and conciseness. Neither is an infallible measure of truth. answer.py implements both strategies with standardized score containers. Heuristics provide a stable baseline; judges can assess richer language relationships but add provider cost and evaluation-model variability. Overlap can reward copying a wrong answer, while judges can exhibit bias, prompt sensitivity, and correlated mistakes. A judge may also be influenced by adversarial content in the answer or evidence. Use deterministic metrics for broad regression feedback and calibrated human review for critical cases. Model judging can expand coverage if its agreement with humans is measured. Score a faithful paraphrase, a copied incorrect answer, and a fluent unsupported answer using both approaches. Explain disagreements rather than averaging them away."}, {"id": 115, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "What does a high-value test strategy look like for this project?", "text": "What does a high-value test strategy look like for this project? Test behavioral invariants at seams: no mutation after invalid preparation, scope constraints on both stages, explicit backend failure, stale-worker rejection, revision-correct lookup, and safe repeated job processing. Use focused fakes for deterministic races and real adapter tests for actual storage semantics. The test suite has targeted modules for ingestion locking, embedding execution, retrieval capabilities, and sync coordination. These boundaries are more consequential than mirroring every helper's internal implementation. Passing fake-based tests cannot prove production rollback, SQL locking, or vendor filter behavior. Conversely, an enormous end-to-end suite can be slow and poor at locating failures. A layered mix gives fast feedback and credible integration evidence. Choose the smallest test that can actually falsify the relevant guarantee. For one transaction guarantee and one retrieval filter guarantee, state which test needs a real database and which can use a controlled fake."}, {"id": 116, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "What is the difference between liveness, readiness, and degraded health?", "text": "What is the difference between liveness, readiness, and degraded health? Liveness asks whether a process can run. Readiness asks whether it can serve its intended work. Degraded health means some useful behavior remains but a dependency or freshness condition is impaired. One healthy HTTP process does not prove its index is current or its embedding provider works. HealthChecker collects configuration, connectivity, counts, sync, job, and evaluation information. HealthReport exposes distinctions that a host can map to probes and user-facing status. An expensive consistency scan in every readiness probe can overload the database. A permissive count returning zero after an error can also mask a dependency failure if interpreted carelessly. Deeper checks improve diagnosis and cost more I/O. Separate cheap frequent probes from scheduled expensive consistency checks. Define probe behavior when the process is alive, the vector store is down, and the index is stale but readable. Explain whether traffic should continue in each case."}, {"id": 117, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "What operational signals should be monitored?", "text": "What operational signals should be monitored? Track ingestion lag, scan authority failures, embedding retries and queue pressure, active-generation age, job backlog and oldest age, search latency, empty and failed retrieval rates, evidence truncation, and quality regressions. Each signal should connect to a user-visible consequence. Events and health checks expose stage and lifecycle information. Optional OpenTelemetry integration can connect this information to a host's tracing system without making it a mandatory runtime dependency. Large numbers of document IDs or tenant IDs as metric labels create cardinality and privacy problems. Raw query logging can expose sensitive user intent. Aggregated metrics are scalable but less detailed; controlled traces aid diagnosis. Correlate with sanitized operation identifiers and use retention policies appropriate to the data. Define an alert for rising stale-index age and another for rising backend-failure rate. Explain why their remediation differs even if both reduce answer quality."}, {"id": 118, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How should a system meet an end-to-end latency target?", "text": "How should a system meet an end-to-end latency target? Allocate a request deadline across stages, measure tail latency, and propagate cancellation and remaining time. Per-call timeouts and retry ceilings are ingredients, not an end-to-end deadline. Query variants, sequential stages, reranking, and generation can each consume the budget. EmbeddingExecutor and query transformation expose local bounds; telemetry gives stage durations. The host must compose them into a product-level deadline and decide which optional stages can be skipped safely. Summing independent p95 measurements does not yield an exact request p95, and a timeout can leave remote work running. Retry storms can worsen the same overload they attempt to recover from. Tight deadlines improve responsiveness while reducing recovery and ranking opportunities. A slower asynchronous ingestion path can tolerate different retry policy from an interactive search. Trace a request with one retry and two query variants. Compare actual elapsed time with your budget and identify which optional stage should be shed first."}, {"id": 119, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How do you roll out a retrieval change safely?", "text": "How do you roll out a retrieval change safely? Pin the corpus and configuration, evaluate the candidate offline, compare shadow traffic where permitted, deploy to a limited audience, and watch quality plus operational signals. Preserve a rollback path to the prior profile, generation, and strategy configuration. Explicit snapshots, profiles, typed requests, and replaceable strategies make changes observable. They do not automatically implement feature flags, traffic splitting, or deployment rollback; the host supplies those controls. A model change can alter score distributions and invalidate thresholds, caches, and labels. A schema change can also break an older application version during rollback. Parallel operation costs resources but exposes regressions before broad impact. A direct cutover is simpler and appropriate only when risk and recovery are well understood. Write a release plan for enabling reranking with acceptance thresholds, cost limits, denial tests, and a one-step configuration rollback."}, {"id": 120, "chapter": "Evaluation, testing, and operations", "slug": "evaluation", "title": "How should you investigate quality drift over time?", "text": "How should you investigate quality drift over time? Separate source drift, query-distribution drift, model or provider changes, extraction changes, index freshness, and application-policy changes. Re-run a stable benchmark and inspect newly failing real-world cases. A single aggregate score rarely identifies the cause. Profiles and ingestion snapshots record important configuration identity, while telemetry and evaluation capture operational and quality observations. A host should retain run metadata tying them together. Silent provider updates or hidden preprocessing changes may not be visible unless a model revision and adapter configuration are recorded. Privacy limits also constrain how production questions can be retained for analysis. Stable benchmarks preserve comparability but can become unrepresentative. Add reviewed new cases while keeping a fixed core set for historical comparison. Simulate an unchanged corpus with a changed query mix, then a changed extractor with the old query mix. Show how you would distinguish the resulting metric changes."}, {"id": 121, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "How do AI, ML, DL, NLP, and GenAI relate?", "text": "How do AI, ML, DL, NLP, and GenAI relate? Artificial Intelligence is the broad field of building systems that perform intelligent tasks. Machine Learning learns behavior from data. Deep Learning uses layered neural networks. Natural Language Processing concerns language tasks. Generative AI produces new outputs such as text. A RAG application combines these ideas with conventional data and backend engineering. Ragnor can call learned embedding and reranking providers, but much of its code is deterministic orchestration, validation, indexing, and coordination. Understanding that division prevents attributing every system behavior to a neural model. Acronyms can hide missing skills. Knowing how to call a model API is different from understanding representation learning, and understanding attention is different from securing a multi-tenant API. An AI full-stack engineer needs practical breadth and selective depth. Research-level model training is not required for every product, but model limitations must inform system design. Label each project module as deterministic infrastructure, learned-model adapter, or host application boundary. Explain why several modules belong to more than one learning discipline."}, {"id": 122, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What does attention compute, and why does context length matter?", "text": "What does attention compute, and why does context length matter? Scaled dot-product attention forms weights from queries and keys, then combines value vectors: softmax(QK-transpose divided by square-root d) times V. This lets token representations depend on other tokens. Standard dense attention has pairwise interactions whose work and memory grow quadratically with sequence length, though implementations vary. A retrieval application supplies selected context rather than every document. Ragnor's evidence budget controls that supplied material; it does not implement Transformer attention or determine a provider's internal serving architecture. A model accepting a long context does not guarantee equal use of every passage. More evidence can increase cost and distraction, so context limits and useful context are different concepts. Larger context can preserve more information while reducing the pressure to retrieve precisely. Retrieval remains useful for selection, access control, attribution, and cost even with long windows. Draw token-to-token attention for four tokens, then estimate how pair counts change when sequence length doubles."}, {"id": 123, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "How are encoder embeddings, decoder generation, bi-encoders, and cross-encoders different?", "text": "How are encoder embeddings, decoder generation, bi-encoders, and cross-encoders different? An embedding encoder maps text into a reusable representation. A decoder language model generates tokens conditioned on prior context. A bi-encoder embeds query and document separately for efficient comparison. A cross-encoder jointly processes a query-document pair for more detailed scoring. Ragnor's embedding interface fits reusable vector production, while the Reranker protocol permits more expensive pairwise relevance scoring. The application generator remains outside the ordinary search pipeline. A provider's model name or API endpoint does not prove which architecture or training objective it uses. Follow its documented task contract and validate retrieval quality rather than assuming all embeddings or rerankers are interchangeable. Separate encoding enables indexing documents once and searching quickly. Joint scoring spends more work per pair and often belongs after candidate retrieval. Estimate how many model computations are needed to retrieve from 100,000 documents using precomputed bi-encoder vectors versus scoring every pair with a cross-encoder."}, {"id": 124, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What is contrastive learning, and why does it matter for retrieval?", "text": "What is contrastive learning, and why does it matter for retrieval? Contrastive learning trains representations so relevant pairs score above irrelevant pairs. A common objective compares a query's positive passage with negatives using a softmax-based loss. Negative selection, label quality, and the task distribution shape what similarity means. Ragnor consumes pretrained embedding functions rather than training them. Understanding their objective helps explain why a general embedding may confuse domain-specific exceptions or why a specialized model improves some queries and harms others. False negatives can teach a model to separate two genuinely relevant passages. Training leakage can also inflate benchmark scores, and same-dimension models remain incompatible despite similar training goals. Domain adaptation can improve ranking but needs representative data, compute, evaluation, and a new index generation. Better query and chunk design may solve the problem more cheaply. Create one query, one positive passage, one easy negative, and one hard negative. Explain what an accidental false negative would teach."}, {"id": 125, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What are pretraining, SFT, and instruction tuning?", "text": "What are pretraining, SFT, and instruction tuning? Pretraining learns broad statistical structure from large datasets. Supervised Fine-Tuning trains on task examples or desired responses. Instruction tuning is supervised adaptation aimed at following instructions across tasks. These change model parameters; indexing new documents changes external knowledge storage instead. The repository does not include a model-training pipeline. Its datasets and answer evaluators support application evaluation, not an automatic SFT trainer. A host can use evaluation failures to decide whether behavior adaptation is warranted. Fine-tuning on private facts can create memorization and retention problems without solving freshness or citation. Poor examples can also teach confident unsupported answering. Behavior tuning may improve format and task consistency, while retrieval is usually more direct for changing private documents. Combining them requires separate evaluation of behavior and knowledge access. Divide twenty failures into missing evidence, bad ranking, bad prompt policy, and persistent behavioral problems. Choose which, if any, justify SFT."}, {"id": 126, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What are LoRA, PEFT, and quantization?", "text": "What are LoRA, PEFT, and quantization? Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning adapts a subset or compact parameterization instead of updating all weights. LoRA is one PEFT method that adds low-rank updates to frozen weights. Quantization represents weights or activations with reduced numerical precision; it addresses memory and compute rather than directly supplying new knowledge. None of these training or serving methods is implemented by Ragnor. They belong to the model layer a host may select, while embedding-profile changes still require compatible indexing and evaluation. Reduced precision or adaptation can change quality, and an efficient technique is not automatically safe for every model or hardware target. Avoid promising universal speedups or equal accuracy. These methods can reduce adaptation or serving resources while adding tooling and compatibility considerations. Provider-hosted models transfer much of that operational work elsewhere. Compare full fine-tuning, LoRA adaptation, and quantized inference in a table of what changes, what stays frozen, and which evaluation is needed."}, {"id": 127, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "How do RLHF and DPO differ from RAG?", "text": "How do RLHF and DPO differ from RAG? Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback uses human preference information to shape model behavior, often through a learned reward and policy optimization. Direct Preference Optimization trains from preferred and rejected response pairs without the same explicit reinforcement-learning loop. RAG supplies external evidence at inference time. These methods operate at different layers and can coexist. This repository provides retrieval and evaluation components, not RLHF or DPO training. Its answer-quality failures may motivate better behavior data, but indexing is not preference optimization. Preferences can reward pleasing or verbose answers instead of faithful ones. A preference-trained model can still hallucinate, and a retrieved passage can still be wrong. Alignment training addresses behavioral tendencies and requires datasets and training infrastructure. Retrieval addresses access to evidence and requires corpus infrastructure. Neither replaces the other's evaluation. Write a preferred and rejected answer pair where the preference is justified by citation faithfulness. Explain what that pair teaches and what source freshness it cannot teach permanently."}, {"id": 128, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What are temperature, top-p, tokens, and structured output?", "text": "What are temperature, top-p, tokens, and structured output? Tokens are the model's discrete input and output units. Temperature changes the sharpness of sampling probabilities; top-p restricts sampling to a cumulative probability mass. Structured output constrains response form, such as a schema. These are generation controls, not retrieval relevance measures. A host chooses generation parameters and serialization outside the library's search path. Evidence budgets reserve output capacity, but the actual generator must honor the chosen output contract and handle parse or validation failures. Low temperature does not guarantee truth or reproducibility across providers. Valid JSON can still contain unsupported claims or unauthorized source references. Tighter structure improves integration and can constrain expressive answers. More stochastic generation can help creative tasks while making faithful factual workflows harder to evaluate. Define an answer schema with status, text, and citation references. Validate citation membership against the actual EvidenceBundle rather than only validating JSON syntax."}, {"id": 129, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "What are HyDE, agentic RAG, GraphRAG, and multimodal RAG?", "text": "What are HyDE, agentic RAG, GraphRAG, and multimodal RAG? HyDE retrieves using an embedding of a generated hypothetical answer or document. Agentic RAG lets a controller choose repeated retrieval or tool actions. GraphRAG adds graph relationships to retrieval or synthesis. Multimodal RAG includes non-text modalities. These are families of techniques, not automatically enabled project features. Query-transform, strategy, tool-bridge, and extractor seams can support experiments, but a host must implement the actual controller, graph, modality representation, and evaluation. A source connector alone does not create multimodal reasoning. Each added generative or traversal step expands cost and failure modes. Hypothetical content can introduce invented facts; agents can loop; graph edges can be stale; modality extraction can lose evidence fidelity. Advanced methods can solve demonstrated complex cases while making the baseline harder to understand and operate. Add them incrementally with bounded execution. Pick one baseline failure and design the smallest extension that addresses it. Define a cost ceiling and a benchmark that could prove the extension unnecessary."}, {"id": 130, "chapter": "Deep learning and generative AI", "slug": "models", "title": "How should an AI full-stack engineer learn deep learning alongside this project?", "text": "How should an AI full-stack engineer learn deep learning alongside this project? Learn vectors and similarity first, then tokenization, neural training objectives, attention, model adaptation, and inference. Tie each topic to an observable product failure: incompatible vectors, poor paraphrase recall, context overflow, unsupported generation, or latency under load. The code offers a concrete laboratory for interfaces around learned systems while keeping infrastructure inspectable. Training a small encoder or language model is a separate learning exercise that deepens understanding without requiring a rewrite of the retrieval subsystem. Studying only theory misses deployment and security; studying only APIs leaves model behavior mysterious. A portfolio should demonstrate both a measured model comparison and a robust application boundary. Breadth requires sustained practice. Sequence small, testable projects rather than attempting to master distributed storage and large-model training simultaneously. Follow the roadmap labs, then train a tiny contrastive encoder on a reviewed toy dataset. Compare it with the hashing baseline and a pretrained model using the same held-out queries."}, {"id": 131, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "What application components are still needed around Ragnor?", "text": "What application components are still needed around Ragnor? A complete product needs identity, authorized API routes, a document-management workflow, durable adapters, background workers, generation policy, a user interface, monitoring, deployment, and evaluation. The repository supplies important subsystem pieces and examples, not all of these production services. Build around the evidence contract: the API obtains authorized evidence, then a host generator produces an answer, while the UI can open source spans and show status. This keeps source verification useful even without generation. Assuming the library owns users or migrations can leave critical responsibilities unimplemented. A polished chat screen does not compensate for missing tenant isolation or recovery behavior. Explicit host ownership requires more work but fits an existing backend. A monolithic demo can be quicker and should be labeled according to its actual guarantees. Draw your target application with browser, API, workers, identity provider, source system, vector store, snapshot store, and model provider. Mark every trust boundary."}, {"id": 132, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How would you design the search and chat API contracts?", "text": "How would you design the search and chat API contracts? Expose structured request and response objects with query, permitted collection choice, bounded retrieval options, operation identity, status, evidence, and citations. Resolve user identity and allowed scope on the server. Keep internal database names and arbitrary backend filters out of the public API unless deliberately supported. RetrievalRequest and EvidenceBundle provide useful internal shapes, but the host should create a stable transport contract rather than expose all library internals. Errors should distinguish validation, denied access, backend outage, and successful empty search. Letting a browser select raw table names or trusted client IDs bypasses the composition boundary. Returning provider exceptions directly may leak secrets or infrastructure details. A narrow API is safer and easier to evolve; an expert administration API may expose more controls under stronger permissions. Write example request and response JSON for success, denied scope, and backend failure. Explain which fields the server derives instead of trusting the client."}, {"id": 133, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How should streaming work without misleading the user?", "text": "How should streaming work without misleading the user? Complete authorization and evidence selection before presenting factual generated claims as supported. Stream generation events with an operation ID and explicit stages, then preserve a final status and citation mapping. The exact HTTP transport is a host design choice, not a provided chat server feature. Evidence assembly produces a stable bundle before a generator consumes it. The UI can show searching, assembling, generating, complete, cancelled, or failed states without pretending that partial text is a verified final answer. A network disconnect can leave server work running, and partial output may contain incomplete citation syntax. Propagate cancellation where possible and never silently mark a truncated stream complete. Early streaming improves perceived responsiveness while complicating error recovery and state handling. A non-streaming first version is easier to validate. Design events for evidence_ready, answer_delta, complete, and failure. Simulate a disconnect after two deltas and specify what remains visible and retryable."}, {"id": 134, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "What should a useful RAG interface show besides the answer text?", "text": "What should a useful RAG interface show besides the answer text? Show source titles and bounded excerpts, revision or freshness when relevant, citation navigation, loading and failure states, and a clear way to refine an ambiguous question. Make evidence accessible by keyboard and avoid presenting similarity as factual confidence. The bundle already contains attribution and status data that a frontend can render. SourceLookup provides a verification path when the user opens a citation; that path must retain the same identity and scope policy. Rendering document text as raw HTML can create cross-site scripting. Use safe text rendering or a deliberately sanitized markup pipeline, and treat citation URLs as untrusted input. More evidence improves transparency but can overwhelm users. Start with concise source summaries and progressive disclosure of full spans. Build a search-only UI before chat. Include empty, stale, failed, and insufficient-budget examples, then test it entirely with keyboard navigation."}, {"id": 135, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How should frontend state be separated from backend truth?", "text": "How should frontend state be separated from backend truth? Keep local interaction state such as expanded sources or draft input separate from server state such as operation status, evidence, and active document versions. Key cached results by the relevant identity, scope, query, and configuration or revision where necessary. Ragnor returns structured results, leaving the browser framework and caching library to the host. A frontend must preserve status distinctions and invalidate data when permissions or active generations change. Reusing a browser cache across sign-out or tenant switching can expose another user's evidence. Optimistically marking ingestion complete before activation can also misrepresent searchability. Explicit invalidation and cache partitioning add complexity but preserve correct user expectations. A simple no-cache first implementation can be preferable to unsafe reuse. Switch tenants while a request is in flight. Ensure its late response cannot populate the new tenant's visible state or source drawer."}, {"id": 136, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How should document upload and indexing progress be designed?", "text": "How should document upload and indexing progress be designed? Treat upload acceptance, source storage, extraction, embedding, activation, and searchability as distinct states. Persist the accepted source and enqueue background work according to the host's transaction policy. Return an operation identifier rather than blocking the request until every provider call completes. Outbox and drainer interfaces decouple request latency from processing. Sync reports and events provide lifecycle information that a host can translate into progress, while active visibility determines when content can actually be searched. A successful upload can later fail extraction or embedding. The UI must not claim indexed status solely because bytes were received, and retry actions must avoid duplicate logical documents. Background workflows need polling or event delivery and durable state. Synchronous ingestion is simpler for small local experiments but less resilient under provider latency. Define a document state machine from uploaded to searchable, with retry and failed states. Associate each transition with the component that can authoritatively report it."}, {"id": 137, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "What does production deployment require beyond installing optional extras?", "text": "What does production deployment require beyond installing optional extras? Provision durable stores and schemas, configure credentials and network access, run workers, set identity and permissions, select compatible profiles, validate startup, and establish backups, monitoring, and rollback. An optional dependency installs client code; it does not provision or secure its service. pyproject.toml separates backend extras from dependency-free core, while health checks and integration examples help validate wiring. Actual database indexes and extension configuration remain deployment responsibilities. Multiple replicas using in-memory manifests or rate limits do not share authoritative state. Secrets in configuration fingerprints or public diagnostics can also create exposure. Managed services reduce some operations work and add cost or provider dependence. Self-hosting gives control and requires deeper operational expertise. Produce a deployment inventory with ownership, persistence, backup, restart behavior, and secret source for every component. Include what happens when one replica restarts."}, {"id": 138, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How would you plan capacity and handle overload?", "text": "How would you plan capacity and handle overload? Model request arrival, per-stage service time, concurrency, queue capacity, database pools, and provider quotas. Apply admission control before expensive work, bound jobs, and shed optional processing when appropriate. Interactive search and batch ingestion should not starve each other accidentally. The executor semaphore, transport limiter, job store, and health signals are local building blocks. The host must coordinate quotas across replicas and enforce end-to-end budgets. Increasing worker count can increase throttling rather than throughput. An unbounded backlog hides overload until memory, storage, or freshness collapses. Separate worker pools and queues improve isolation but add infrastructure. A shared small deployment is economical until contention becomes measurable. Estimate sustainable throughput from provider concurrency and observed call duration, then load-test around that estimate. Report queue age as well as requests per second."}, {"id": 139, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "What portfolio project would demonstrate AI full-stack competence?", "text": "What portfolio project would demonstrate AI full-stack competence? Build a multi-tenant knowledge assistant with a search-first interface, revision-aware citations, background ingestion, a grounded answer policy, and a reproducible evaluation report. Show a failure and its diagnosis, not only a polished happy-path recording. This combines frontend state, API design, identity, async jobs, data modeling, retrieval, generation, evaluation, and operations. Ragnor provides the subsystem foundation while your work proves the application integration. A public demo can accidentally expose private sample documents or provider credentials. Use a reviewed fixture corpus and document implemented guarantees rather than aspirational architecture. A narrow reliable project is more convincing than many unfinished advanced features. Add reranking or multi-query only when your benchmark shows why they help. Deliver a README with architecture, a threat-boundary map, benchmark results, three recovery demonstrations, and a five-minute walkthrough that opens a cited source revision."}, {"id": 140, "chapter": "From subsystem to AI full-stack product", "slug": "fullstack", "title": "How should I answer a senior system-design interview using this project?", "text": "How should I answer a senior system-design interview using this project? Start with requirements and constraints, then describe the offline and online flows, data identities, permission boundary, consistency model, failure recovery, quality evaluation, and capacity plan. Defend choices with source-backed mechanisms and acknowledge which guarantees depend on host adapters. The project's strongest lessons are explicit boundaries: prepare versus commit, candidates versus final evidence, logical visibility versus physical cleanup, and retrieval quality versus answer faithfulness. These distinctions scale beyond one library. Memorized pattern names and invented timing claims collapse under follow-up questions. Be ready to explain a crash interleaving, an authorization failure, and a measured quality trade-off in concrete terms. A good design is conditional on requirements. State when a keyword-only system, one transactional database, or a simpler synchronous workflow would be the better choice. Give a 45-minute mock interview: five minutes requirements, ten architecture, ten consistency and security, ten evaluation and scale, and ten trade-offs and alternatives."}, {"id": 141, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "What does the insurance backend add beyond the Ragnor subsystem?", "text": "What does the insurance backend add beyond the Ragnor subsystem? OmniCare supplies FastAPI routes, authenticated users, claims and policies, an ADK agent, confirmation workflows, persistent conversation history, background jobs, evaluation bindings, and observability. Ragnor remains a dependency providing retrieval infrastructure. A library capability and an application using that capability are separate implementation claims. pyproject.toml declares ragnor[pgvector], google-adk, LiteLLM, LangSmith, SQLAlchemy, and FastAPI. Agent construction uses LlmAgent and a tool registry; domain adapters bind generic retrieval to concrete tables. This is the real host that the first fourteen chapters discussed abstractly. Do not infer that every newer Ragnor feature is integrated. The policy tool concatenates retrieved context directly; it does not automatically use the library's full EvidenceAssembler and revision-lookup contract. A narrow host binding is easier to ship, while richer evidence contracts add explicit statuses and provenance. Adoption requires wiring and migration rather than only upgrading a dependency. Trace a coverage question from the chat route through ADK, query_policy, the domain retriever, and PostgreSQL. Mark each conversion in result shape."}, {"id": 142, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "Which framework actually controls the agent loop?", "text": "Which framework actually controls the agent loop? Google ADK controls the current agent loop through LlmAgent and Runner. LiteLLM adapts model access. Ragnor retrieves evidence. LangSmith observes execution. These packages occupy different layers; LangSmith's presence does not mean LangChain controls orchestration. create_agent passes a LiteLlm model, reviewed instructions, registered handlers, and a before-model history callback to LlmAgent. No LangGraph graph or A2A server is constructed by this path. Framework confusion leads to examples that cannot run against the project's dependencies. The later LangChain, LangGraph, and A2A chapters explicitly describe alternatives or additions rather than pretending those frameworks are already installed. Staying with one orchestrator reduces state-model overlap. Adding another can solve a measured workflow need but introduces serialization, tracing, error, and deployment boundaries. Make a dependency-layer table with web framework, agent runtime, model adapter, retrieval library, persistence layer, and tracing service. Name their concrete constructors."}, {"id": 143, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "How does a coverage question differ from a claim-status question?", "text": "How does a coverage question differ from a claim-status question? Coverage requires retrieving policy language and applying a reviewed procedure to the user's facts. Claim status is a lookup of structured application state under the authenticated owner's scope. An answer about a policy is evidence-based interpretation; an answer about an existing claim must reflect the actual recorded claim. The registry exposes query_policy, get_procedure, get_claim_status, and search_claims as distinct tools. Known claim IDs route to exact lookup; vague references route to owner-scoped semantic search. Semantic similarity cannot establish that an arbitrary claim ID belongs to the user or that a policy provision applies to their contract. Tool-side identity checks remain necessary even when prompting selects the right tool. Separate tools make intent and authority clearer but require route-selection evaluation. One universal search tool is simpler and can blur exact facts with approximate matches. Write expected tool sequences for an exact claim ID, a remembered incident without an ID, and a coverage question with missing facts."}, {"id": 144, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "What is the difference between an agent session and a conversation record?", "text": "What is the difference between an agent session and a conversation record? Agent sessions hold runtime state and events used by ADK. Conversation records hold application-visible messages, ownership, archival state, and IDs used by feedback and history APIs. They overlap in content but serve different contracts and lifecycle requirements. agent.py defines session storage and replay, while conversation_store.py persists chat turns. The before-model callback can rehydrate recent history from conversation messages. Persistent chat history therefore does not by itself prove persistent runtime state. Divergence can replay a turn twice, omit tool results, or show UI messages that the agent cannot recall. Switching storage modes also needs a deliberate migration and restart story. Separate stores decouple product history from runtime implementation but require clear reconciliation and retention ownership. A single store reduces duplication and can bind the product tightly to one framework. Trace which IDs identify the user, conversation, ADK session, invocation, user message, and assistant message. Explain which remain stable after a restart."}, {"id": 145, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "What is the real prepare-and-confirm boundary for claim creation?", "text": "What is the real prepare-and-confirm boundary for claim creation? Preparation validates fields and stores a pending submission with a confirmation token and expiry. Confirmation is an authenticated API operation that locks the pending submission, creates the claim and associated records, marks completion, and commits. Agent prose does not itself complete the financial workflow. The model-facing registry includes prepare_claim_submission, while submit_claim_internal is reached through the confirmation route. This preserves a server-side boundary between collecting intent and applying an authoritative write. The prompt's phrase that a tool call is the confirmation action can be misunderstood. The actual database submission still requires the separate route. A textual yes from the model is not proof that the API operation succeeded. Two steps add user interaction and pending-state management while reducing accidental writes. A one-step workflow is faster but gives less opportunity to review material fields. Follow one token from preparation to a locked confirmation transaction. Identify which operation first makes the claim durable."}, {"id": 146, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "Why separate policy procedures from policy facts?", "text": "Why separate policy procedures from policy facts? A procedure describes how to investigate a question or route an unclear request. A policy source supplies actual limits, deductibles, exclusions, and conditions. The procedure should not become an alternative authority for contractual facts merely because it is easier to retrieve. The prompt asks for coverage-review before a determination and query_policy for the underlying facts. Procedure ingestion and retrieval have their own domain modules and version records. A stale procedure can direct an incomplete investigation, while a current procedure can still be combined with the wrong policy revision. Both source identities need to be visible in evaluation and operational diagnosis. Separating reasoning guidance from factual material supports independent review but adds retrieval calls and version combinations. Hardcoding all instructions in one prompt is easier to start and harder to govern. Label every sentence in the coverage-review playbook as a workflow rule or factual claim. Move any unsupported numeric policy claim back to the authoritative source layer."}, {"id": 147, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "Where does eventual consistency appear to a policyholder?", "text": "Where does eventual consistency appear to a policyholder? A claim can be durably submitted before its embedding job completes, so exact structured lookup and semantic search may temporarily disagree. Notification delivery can also lag the claim transaction. These are different read and delivery projections of one business operation. Submission creates a claim plus pending embedding work in one transaction. Workers later process indexing and notification tasks. The UI should distinguish accepted, searchable, and notified rather than treating one successful HTTP response as completion of every downstream effect. A search miss immediately after filing must not be reported as proof that no claim exists. An authoritative exact lookup can confirm persistence while the index catches up. Asynchronous processing keeps submission independent of provider latency but introduces lag and recovery work. Synchronous indexing is simpler conceptually and couples business availability to model services. Pause the embedding worker, file a claim, and compare exact lookup with semantic search. Define the appropriate user-visible status for each result."}, {"id": 148, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "What are the main trust boundaries in this application?", "text": "What are the main trust boundaries in this application? Boundaries include browser-to-API identity, model-to-tool authority, source-to-prompt content, application-to-database privileges, application-to-model disclosure, and application-to-observability export. Each boundary has a different actor and failure mode. JWT validation establishes a user, ContextVar carries that identity to tools, domain queries constrain records, and tracing can export execution data. Authorization must remain effective even when the model selects the wrong action. A protected chat endpoint is insufficient if a tool trusts model-supplied owner IDs or a trace exports private claim text. Read and write paths require separate evidence of enforcement. Explicit boundaries add policy and testing effort but make failures diagnosable. Combining all access behind a powerful agent credential simplifies integration and magnifies mistakes. Draw the insurance runtime diagram and annotate every edge with identity, permitted data, and whether the receiver can initiate authoritative writes."}, {"id": 149, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "Which application guarantees are implemented, and which remain conditional?", "text": "Which application guarantees are implemented, and which remain conditional? Implemented mechanisms include owner-scoped claims retrieval, a locked confirmation row, caller-owned submission transactions, typed streaming events, optional shared idempotency, and trace instrumentation. Their deployment guarantees depend on real database behavior, chosen storage settings, provider configuration, and complete use of the intended paths. Configuration defaults session_storage to memory; a database-backed service exists but is not automatically selected in every deployment. Administrative claim transitions also use a currently empty allowlist despite the broader role-support code. Reading a comment about deployment defaults or a role model as proof of configured behavior creates false confidence. Follow defaults and call sites rather than naming available classes. Conservative defaults support local development but may require production gates. Rejecting startup on missing critical state is stricter than silently falling back to local state. Create a deployment evidence checklist showing the selected session, idempotency, rate-limit, and tracing backends without printing secrets."}, {"id": 150, "chapter": "The insurance application end to end", "slug": "insurance-system", "title": "How should the first fourteen chapters be reinterpreted against this host?", "text": "How should the first fourteen chapters be reinterpreted against this host? Their library concepts remain useful, but each host guarantee needs its own wiring proof. Generic strict retrieval does not mean this host preserves errors, a revision-aware evidence type does not mean policy citations include revisions, and a generic outbox protocol does not prove the concrete adapter's claim semantics. The coverage audit maps every original question to an insurance extension while preserving Q1–140 and saved progress. The new source atlas pins both repositories so readers can compare capabilities with their actual integration. Architectural diagrams can accidentally describe an ideal composition rather than the running path. The new diagrams label proposed integrations and the handbook records observed gaps beside the relevant mechanism. Cross-project tracing takes more effort than a single overview but teaches integration judgment, the central skill in full-stack AI engineering. Pick three earlier answers and find the corresponding host call site. Write one sentence of confirmed behavior and one remaining integration condition for each."}, {"id": 151, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "Why store insurance claims in relational tables instead of only vector documents?", "text": "Why store insurance claims in relational tables instead of only vector documents? Claims have exact ownership, monetary amounts, lifecycle states, references, and transaction boundaries. A vector index optimizes similarity retrieval and does not replace those relational invariants. The same claim can have an authoritative row and a derived search representation. Claim maps UUID identity, a unique human-readable claim_id, owner_id, Numeric amount, timestamps, and search columns. Submission and lifecycle routes update business state, while embedding jobs maintain retrieval data. Treating the vector record as the sole source of truth makes exact authorization, status transitions, and recovery dependent on a derived index. Search ranking cannot decide whether a claim exists or who owns it. Relational plus search modeling creates projection maintenance work but supports exact operations and flexible discovery. A text-only prototype is smaller and insufficient for authoritative workflows. Mark every Claim column as identity, business fact, authority constraint, lifecycle metadata, or derived search data. Explain which can be rebuilt."}, {"id": 152, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "Why have both a UUID primary key and a human-readable claim ID?", "text": "Why have both a UUID primary key and a human-readable claim ID? A UUID provides an internal identifier suitable for references; a separate claim ID provides a support-friendly external label. They have different display and collision properties. Neither identifier should be treated as an authorization secret. The model uses a UUID primary key and a unique claim_id. Submission generates the external identifier from a shortened UUID fragment, so uniqueness is ultimately enforced by the database constraint rather than guaranteed by formatting. Shortened random identifiers can collide. A production collision-handling path should distinguish a retryable uniqueness conflict from unrelated database errors, and users must still be scoped even when an ID is known. Friendly IDs improve support and communication while adding an extra index and identity mapping. Full opaque IDs are simpler but awkward to read aloud. Explain how an identifier collision differs from an idempotent replay. Design a bounded generation retry that does not duplicate a pending submission."}, {"id": 153, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "Why use Numeric and Decimal for claim amounts?", "text": "Why use Numeric and Decimal for claim amounts? Decimal arithmetic and a fixed-scale Numeric column represent monetary values more predictably than binary floating point. Currency precision, rounding, allowed scale, and upper bounds are part of the domain contract rather than formatting details. Claim.amount uses Numeric(12,2), and preparation serializes the validated Decimal as a string in JSONB. The confirmation route converts that string to float before calling the internal function, which is an observed precision boundary worth revisiting. A Decimal column cannot retroactively undo precision lost earlier in JSON parsing, float conversion, or arithmetic. Validation and serialization must preserve the intended amount end to end. Decimal values need explicit JSON handling and careful provider-tool schemas. Float is convenient for generic numeric tools but weakens exact-money guarantees. Trace an amount with cents from browser JSON through Pydantic, pending JSONB, confirmation, and SQL storage. Propose a Decimal-preserving signature and matching tests."}, {"id": 154, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "How do foreign keys, unique constraints, and check constraints complement application validation?", "text": "How do foreign keys, unique constraints, and check constraints complement application validation? Application validation gives useful error messages; database constraints protect against every writer sharing the database. Foreign keys preserve references, unique constraints enforce identity rules, and check constraints reject invalid row-level values even when a route is bypassed. Claim.owner_id references users and claim_id is unique. The embedding outbox explicitly documents that claim_uuid has no foreign key, making transactional enqueue discipline important. Not every Python enum is automatically a database-level constraint. Assuming all invariants are enforced because ORM types exist leaves gaps for scripts, workers, and future routes. Constraints also need deliberate delete and migration behavior. Strong constraints simplify consistency reasoning but can complicate staged migrations and bulk imports. Relaxing them requires another proven enforcement mechanism. Compare the Claim, ClaimSubmission, EmbeddingJob, and ClaimEvent models with their migrations. List invariants enforced only in Python and those enforced by PostgreSQL."}, {"id": 155, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "What belongs in normalized columns versus JSONB?", "text": "What belongs in normalized columns versus JSONB? Put fields used for relationships, strict constraints, frequent filtering, and stable reporting in typed columns. JSONB is useful for variable event payloads or a prepared-form snapshot. Its flexibility does not remove the need for schema versions, validation, or selective indexing. Claims use explicit business columns, while pending submissions and agent events store JSON structures. This lets preparation retain a reviewed request without immediately creating the final claim, and lets the runtime serialize framework events. Unversioned JSON can become unreadable after schema changes. Copying an entire stale state blob can also overwrite concurrent updates, while arbitrary JSON may retain more private data than needed. JSONB speeds evolving payloads and weakens some relational guarantees. Normalize stable, high-value fields instead of treating the database as a dictionary with no contracts. Design a version field and migration reader for a prepared submission whose incident_date field was added later. Explain the fallback for older records."}, {"id": 156, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "Why does the owner-and-created-at composite index matter?", "text": "Why does the owner-and-created-at composite index matter? The claim list filters by owner and orders by creation time. An index beginning with owner_id and then created_at can support that access pattern more directly than two unrelated indexes. Index design should follow real predicates, ordering, and pagination. Claim declares ix_claims_owner_id_created_at with both columns, alongside a GIN index for tsvector. These indexes serve different query families: exact scoped listings and lexical search. An index declaration does not prove an efficient plan at production data sizes. Additional sort keys, selective predicates, and row estimates can change the plan; unused indexes still cost writes and storage. More indexes accelerate selected reads while slowing mutation and increasing maintenance. Validate with representative EXPLAIN plans rather than indexing every column reflexively. Compare the owner-scoped listing plan with and without the composite index in a disposable database. Include a tie-breaking ID if you design keyset pagination."}, {"id": 157, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "How do policy versions differ from current policy chunks?", "text": "How do policy versions differ from current policy chunks? A policy version records the source and indexing configuration over time. Policy chunks are searchable material for a version. Keeping version records does not necessarily mean every historical chunk remains retained or that a query selects the contract applicable to an incident. PolicyVersion records effective dates, source_hash, embedding information, and chunking settings. The host policy retrieval spec exposes section and source metadata but not a complete incident-date and policy-entitlement selection contract. Current-source retrieval can answer the wrong historical question even when its text is accurately quoted. Historical evidence needs both retention and an explicit applicable-version filter. Retaining every version supports audits and costs storage and selection logic. Current-only indexing is simpler and must state its scope honestly. Create two policy revisions with different deductibles and one incident between their effective dates. Define the query and citation fields required to choose correctly."}, {"id": 158, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "Why use Alembic migrations instead of create_all for production evolution?", "text": "Why use Alembic migrations instead of create_all for production evolution? create_all creates missing schema objects represented in current metadata; it is not a reviewed sequence for changing existing columns, backfilling data, or coordinating old and new application versions. Migrations record explicit upgrade steps and dependencies. The backend has Alembic revisions for claim dates, policy versions, audit records, jobs, and evaluation runs. database.py labels create_all_tables for development and CI. Agent session tables are a separate runtime-owned create_all exception. Treating all schema as migration-owned overlooks that exception. Concurrent startup and runtime DDL require operational planning, and a failed migration must not leave an apparently healthy service using an incompatible schema. Migrations add review and deployment sequencing but make history inspectable. Runtime creation is convenient for framework state and harder to govern consistently. Inventory which tables are created through Alembic and which through agent.py. Propose a single-owner policy for production schema evolution."}, {"id": 159, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "What is an expand-and-contract migration?", "text": "What is an expand-and-contract migration? Add a compatible schema surface first, deploy code that can read old and new forms, backfill safely, switch writers, then remove the old form in a later release. The sequence allows rolling deployment and rollback without requiring every process to switch simultaneously. This is a proposed deployment method for evolving the insurance schemas, not proof that every existing migration follows it. New incident-date or money-handling fields are concrete opportunities to reason about old rows and older workers. A backfill can hold locks or overload the database; a destructive cleanup can make application rollback impossible. Background jobs may also contain payloads written by an older schema version. Compatibility periods increase temporary complexity and reduce deployment coupling. A single destructive migration is shorter and can require a maintenance window. Design a migration from float-shaped tool input to explicit decimal-string amounts. Include old pending submissions and jobs, not just current Claim rows."}, {"id": 160, "chapter": "Relational data modeling and migrations", "slug": "database-modeling", "title": "How should you model authoritative events versus derived projections?", "text": "How should you model authoritative events versus derived projections? ClaimEvent records lifecycle history; Claim.status is a current-state projection. Embedding records and notifications are downstream projections. Agent session events describe runtime execution, while audit events describe selected high-value actions. These logs are related but not interchangeable event-sourcing systems. The backend persists claim transitions and outbox work within business transactions. It separately records observability and audit information, each with different failure policy and consumers. Calling any append-only table event sourcing overstates replayability. A complete event-sourced design needs sufficient immutable events, deterministic projection rebuilding, versioning, and a declared source of truth. Current rows plus a transition trail provide useful history with lower complexity. Full event sourcing improves replay options and adds substantial schema and operational obligations. For each event table, state whether it can rebuild a Claim exactly. Identify missing facts before claiming replay-based recovery."}, {"id": 161, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "How does SELECT FOR UPDATE prevent duplicate confirmation?", "text": "How does SELECT FOR UPDATE prevent duplicate confirmation? Confirmation locks the pending submission selected by token, owner, and pending status. A second transaction targeting the same row waits; after the first commits, the second must observe the updated state under the actual isolation rules. The lock protects a database transition, not the browser button. The route holds the row lock through claim creation, filing event, outbox writes, and submission completion. submit_claim_internal flushes a supplied session instead of committing it early. An internal commit would release the lock before the pending row changes, enabling duplicate creation. A request timeout or double click is expected behavior to test, not evidence that the client will never retry. Pessimistic locking simplifies this contested transition but makes lock duration important. Keep remote model work out of the locked transaction. Start two confirmations simultaneously in separate database sessions. Assert one logical claim and one consumed submission, then inspect the losing request's result."}, {"id": 162, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "What is the difference between flush, commit, and rollback?", "text": "What is the difference between flush, commit, and rollback? Flush sends pending ORM changes to the transaction, allowing generated identities and database constraints to be observed. Commit makes the transaction's successful changes durable. Rollback discards its transactional changes. Flushed data is not therefore committed, and another session's visibility depends on isolation. Submission flushes the Claim to obtain its UUID, adds the embedding job, and leaves commitment to the confirmation route. The caller can then commit claim, job, lifecycle event, notification, and token state together. Catching a database exception and continuing with the same failed transaction can produce further errors. A swallowed exception is not a successful recovery; rollback or a correctly scoped savepoint is needed. Caller-owned transactions permit atomic composition but require explicit ownership. Helpers that commit themselves are easier in isolation and unsafe inside larger workflows. Inject failure after the claim flush but before the final commit. Inspect all related tables from another session after rollback."}, {"id": 163, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "What does PostgreSQL Read Committed mean for an application workflow?", "text": "What does PostgreSQL Read Committed mean for an application workflow? Ordinary statements see committed state as of each statement's snapshot, so two reads in one transaction can observe intervening commits. A transaction boundary alone does not make a sequence of business decisions appear serial. Locking or conditional updates must protect the relevant invariant. The backend constructs an async engine without an explicit isolation override here. Read the deployment's actual setting before assuming a level. Confirmation uses a row lock rather than relying on an unprotected read-then-write sequence. A pattern that reads pending, calls external work, then updates completed can race under ordinary concurrency. Shared data snapshots and write authority are different properties. Stronger isolation can reduce anomalies while increasing retries and contention. It does not eliminate the need for idempotent external effects. Reproduce a changing read in two sessions, then compare a locked confirmation and a conditional UPDATE. Record actual transaction settings."}, {"id": 164, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "When would optimistic concurrency control be useful?", "text": "When would optimistic concurrency control be useful? Optimistic control updates a row only when its version or prior state matches what the caller observed. A zero-row update signals a conflict. It suits edits where collisions are uncommon and a caller can reload rather than hold a lock during long user interaction. The outbox's mark_processing uses a guarded status update and returning clause to determine who actually claimed work. A similar version-column pattern is a proposed option for user-visible claim edits or session state. Checking a version in Python and then issuing an unconditional update is not optimistic concurrency control. The comparison and mutation must be one database operation. Short conditional writes reduce waiting but require conflict handling. Row locks can be simpler for a small critical transition with many related writes. Write a versioned update for a hypothetical claim note. Make two editors submit the same expected version and verify only one succeeds."}, {"id": 165, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "Why does SKIP LOCKED not describe the entire outbox claim guarantee here?", "text": "Why does SKIP LOCKED not describe the entire outbox claim guarantee here? claim_pending selects rows with SKIP LOCKED inside a session that closes before mark_processing. That selection lock does not persist through remote processing. The later guarded update and returned subset decide which worker successfully changes a still-claimable job to processing. Reading both methods reveals the true coordination boundary. A second worker may observe the same candidate after the selection transaction ends, but only a matching conditional status update should claim it. Describing selection alone as exclusive ownership hides a race and encourages unsafe future changes. Completion and reclamation also need ownership-aware conditions to prevent obsolete workers from overwriting newer outcomes. Split selection and guarded claiming compose with the generic protocol but require more careful reasoning. One atomic claim statement can simplify the implementation at the cost of adapter-specific SQL. Interleave two workers between selection and marking. Verify the loser receives no processing payload for the contested job."}, {"id": 166, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "Why is one AsyncSession per concurrent task important?", "text": "Why is one AsyncSession per concurrent task important? AsyncSession represents mutable transactional and ORM state. Independent concurrent operations should use independent sessions rather than sharing one session inside asyncio.gather. An async driver does not make one transaction object safe for arbitrary concurrent use. get_db supplies request-scoped sessions, and workers create their own sessions. Any proposal to parallelize dense and lexical queries must account for whether they share a session and connection. Sharing can cause state errors, unintended transaction coupling, or unpredictable query sequencing. Creating too many sessions instead can exhaust the pool, so concurrency also needs a bound. Separate sessions allow independent I/O while consuming additional connections and potentially observing different snapshots. Sequential queries on one session are easier when one consistent transaction is required. Compare concurrent reads with separate sessions and sequential reads with one session. Measure pool checkout waits and document the required consistency model."}, {"id": 167, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "Can persistent ADK session state still suffer lost updates?", "text": "Can persistent ADK session state still suffer lost updates? Yes. Persistence prevents process-local loss but does not automatically merge concurrent state safely. The session event writer builds a full persisted_state from the in-memory session plus a delta and writes that blob. Two stale in-memory sessions can overwrite independent changes unless execution is serialized or updates are guarded. _store_event inserts an event and updates session state in a transaction, but the observed code does not add a per-session version comparison around the full blob update. The event log and current state therefore deserve separate concurrency tests. An append-only event record can survive while the latest state loses another turn's field. Sequence numbers also do not prove the model saw a serial conversational history. Per-session turn serialization improves consistency and reduces same-conversation throughput. Delta updates or optimistic versions offer alternatives with their own conflict policy. Load the same session twice, apply different state keys concurrently, and inspect both the event log and final state."}, {"id": 168, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "Why is the Redis idempotency claim a best-effort mechanism rather than exactly-once execution?", "text": "Why is the Redis idempotency claim a best-effort mechanism rather than exactly-once execution? The claim has a finite TTL, waiters stop waiting after a bounded time, and Redis outages permit local execution. A slow winner can still run while another replica gives up and recomputes. The claim value is not a durable operation result or a fencing token for a business write. RedisIdempotencyBackend uses a short NX claim and a separate response cache. Its release deletes the claim key without an owner token comparison. These choices favor availability and reduction of duplicate model work, not an unconditional exactly-once contract. A resumed old worker could affect a later claim, and duplicate generation may incur cost. Claim submission must rely on its database confirmation boundary rather than this chat cache. Best-effort deduplication improves responsiveness. Stronger operation state requires ownership tokens, durable result identity, and explicit timeout/retry semantics. Make a model turn outlast the claim TTL and waiter deadline. Count model executions separately from durable business effects."}, {"id": 169, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "How should deadlocks, lock timeouts, and serialization failures be handled?", "text": "How should deadlocks, lock timeouts, and serialization failures be handled? Acquire shared resources in a consistent order, keep transactions short, bound waiting, and retry only the whole appropriate transactional unit when the database reports a retryable conflict. Reusing a partially failed session or blindly retrying external effects is unsafe. The locked confirmation path is a concrete unit to analyze. Future workflows touching multiple claims or users need an explicit lock order; the current single-row example is not proof that all later operations are deadlock-free. Retrying a transaction can repeat already-sent emails or model calls if they occur inside the unit. Outbox writes let the database retry without repeating external delivery synchronously. Retries improve recovery while increasing tail latency. An overloaded system needs admission control, not only more retry attempts. Create a two-row opposite-order locking exercise in a disposable database. Record the failure class and retry the complete unit with a bounded policy."}, {"id": 170, "chapter": "Transactions, locks, and concurrency", "slug": "database-concurrency", "title": "How do you test concurrency without relying on random sleeps?", "text": "How do you test concurrency without relying on random sleeps? Use controlled barriers or events to pause workers at known boundaries, then force a specific interleaving. Assert durable state and ownership outcomes, not only response codes. Use a real PostgreSQL instance for lock and isolation claims. The backend contains contract and integration tests for claim atomicity, jobs, and shared state. They are useful starting points, while mocks can only demonstrate the ordering they simulate. A test that runs two coroutines on one session may test an invalid integration rather than a real race. An in-memory SQLite database cannot establish PostgreSQL row-lock semantics. Deterministic integration tests take setup but produce stronger evidence and fewer flaky timing assumptions. Broad stress tests complement them and do not replace them. Specify barriers for two confirmations, two job claims, and two session-state updates. State the invariant each test could falsify."}, {"id": 171, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "What does least privilege mean for application, migration, and reporting database roles?", "text": "What does least privilege mean for application, migration, and reporting database roles? Use separate credentials for runtime queries, schema migration, and analysis. The application role should have only the tables and operations its workload needs; it should not need unrestricted DDL or superuser access. Read-only reporting still needs row and data-disclosure controls. The backend centralizes the engine URL, making credential selection a deployment concern. The inspected code does not establish a complete least-privilege role provisioning policy, so this chapter treats role separation as hardening work. A single powerful credential turns a query injection, tool bug, or compromised worker into a broader database incident. A model must never receive that credential or arbitrary SQL authority. Separate roles require grants and migration coordination but reduce blast radius. One admin credential is convenient locally and inappropriate as evidence of production isolation. Design a grant matrix for API, embedding worker, notification worker, migrator, and analyst. Include which sensitive tables each must never read."}, {"id": 172, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "How could PostgreSQL Row-Level Security complement owner-scoped queries?", "text": "How could PostgreSQL Row-Level Security complement owner-scoped queries? RLS applies database policies to rows visible or writable through a role. It can add a second barrier if an application query forgets owner filtering. The inspected backend does not define RLS policies, so it is a proposed defense rather than an existing guarantee. ClaimsRetriever always supplies owner_id, and API reads also scope ownership. A proposed RLS design must set trusted per-transaction identity and use a role subject to the policy, with correct USING and WITH CHECK rules. Table owners or privileged roles can bypass policies under documented conditions. Connection reuse can leak session-level identity if it is not reset; transaction-local settings and pool tests matter. Database enforcement strengthens defense in depth but complicates jobs, migrations, tests, and administrative workflows. It does not replace server authentication. In a disposable schema, create a transaction-local owner setting and policy. Test two users through reused pooled connections and verify writes as well as reads."}, {"id": 173, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "Why are parameterized values insufficient for arbitrary query generation?", "text": "Why are parameterized values insufficient for arbitrary query generation? Binding values prevents them from being interpreted as SQL syntax in the intended parameter position. It does not make arbitrary table names, operators, SQL fragments, or model-generated statements safe. Query structure needs a trusted construction path and an allowlisted operation surface. Ragnor validates identifiers while the host uses SQLAlchemy expressions for business queries. The current tools expose domain operations rather than a free-form SQL executor, a useful authority boundary to preserve. An LLM-generated SELECT can still disclose unauthorized rows, invoke expensive functions, or exploit permissions even if it contains no classic string-concatenation injection. Narrow domain tools limit exploratory flexibility and support predictable authorization. A natural-language analytics feature needs an independently constrained read model and query policy. Design an exact aggregate tool for a user's claims using typed inputs. Explain why it is safer than giving the model a raw database connection."}, {"id": 174, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "How do database credentials and TLS fit into the trust model?", "text": "How do database credentials and TLS fit into the trust model? Credentials authenticate a process to the database; TLS protects transport and can verify server identity. Neither automatically limits which rows the process may read. Rotate credentials, use appropriate secret storage, and validate certificate settings in the actual deployment. database.py reads a configured URL and constructs the engine. That call site alone does not prove encrypted connections, certificate verification, rotation, or a private network boundary. Printing URLs or raw driver errors can expose passwords or infrastructure details. Copying secrets into model context, traces, sample files, or shell arguments creates additional disclosure paths. Managed identities or short-lived credentials reduce secret lifetime where supported, while adding integration requirements. Static credentials are simpler and need deliberate rotation and incident response. Create a secret-free deployment evidence record showing connection identity, TLS verification mode, role grants, and rotation ownership. Do not include the secret value."}, {"id": 175, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "Which timeouts protect database availability?", "text": "Which timeouts protect database availability? Bound pool checkout, connection establishment, statement execution, lock waits, and idle transactions according to the workload. These limits solve different problems. An HTTP timeout alone may leave server-side queries or open transactions consuming resources. The current engine setup is minimal, and provider timeouts live elsewhere. Production hardening should validate driver, pool, database, and application deadline behavior together rather than assuming one setting covers the stack. An expensive permitted query can be a denial-of-service vector. Very short timeouts can also cause retry amplification or repeatedly abort useful work. Tighter limits improve isolation and can reduce completion rates. Use separate policies for interactive search, migrations, and backfills instead of one global number. Propose settings for pool wait, lock wait, and statement time with a measured request budget. Simulate a blocked transaction and observe rollback and connection reuse."}, {"id": 176, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "What is the difference between secure deletion, logical revocation, and retention?", "text": "What is the difference between secure deletion, logical revocation, and retention? Revocation stops authorized disclosure. Logical deletion changes application visibility. Physical deletion removes stored copies, subject to backups and retention. Insurance records, embeddings, pending payloads, conversations, traces, and notifications can each retain different copies of the same private facts. The host has owner-scoped tables, audit records, derived embeddings, and observability exports. A cascade on Claim.owner_id is not a complete cross-system deletion policy. Deleting a relational row may leave vectors, trace payloads, exports, or historical messages accessible. Conversely, deleting everything indiscriminately can violate a required evidence-retention policy; the applicable policy must be established outside this engineering guide. Purpose-specific retention minimizes unnecessary copies while preserving required records. One indefinite retention setting is easy and accumulates disclosure risk. Draw a data inventory for a single claim description and specify revocation, retention, deletion, and verification owners for every copy."}, {"id": 177, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "Is an append-only audit table automatically tamper-proof and complete?", "text": "Is an append-only audit table automatically tamper-proof and complete? No. Append-only is an application behavior unless database privileges and external retention enforce it. Completeness depends on what is recorded and what happens when recording fails. Trace data and business audit records also have different reliability requirements. audit/service.py intentionally catches failures and allows business actions to continue, describing a best-effort trail. The code therefore does not justify a universal claim that every successful action has an audit row. A missing trail can matter during an investigation. Also, swallowing a failed SQL statement does not by itself restore a PostgreSQL transaction; savepoint and rollback behavior require testing. Best-effort audit favors availability. Mandatory audit or a transactional audit outbox provides stronger completeness at the cost of possible business-operation failure or more infrastructure. Inject an audit insertion failure and inspect the surrounding transaction. Define which actions must fail closed if audit cannot be guaranteed."}, {"id": 178, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "How should backup and point-in-time recovery be tested for an AI application?", "text": "How should backup and point-in-time recovery be tested for an AI application? Restore the relational database and verify business records, ownership, migrations, outbox state, and policy versions. Then decide whether vector projections should be restored or rebuilt. The recovery objective concerns a coherent application state, not only a successful backup command. Claims have derived embeddings and jobs that may have completed after the restored database point. A restored snapshot can therefore need reconciliation before search and notifications agree with authoritative state. Replaying old notification or submission jobs can duplicate effects. A backup also contains private data and requires its own encryption, access, and retention policy. Rebuilding vectors reduces backup coupling and takes time and provider cost. Snapshotting every projection can accelerate recovery while making consistency coordination harder. Write a restore drill with a known claim, pending job, policy revision, and revoked user. Verify exact reads, search visibility, and safe job resumption."}, {"id": 179, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "Why separate operational metrics from database content exports?", "text": "Why separate operational metrics from database content exports? Counts, durations, and sanitized error categories support capacity and health without automatically exporting claim text, policyholder identifiers, or confirmation tokens. Content exports need an explicit purpose and access policy rather than being a side effect of debugging. Ragnor has a redacted event abstraction, but host tools and LangSmith wrappers can capture arguments and results through different paths. Some preparation logs include confirmation tokens, so universal redaction cannot be inferred from the library. A credential-safe SQL logger can still expose PII through parameter values. Traces may be readable by a broader audience than the application database. Minimal telemetry reduces diagnosis detail; controlled replay fixtures and opt-in redacted samples can provide useful debugging with less disclosure. Trace one claim description and token through logs, tool spans, model callbacks, and telemetry endpoints. Record exactly what leaves the process."}, {"id": 180, "chapter": "Securing database operations", "slug": "database-security", "title": "How should database security be verified before adding agent autonomy?", "text": "How should database security be verified before adding agent autonomy? Prove owner filtering, least-privilege credentials, constrained tools, input validation, timeout behavior, audit policy, and recovery using the actual storage setup. A more autonomous planner increases the number and variety of operations, so it should inherit a secure capability surface rather than broadening it. The existing backend already provides concrete domain tools and protected routes. LangChain, LangGraph, or A2A adoption should reuse those service boundaries instead of exposing unrestricted database operations. A framework demo may call internal functions directly and skip route dependencies. The same tool needs an identity context or service principal appropriate to its execution path. A capability-oriented service layer takes design work and supports safe reuse across orchestration frameworks. Direct ORM access inside every agent is fast to prototype and hard to govern. Build a deny-first test matrix for interactive users, background workers, and remote agents. Include actions each principal may read, prepare, confirm, or never perform."}, {"id": 181, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "How are JWTs validated and converted into database ownership?", "text": "How are JWTs validated and converted into database ownership? The authentication dependency resolves a bearer token or cookie, verifies its signature and expiration using an explicit algorithm, parses the subject as a UUID, and looks up the user. Downstream code uses that trusted identity rather than accepting an arbitrary owner from model-generated arguments. _decode_token validates a fixed HS256 algorithm list; get_current_user sets current_user_id for tool access. A JWT payload is signed data, not encrypted private storage, so keeping claims minimal reduces disclosure. A valid token can remain usable until expiry unless revocation or credential/session-version checks are added. The observed access lifetime is one week, a choice that should be evaluated against the product's threat model. Stateless token checks scale well, while immediate revocation needs additional state or shorter-lived credentials. Database user lookup already provides one current-state validation point. Test expired, malformed, deleted-user, wrong-signature, and valid tokens. Verify that a known claim ID still requires owner authorization."}, {"id": 182, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "Why are HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite, CORS, and CSRF different controls?", "text": "Why are HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite, CORS, and CSRF different controls? HttpOnly limits script access to cookies, Secure limits cookie transport to HTTPS, and SameSite constrains some cross-site sending. CORS controls browser access across origins. CSRF protection addresses unwanted state-changing requests made with ambient credentials. None is interchangeable with user authorization. set_session_cookie derives Secure from the environment and uses HttpOnly with SameSite=Lax; configuration rejects wildcard credentialed CORS. The module's older comment about secure=False is less precise than the actual function. These settings do not establish a complete CSRF review of all mutations and deployment origins. XSS can still issue authorized actions even when it cannot read an HttpOnly cookie. Cookie authentication simplifies browser sessions but requires deliberate origin and state-change policy. Bearer clients have different storage and transport concerns. Build a matrix for sign-in, confirmation, cancellation, and logout across trusted and untrusted origins. State which server-side checks enforce each result."}, {"id": 183, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "How does ContextVar prevent cross-request identity leakage?", "text": "How does ContextVar prevent cross-request identity leakage? ContextVar associates a value with an asynchronous execution context rather than one mutable process-global variable. Concurrent requests can therefore carry different identities through await boundaries. It is a context propagation mechanism, not an authorization decision or durable identity store. Tools read current_user_id after authentication or trusted agent binding. Background tasks, new threads, and remote workers need explicit lifecycle rules because they do not automatically represent a current HTTP request forever. Capturing context for a task that outlives a request can retain stale identity. Failing to reset temporary bindings can also confuse tests or reused execution contexts. Server code must define where identity begins and ends. Implicit context reduces parameter threading and can hide dependencies. Explicit identity parameters improve local clarity and require more signatures. Run two concurrent tool calls under separate context bindings. Test missing identity and an outliving background task; verify both fail or receive an explicit service identity as intended."}, {"id": 184, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "Why must claim ownership be checked in tools as well as routes?", "text": "Why must claim ownership be checked in tools as well as routes? Agent tools may execute through a runner, test harness, or future remote protocol without passing through a particular HTTP route. A protected route cannot be the only place a domain operation learns whose data it may access. ClaimsRetriever requires a user ID and inserts owner_id into retrieval filters. Claim-status and preparation tools obtain the current trusted context. This preserves a domain-level boundary when orchestration changes. A model-supplied user_id is untrusted even if it is a valid UUID. A future LangChain adapter must not expose that internal parameter as a tool argument the model can freely choose. Repeated enforcement at domain boundaries adds code and protects alternate callers. Duplicated policy implementations can drift, so centralize shared service authorization where possible. Invoke the claim-search tool directly without an HTTP request and with the wrong user context. Verify that knowing another user's claim ID does not reveal it."}, {"id": 185, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "What is the distinction between RBAC and resource ownership here?", "text": "What is the distinction between RBAC and resource ownership here? Role-Based Access Control determines whether a principal may perform a category of action; ownership determines which records that principal may affect. A support operator role does not automatically justify unrestricted access to every policyholder's claims. require_role reads current roles from the database, while claim routes and retrieval use owner constraints. Administrative claim transitions currently retain a separate empty allowlist, so role support elsewhere does not mean those transitions are enabled. Replacing the allowlist with a broad role check without resource policy can overgrant authority. Tests that widen the allowlist for fixtures do not prove production administration is configured. Combining role and attribute checks is more expressive and more complex. A deny-by-default seam is safer than silently pretending a missing administrative policy exists. Define reader, policyholder, handler, and administrator actions with both role and record-scope rules. Map each rule to its actual enforcement point."}, {"id": 186, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "Why does password hashing require more than naming Argon2?", "text": "Why does password hashing require more than naming Argon2? A password hash uses intentionally costly computation and salt to resist offline guessing. Operational parameters, input limits, verification errors, rehash policy, and login rate limiting matter too. A hash algorithm name alone does not establish timing-safe user enumeration resistance. The backend uses argon2-cffi and a dummy hash path. The dummy constant resembles a placeholder; because verification catches general exceptions, its actual work factor must be tested rather than assumed equivalent to a valid stored hash. Malformed dummy verification can return faster than a real check. Unbounded password input or excessive concurrent hashing can also consume resources before the agent is involved. Higher hashing cost protects stolen hashes and increases authentication resource use. Choose measured parameters and a valid equivalent dummy path. Benchmark known-user wrong-password and unknown-user checks with reviewed test data. Verify hash validity and compare distributions without asserting perfect constant time."}, {"id": 187, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "How should confirmation tokens be treated as capabilities?", "text": "How should confirmation tokens be treated as capabilities? A confirmation token identifies a specific prepared action and should be bound to the user, payload, pending state, and expiry. Possession alone should not bypass authentication. Material edits should produce a new reviewed operation and invalidate or supersede the old one server-side. Confirmation selects by token and owner and locks the pending row. Preparation creates expiring records. The prompt says an earlier token becomes invalid after correction, but creating a new pending row does not itself prove the old row is cancelled. A model's statement cannot revoke a still-pending token. Some preparation logs also include tokens, increasing the audience that may see them even though owner binding remains required. Server-enforced supersession adds lifecycle bookkeeping and prevents stale confirmation. Keeping multiple live preparations is simpler but needs an honest user-visible model. Prepare an amount, correct it, then try both tokens. Document current behavior and specify the desired supersession invariant."}, {"id": 188, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "Why must rate limiting, request-size limits, and tool budgets coexist?", "text": "Why must rate limiting, request-size limits, and tool budgets coexist? Request rate bounds arrival frequency, size limits bound per-request input, and tool or token budgets bound work initiated by one accepted request. An agent can turn one small request into many expensive provider calls, so requests per minute alone do not control cost. The backend has SlowAPI integration, middleware, and model settings; shared counters are optional. The agent loop and each domain operation still need a coherent per-turn execution and cost policy. Fail-open Redis behavior preserves availability and can weaken fleet-wide limits during an outage. Model-generated loops may also stay within HTTP request limits while exhausting provider budgets. Layered budgets increase configuration and improve predictable resource use. Tight limits can interrupt legitimate multi-intent conversations and need explicit completion states. Estimate worst-case cost from maximum turns, tool calls, retries, candidates, and output tokens. Test overload without using real private claims."}, {"id": 189, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "How can error responses leak information even when access is denied?", "text": "How can error responses leak information even when access is denied? Different messages, status codes, timings, trace payloads, and raw exception strings can reveal record existence or internal details. A denial response should reveal only what the caller is entitled to learn, while internal diagnostics remain appropriately protected. Owner-scoped claim lookup can avoid distinguishing another user's claim from an unknown one. Other paths log raw failures or include result errors, so disclosure policy must be reviewed across API, tools, and observability separately. A model may repeat an internal error returned by a tool. Sanitizing only the final HTTP exception leaves tool results and streaming error events as additional channels. Generic external messages reduce detail for users and support; structured internal error categories preserve diagnosis without exposing private values. Compare responses for unknown and foreign-owned claim IDs, invalid tokens, provider failures, and database errors. Inspect both the streamed events and final response."}, {"id": 190, "chapter": "API identity and application security", "slug": "api-security", "title": "What does an effective threat-model review produce?", "text": "What does an effective threat-model review produce? It produces a concrete asset and actor map, trust boundaries, abuse cases, enforcement points, and tests. For this product, include private claim text, confirmation tokens, policy versions, prompt data, provider keys, session history, and traces. The code has useful controls, but a complete deployment needs evidence that every path uses them. The handbook's security diagram distinguishes implemented owner and confirmation boundaries from proposed RLS or remote-agent controls. A checklist detached from actual call sites can certify controls that are merely configured or documented. Threat modeling should include alternate paths such as evaluation, administration, scripts, and workers. A focused model tied to major workflows is maintainable. An exhaustive speculative catalog can consume time without improving concrete denial behavior. Pick cross-user disclosure, duplicate submission, and malicious-source instructions. For each, identify the earliest reliable enforcement point and a test proving denial."}, {"id": 191, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "What makes this an agent rather than a fixed retrieval pipeline?", "text": "What makes this an agent rather than a fixed retrieval pipeline? A model can select tools, inspect results, and decide whether to continue before answering. A fixed pipeline executes a predetermined path for a known request shape. The current agent has bounded domain capabilities but flexible intent routing and multi-tool turns. LlmAgent receives five registered handlers and SYSTEM_INSTRUCTION. Runner manages events and sessions. Domain tools continue to enforce business behavior rather than giving the model direct database authority. Flexible selection introduces wrong-tool, repeated-tool, and premature-answer failures beyond retrieval quality. Tool availability should reflect server authorization, not only instructions saying which tools are appropriate. Agents handle conversational ambiguity and multiple intents while requiring trajectory evaluation and execution limits. A deterministic route remains preferable for exact confirmation and lifecycle mutation. Compare a fixed policy-search endpoint with an ADK turn asking both a coverage and claim-status question. Identify the additional decisions the agent makes."}, {"id": 192, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "How does the tool registry become a model-facing contract?", "text": "How does the tool registry become a model-facing contract? The registry returns callable handlers. Their signatures and docstrings inform tool declarations; registry metadata and human descriptions are not automatically security enforcement or the exact text the model sees. ToolRegistry explicitly documents that ToolSpec.description is documentation-only in build_tool_list. This makes the handler docstring and prompt the real behavioral interface to inspect when routing changes. Adding requires_auth metadata does not prove authentication runs. Wrappers that alter signatures can also accidentally expose internal configuration or remove required field information. Function-derived tools are convenient and reduce duplicated schemas. They require disciplined annotations, docstrings, wrapper preservation, and server validation. Inspect the generated declaration of prepare_claim_submission and compare it with ToolSpec. Verify that owner identity is absent from model-controlled arguments."}, {"id": 193, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "Why preserve function signatures when instrumenting tools?", "text": "Why preserve function signatures when instrumenting tools? Agent runtimes introspect callable signatures and annotations to construct schemas. A tracing wrapper can add internal parameters or change annotation resolution, causing the model to see a contract the underlying function does not support. tool_instrumentation pins __signature__ to the original handler and resolves annotations using the handler's context. An installation marker prevents repeated startup from stacking wrappers and duplicating telemetry. A wrapper that preserves results but changes the model declaration can still break behavior. Likewise, exception-only success recording can classify a returned error payload as a successful call. Transparent instrumentation needs careful compatibility tests but keeps business tools vendor-neutral. Hand-instrumenting every function is simpler locally and prone to inconsistent coverage. Compare inspect.signature and generated schema before and after instrumentation. Test both a raised exception and a returned success=False payload."}, {"id": 194, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "What does LiteLLM abstract, and what remains provider-specific?", "text": "What does LiteLLM abstract, and what remains provider-specific? LiteLLM offers a common access surface for different model providers. Provider capabilities still vary in tool calling, structured output, token accounting, streaming, pricing, context windows, and error behavior. A common adapter does not make all models behaviorally interchangeable. create_agent passes the configured model and optional sampling settings through ADK's LiteLlm adapter. Evaluation and telemetry must record which model and configuration actually ran. A provider switch can change tool selection or output schemas without a Python type error. The current temperature override uses truthiness, so an explicit zero does not follow the same branch as a positive configured value. Routing flexibility reduces provider coupling while expanding the compatibility matrix. A single well-tested provider can be a sound early production choice. Compare two providers on the same safe tool trajectories and output contracts. Test temperature zero separately from an omitted value."}, {"id": 195, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "How should agent memory be bounded and rehydrated?", "text": "How should agent memory be bounded and rehydrated? Retain enough recent user, assistant, and tool context to resolve references, while bounding replay and clearly separating temporary invocation state from persistent session state. Long history is not automatically useful context and may contain obsolete instructions or private data. DatabaseSessionService caps replayed events and drops temp-prefixed state. The before-model callback reads recent conversation turns, compacts older material, and merges compatible message roles for the model. A lossy summary can forget corrected facts, retain an obsolete confirmation token, or misrepresent a previous tool failure. Message order and tool-result pairing need explicit tests. Compaction reduces tokens and latency while losing detail. Retrieving structured prior facts can be more reliable for critical values than trusting an unconstrained summary. Create a conversation that corrects an amount, cancels a preparation, and returns later. Verify memory does not revive the old action."}, {"id": 196, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "Does a database-backed session service guarantee cross-replica continuity?", "text": "Does a database-backed session service guarantee cross-replica continuity? It provides shared stored events and state when configured and healthy. Continuity also requires stable user/session resolution, compatible serialization, correct ordering, migration ownership, and a concurrency policy for overlapping turns. Durable storage alone does not serialize reasoning. SESSION_STORAGE defaults to memory in settings. The application can warm a database-backed service, but startup has a fallback path. Therefore an available class is not proof that the active deployment uses shared state. Silent fallback can make one replica forget or diverge from another while health appears generally available. Runtime-created session tables also sit outside the ordinary Alembic ownership model. Fallback preserves some availability and weakens continuity. Production may need fail-closed readiness when shared conversation state is a required guarantee. Start two replicas with explicit database sessions, then simulate storage initialization failure. Inspect the effective backend and continuity behavior."}, {"id": 197, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "Why distinguish a returned tool error from a raised exception?", "text": "Why distinguish a returned tool error from a raised exception? A tool can complete its Python call while returning found=False, success=False, or an error field. Those are domain outcomes, not necessarily exceptions. The agent, stream mapper, telemetry, and evaluation need a shared interpretation of those payloads. The prompt distinguishes search failure from successful empty results and prohibits inventing claim status. run_agent_stream has helper logic for tool-result failures, while generic timing instrumentation primarily observes exception behavior. Reporting every returned dictionary as success inflates reliability metrics. Treating every no-result as an error also misleads the user and can trigger unnecessary retries. Structured result types require more explicit schemas and simplify downstream interpretation. Free-form dictionaries are flexible and can accumulate incompatible conventions. Define expected stream and metric outcomes for found=False, empty search, validation_errors, backend error, and a raised exception."}, {"id": 198, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "Why must a human handoff be more than a sentence in a prompt?", "text": "Why must a human handoff be more than a sentence in a prompt? A handoff needs an actual destination, case or ticket identity, permitted context transfer, status, and recovery if delivery fails. Saying that a specialist is being connected does not establish that any operational transfer occurred. SYSTEM_INSTRUCTION contains escalation conditions and a fixed handoff sentence. The inspected default registry has no human-routing or ticket-creation tool, so automated specialist connection is not demonstrated by this path. Users may believe a dispute or urgent request is being handled when the system only generated text. Evaluation should distinguish an escalation recommendation from a completed transfer. A real handoff integration adds external dependencies and privacy choices. An honest support-contact response is simpler and preferable to an unsupported promise. Simulate a dispute and inspect all tool events. Specify a proposed escalation tool with success, pending, failed, and authorized-payload contracts."}, {"id": 199, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "How should agent execution be bounded?", "text": "How should agent execution be bounded? Bound model calls, tool calls, wall time, tokens, retries, and repeated identical actions. Use deterministic server checks for irreversible operations. A prompt asking the model not to repeat a tool is helpful guidance but not a hard execution ceiling. ADK run configuration and application settings provide integration points, while routes and tools own business enforcement. Each configured limit needs a test of the actual active runner path. A looping agent can accumulate cost or keep a stream open while request-rate limits remain satisfied. A process-level cancellation may not stop already-started provider or worker operations. Tight limits improve predictability and can interrupt complex legitimate requests. Explicit partial-completion and escalation states preserve useful progress. Use a fake model repeatedly requesting the same tool. Verify the host stops within a declared time and count budget and returns a truthful incomplete status."}, {"id": 200, "chapter": "ADK agents, tools, and memory", "slug": "adk-agents", "title": "How can deterministic tools and flexible reasoning coexist safely?", "text": "How can deterministic tools and flexible reasoning coexist safely? Let the model interpret requests and propose a narrow operation; let deterministic validation, authorization, and transaction code decide whether and how that operation takes effect. The model's narrative should describe the authoritative result rather than manufacture it. Preparation tools validate fields, confirmation uses authenticated server state, and claim lifecycle code enforces legal transitions. These boundaries remain valuable if ADK is replaced or another agent protocol is added. A future tool that directly approves claims or writes arbitrary status values would bypass the current authority model. Pattern consistency matters more than the orchestrator's brand. Narrow tools reduce open-ended autonomy but make actions auditable and testable. High-autonomy systems need equally strong capability and approval boundaries. Design a proposed handler-assistance tool that recommends a decision without committing one. State who may approve it and which exact record becomes authoritative."}, {"id": 201, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "What would LangChain replace or add in this application?", "text": "What would LangChain replace or add in this application? LangChain can supply model, message, tool, middleware, structured-output, and agent-building abstractions. Adopting its agent factory would replace part of the current ADK orchestration, not PostgreSQL, Ragnor, domain validation, or the confirmation transaction. Current official examples use create_agent with an injected model and tools. The backend instead constructs LlmAgent. The worked example in this chapter is a proposed adapter and is not part of the insurance dependency manifest. Installing a framework without deciding who owns sessions, tool errors, and streaming can create two overlapping runtimes. A dependency change alone does not migrate behavioral contracts. LangChain offers a broad integration surface; staying with ADK avoids migration cost. Choose based on a demonstrated need and an equivalent behavioral benchmark. Map each current ADK constructor argument to the proposed LangChain composition. Identify the contracts that should remain unchanged."}, {"id": 202, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "What are messages, chat models, and invocation methods?", "text": "What are messages, chat models, and invocation methods? Messages represent roles, text, tool calls, and tool results. A chat model consumes a message sequence and produces model output. Invocation, streaming, and batch methods are execution interfaces, not different authorization policies or independent guarantees of model quality. A proposed LangChain adapter must translate the host's conversation and tool-event shapes into the selected model interface. Existing ADK events cannot simply be copied into another runtime and assumed equivalent. Dropping a tool result, role boundary, or corrected user value can change behavior. A model supporting text may not support the tool or structured-output features the application requires. Standard interfaces ease experimentation while provider capabilities still require testing. A single-provider direct adapter can be smaller for a narrow workload. Translate a user question, tool call, tool result, and assistant answer into an explicit message sequence and verify the pairing identifiers."}, {"id": 203, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "How can an existing Ragnor domain function become a LangChain tool?", "text": "How can an existing Ragnor domain function become a LangChain tool? Wrap the existing async domain function with a narrow typed tool whose arguments contain only user-searchable input. Resolve trusted identity in server context and retain the domain function's validation and owner filters. The wrapper should not expose internal session or owner parameters to the model. LangChain's tool decorator builds a callable schema from annotations and descriptions. This is similar in purpose to ADK introspection but not a reason to move authorization into a docstring. A convenient wrapper accepting user_id from the model defeats the current scoping boundary. Context setup and reset must be defined for HTTP, evaluation, and background execution. Thin adapters reuse tested business logic and add a small compatibility surface. Rewriting tools inside each framework risks policy drift. Inspect the proposed read-only policy wrapper in the worked examples. Add a claims wrapper that obtains identity from a trusted service context."}, {"id": 204, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "How do runnable composition and an agent loop differ?", "text": "How do runnable composition and an agent loop differ? A composed chain follows a known sequence such as retrieve, assemble, and answer. An agent loop lets a model choose tools and continuation. Both can use the same model and retriever interfaces, but they create different testing and cost requirements. The current insurance system uses an agent for mixed intents. A deterministic coverage-search path could still be useful for a restricted endpoint or baseline without replacing every conversational feature. Calling a fixed chain an agent can hide the absence of planning; calling an agent a simple chain can hide repeated calls and nondeterministic routing. Name the actual control flow. Fixed composition is easier to bound and diagnose; flexible routing handles ambiguity. Start from the least flexible flow that meets the requirement. Build a deterministic retrieval-and-answer baseline and compare it with agent orchestration on single-intent and multi-intent cases."}, {"id": 205, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "What does structured output validate, and what does it not validate?", "text": "What does structured output validate, and what does it not validate? A structured schema constrains the form and types of a model result, such as an answer with citation IDs and a status. It does not prove the citations exist, the amounts match policy text, or the caller may execute a proposed action. LangChain supports response schemas through its agent interface and provider/tool strategies. A proposed insurance adapter should validate schema output again against the actual evidence and allowed operation set. A valid JSON decision saying approved is still not an authorized claim transition. Schema conformance can otherwise create a false impression of factual or business correctness. Typed outputs simplify UI integration and require explicit parse, retry, and semantic-validation branches. Free-form prose is flexible and harder to validate reliably. Define a recommendation schema with status, cited evidence IDs, missing facts, and explanation. Reject unknown citations before presentation."}, {"id": 206, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "What belongs in middleware instead of a business tool?", "text": "What belongs in middleware instead of a business tool? Cross-cutting policies such as call budgets, model selection, standardized error mapping, and context preparation can sit around execution. Business tools should still own their domain authorization, validation, and transaction behavior. LangChain middleware offers hooks around model and tool execution. The current backend has analogous cross-cutting concerns in tracing wrappers, before-model history rehydration, rate limits, and route-level idempotency. A middleware-only authorization check can be bypassed by direct service calls. Overly broad retry middleware can also repeat side effects whose business idempotency is not established. Central hooks reduce repetition while increasing execution-order and wrapper-compatibility complexity. Keep critical invariants enforceable independently of orchestration hooks. Classify budget checking, owner filtering, signature preservation, and pending-token consumption by the layer that must enforce each one."}, {"id": 207, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "How should short-term memory map to insurance conversations?", "text": "How should short-term memory map to insurance conversations? Short-term memory stores a thread's evolving conversational context. It needs a stable thread identity and a persistence policy. Long-term customer facts, authoritative claims, and pending confirmation state should remain in domain storage rather than only in a message buffer. LangChain documents checkpoint-backed short-term state; the current ADK host has its own session and conversation stores. A migration must map thread ownership, history compaction, and message IDs explicitly. Reusing thread IDs across users or forgetting ownership checks can disclose history. Memory summaries can also retain obsolete tokens or policy facts after a correction. Persistent memory improves continuity and increases retention obligations. Stateless turns simplify privacy and require more explicit context from the user. Design a user-and-conversation thread key and a migration test for archived history, corrected facts, and sign-out followed by another user."}, {"id": 208, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "How should LangChain streams be adapted to the existing UI contract?", "text": "How should LangChain streams be adapted to the existing UI contract? Translate framework-specific chunks into the application's typed events rather than exposing raw runtime objects. Preserve text deltas, tool activity, errors, persisted message IDs, and a truthful terminal status. The current endpoint deliberately isolates ADK through an SSE mapping. A LangChain integration should implement the same outward contract so frontend behavior and feedback authorization do not depend on the orchestrator. A tool error or cancelled stream can otherwise be mislabeled complete. Forwarding arbitrary runtime state may leak internal or private fields. A stable application event layer costs adapter code and decouples UI releases from framework changes. Raw framework streams are faster to demo and fragile to evolve. Feed equivalent synthetic ADK and LangChain event sequences into two adapters and compare the resulting public events."}, {"id": 209, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "Are framework guardrails a replacement for deterministic permissions?", "text": "Are framework guardrails a replacement for deterministic permissions? No. Guardrails can classify inputs, validate outputs, or interrupt unsafe model behavior, but backend authorization must still deny prohibited reads and writes. A classifier can be wrong or unavailable, and a prompt can be ignored. LangChain documents guardrail and human-review patterns. For this insurance application, the locked confirmation transaction and owner-scoped tools remain necessary regardless of framework-provided checks. A content-safety pass does not certify that a policyholder may access another claim or approve a payout. Combining unrelated notions of safety into one boolean hides authority errors. Guardrails add useful signals and latency. Deploy them as complementary checks with explicit error behavior, not as the sole permission system. Make a simulated model issue an unauthorized operation after a guardrail reports safe text. Verify the service still denies it."}, {"id": 210, "chapter": "LangChain as an integration alternative", "slug": "langchain", "title": "How would you decide whether an ADK-to-LangChain migration is worthwhile?", "text": "How would you decide whether an ADK-to-LangChain migration is worthwhile? Compare required integrations, operational complexity, framework support, team familiarity, migration risk, and measured behavior. Require parity on tool routing, scope enforcement, confirmation, streaming, memory, evaluation, and tracing before changing the production runtime. The backend already has substantial ADK-specific event and session integration. A migration should preserve those product contracts or explicitly redesign them with tests, not merely reduce the constructor to fewer lines. Benchmarking only a successful coverage response misses multi-turn correction, tool failure, and duplicate writes. New abstractions can also change trace payload disclosure. A better-fit ecosystem can justify the cost; keeping the current stack may be more valuable when problems are domain contracts rather than orchestration limitations. Write an architecture decision record with one concrete unmet need, a parity checklist, a limited experiment, and a rollback condition."}, {"id": 211, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "What problem would LangGraph solve that a basic agent loop does not?", "text": "What problem would LangGraph solve that a basic agent loop does not? LangGraph expresses a workflow as state, nodes, and transitions, with explicit branching and persistence options. It is useful when retries, human review, resumability, and bounded stages should be visible in the orchestration design rather than left entirely to a model loop. A proposed insurance workflow could classify intent, retrieve policy and procedure evidence, request missing facts, prepare a submission, and wait for authorized confirmation. The current backend does not construct this graph. A graph diagram can suggest stronger guarantees than its nodes implement. Domain writes still need their own transaction and permission boundaries. Explicit control improves inspectability while adding state schema and orchestration code. A simple tool-calling loop can remain sufficient for narrow conversational tasks. Draw a proposed coverage-review graph with answerable, missing-facts, unavailable-backend, and escalation branches before writing nodes."}, {"id": 212, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "How do StateGraph state, nodes, and conditional edges work together?", "text": "How do StateGraph state, nodes, and conditional edges work together? State is the workflow's shared data contract; nodes compute updates; edges choose what executes next. A node can be deterministic code or a model call. Conditional routing should use validated state rather than treating arbitrary generated text as an unrestricted next-node name. The worked example uses a TypedDict, StateGraph, START, and END for a small deterministic insurance teaching flow. It demonstrates control structure without claiming to submit real claims or use a production checkpointer. Overloaded state can retain private text, credentials, or stale authorization. Input schema shape alone does not decide what a stream or trace may disclose. Explicit state improves testing and requires careful versioning. A dictionary with unrestricted keys is flexible and harder to migrate or secure. Separate public output fields, internal evidence references, and trusted identity in the proposed graph contract."}, {"id": 213, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "Why do reducers matter when graph nodes run in parallel?", "text": "Why do reducers matter when graph nodes run in parallel? A reducer defines how multiple updates to a state field combine. Replacing a value, appending messages, and merging keyed results are different semantics. Parallel branches need a declared merge rule rather than assuming the last writer is correct. The current session-state writer provides a contrasting lesson: persisting a full blob can overwrite independent changes. A graph reducer can make an intended merge explicit within the graph's execution model. An append reducer can duplicate retries, and a merge can combine evidence from incompatible revisions or scopes. A reducer is not a distributed database transaction. Explicit merge rules support parallel work and require identity, ordering, and replay discipline. Sequential nodes avoid some conflicts at the cost of latency. Design a reducer for evidence keyed by source, revision, and chunk ID. Show how repeated delivery avoids duplicating the same item."}, {"id": 214, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "What is a checkpoint, and how is it different from a claim commit?", "text": "What is a checkpoint, and how is it different from a claim commit? A checkpoint stores workflow state so execution can resume or be inspected. A claim commit makes an authoritative business transition durable. A checkpoint can refer to a completed operation, but saving it does not atomically commit another service's database transaction. LangGraph persistence uses a checkpointer and thread identity. The insurance backend's confirmation transaction has a separate commit boundary. A production design must reconcile crashes between business commit and workflow checkpoint. On resume, a node may retry an already-completed side effect. An in-memory saver demonstrates state handling only within its lifetime and is not a durable production guarantee. Durable checkpoints support recovery and require storage, retention, access policy, and schema evolution. Pure in-memory graphs are easier for local experiments. Draw a crash after claim commit but before checkpoint save. Specify how an operation ID lets the resumed node discover the existing result."}, {"id": 215, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "How does a human-review interrupt differ from authorization?", "text": "How does a human-review interrupt differ from authorization? An interrupt pauses workflow progress and exposes a question or proposed action for review. Authorization determines whether the identity resuming it is permitted to approve that exact action. A resume payload saying approved is untrusted unless the server validates its actor and scope. LangGraph's interrupt and Command resume mechanisms can model review. The current confirmation token and authenticated route provide a concrete business boundary that a graph integration should call rather than replace with a naked boolean. Guessable or cross-user thread access can resume another person's workflow. Material edits after review can invalidate what was approved even if the resume token is technically valid. Human pauses reduce accidental actions while adding persistence and expiry management. For low-risk reads they may be unnecessary. Bind a proposed review to user, payload hash, operation ID, and expiry. Test an unauthorized resume and a changed amount."}, {"id": 216, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "Why must code before an interrupt or retry be safe to execute again?", "text": "Why must code before an interrupt or retry be safe to execute again? Resuming or recovering a workflow may re-enter node code. Work performed before the pause can therefore repeat. Deterministic computation is usually safe; sending a notification or creating a claim needs a durable idempotent operation boundary. The insurance outbox and confirmation state provide reusable mechanisms for making repeat attempts converge on one logical effect. Framework persistence should record operation results, not be assumed to make arbitrary side effects exactly once. Repeating a preparation can leave multiple live tokens; repeating an external notification can confuse the user. A cached Python return value is not enough across process crashes. Smaller nodes with explicit side-effect tasks improve replay reasoning and add orchestration detail. One large node is shorter and harder to recover safely. Put a harmless counter and a mock external action before a teaching interrupt. Observe replay, then move the action behind an idempotent service contract."}, {"id": 217, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "How should graph retries and timeouts be layered?", "text": "How should graph retries and timeouts be layered? Retry only failures likely to recover, with bounded attempts and an overall deadline. A node retry, provider retry, database retry, and client retry can multiply each other. Track the full operation budget and avoid retrying invalid authorization or schema errors. Ragnor already has provider-local retry behavior, while host routes and jobs have their own recovery. A proposed graph must compose those policies rather than independently maximizing every retry count. Nested retries amplify load and can execute write nodes repeatedly. A model timeout also does not prove the provider or remote agent stopped processing. Local resilience helps transient failures, while a simpler centralized budget is easier to reason about. Use stage-specific policy with one overall ceiling. Calculate worst-case attempts for two client retries, three node attempts, and four provider attempts. Replace that accidental multiplier with an explicit budget."}, {"id": 218, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "Can graph private state be streamed safely by default?", "text": "Can graph private state be streamed safely by default? Do not assume a private schema is a redaction policy. A framework may stream internal state or custom events depending on mode. Define an application event projection that allowlists public fields and removes private evidence, identity, and credentials. Current graph documentation distinguishes state schemas from stream visibility. The insurance SSE adapter already illustrates why a stable public event layer should sit between a runtime and the browser. Forwarding raw values or updates can expose claim details or internal analysis. A UI that hides a field visually has still received it over the network. Allowlisted projections take adapter work and preserve privacy across framework updates. Raw graph streaming is convenient for development diagnostics only under controlled access. Insert a synthetic private field into graph state and inspect every chosen stream mode. Verify the public SSE adapter never emits it."}, {"id": 219, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "How should graph state and checkpoints evolve across deployments?", "text": "How should graph state and checkpoints evolve across deployments? Version state schemas and maintain readers or migrations for active checkpoints. A suspended insurance workflow may resume after code changes, so renamed fields, changed node semantics, and new authorization requirements need compatibility handling. The host already has multiple persisted schemas: ORM rows, JSONB submissions, agent events, and conversation messages. Adding graph checkpoints introduces another durable contract, not merely a control-flow diagram. A resumed old workflow can invoke a new write path with stale assumptions. Authorization should be re-evaluated at action time rather than frozen indefinitely in a checkpoint. Compatibility readers increase maintenance and preserve resumability. Expiring old workflows simplifies migration but must communicate how users restart safely. Design a v1-to-v2 checkpoint migration that adds policy revision and payload hash before confirmation. Specify what happens if the old evidence no longer exists."}, {"id": 220, "chapter": "LangGraph stateful workflows", "slug": "langgraph", "title": "When is LangGraph a better choice than ADK or a plain function?", "text": "When is LangGraph a better choice than ADK or a plain function? Choose it when explicit state transitions, branching, durable pause/resume, or inspectable parallel work solve a real product requirement. Keep a plain function for deterministic small operations and consider retaining ADK when flexible tool routing already meets the need. The insurance confirmation flow is already a deterministic API transaction. A graph may organize the surrounding long-running review without replacing that proven write boundary. Introducing a graph solely to draw a more impressive architecture can add persistence and deployment obligations without improving outcomes. More orchestration layers can also fragment traces and ownership. Explicit workflow control is valuable when recovery and review dominate. Simpler code is preferable when the path is short and has no durable pauses. Write a decision record for one proposed graph workflow and one operation you deliberately keep as a normal function. Defend both choices."}, {"id": 221, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "What is A2A, and is it implemented in this backend?", "text": "What is A2A, and is it implemented in this backend? Agent2Agent is a protocol for interaction between independently hosted agents. It describes discovery, exchanged messages, tasks, and outputs without requiring one shared orchestration framework. The inspected backend does not expose or invoke an A2A endpoint in its current application path. ADK has documented A2A integration capabilities, but this project's dependency on ADK does not automatically enable them. A proposed specialist-review agent would require an explicit client/server adapter, authentication, task storage, and data policy. Calling two local functions agents is not proof of protocol interoperability. A diagram arrow labeled A2A must correspond to an implemented and version-tested transport contract. A2A can support organizational and runtime boundaries while adding network, identity, compatibility, and operational complexity. A local domain service is simpler when independent deployment is unnecessary. Identify one insurance capability that genuinely needs an independently operated agent and one that should remain an ordinary local tool."}, {"id": 222, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How do A2A, MCP, and ordinary APIs differ?", "text": "How do A2A, MCP, and ordinary APIs differ? A2A concerns agent-to-agent interactions and task-oriented collaboration. MCP commonly exposes tools and resources to model-enabled clients. A normal API can provide deterministic domain operations without claiming to be an autonomous peer. These interfaces can coexist but do not grant authority by themselves. The Ragnor MCP bridge and OmniCare's HTTP routes illustrate tool and application boundaries. A remote specialist could use A2A while its internal tools still call authorized domain APIs. Treating a peer agent as a trusted internal function can bypass user scope or disclose entire conversations. Capability discovery is not consent to every requested action. Protocol choice affects interoperability and lifecycle semantics. Use the narrowest interface that meets the integration need rather than wrapping every endpoint in an agent protocol. Map policy search, claim confirmation, long-running specialist review, and metrics export to appropriate interfaces and explain the decision."}, {"id": 223, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "What is an Agent Card, and why is it not an authorization token?", "text": "What is an Agent Card, and why is it not an authorization token? An Agent Card describes an agent's identity, capabilities, skills, endpoint, and security requirements. Discovery helps a client select a compatible service. Credentials and authorization are separately validated when invoking protected operations. A proposed insurance specialist should publish only the capabilities intended for its audience. Private discovery or protected extended descriptions may be appropriate for sensitive internal services. A malicious or stale card can point at an unintended endpoint or overstate capabilities. Do not send credentials or private claim data to arbitrary discovered destinations without trust and network policy. Dynamic discovery improves flexibility and adds authenticity, caching, and allowlist requirements. Explicitly configured peers are simpler for a small controlled deployment. Draft a secret-free capability description for a review-only specialist. List information that belongs in deployment configuration rather than its public card."}, {"id": 224, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How do messages, tasks, and artifacts differ in A2A?", "text": "How do messages, tasks, and artifacts differ in A2A? A message is an exchange of content, a task represents tracked work with lifecycle state, and an artifact is an output produced by that work. A reply being delivered does not necessarily mean the task completed or its output is authorized for all users. A proposed insurance review might acknowledge receipt, request missing documents, and later return a cited recommendation. The host should map that task to an internal case or operation ID rather than only appending free-form chat text. Treating progress text as a final approved decision can trigger premature downstream action. Artifact references also need ownership, integrity, revision, and retrieval limits. Explicit tasks support long-running work and require durable tracking. One synchronous response is easier when the operation is genuinely short. Design an internal task record for a review request with owner, remote ID, status, result reference, deadline, and idempotency key."}, {"id": 225, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How should remote-agent authentication and user delegation work?", "text": "How should remote-agent authentication and user delegation work? Authenticate the service peer and separately authorize the delegated action for the originating user or case. A service identity should receive only the evidence and actions needed for its task. Do not forward a browser cookie indiscriminately across organizational boundaries. Current tools derive user scope from trusted local context. A2A adds a network boundary where that implicit context must become a validated delegation contract with expiry and audience restrictions. A powerful remote agent can become a confused deputy, using its own privileges for a caller who lacks them. Agent-card metadata does not enforce resource authorization. Narrow delegated capabilities reduce disclosure and require identity integration. Broad shared credentials simplify demos and make accountability difficult. Define which identity approves a review, which identity reads evidence, and which identity may confirm a claim. Keep final write authority local unless explicitly delegated."}, {"id": 226, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How do remote retries avoid duplicate work?", "text": "How do remote retries avoid duplicate work? Give one logical request a stable operation identity, persist its relationship to the remote task, and query or reconcile before creating another task after an ambiguous timeout. Transport request IDs and business idempotency keys may serve different purposes. The existing chat response cache is best effort and not a durable distributed workflow registry. A proposed A2A adapter should use a stronger operation record for consequential review or submission work. A timeout can mean the remote service accepted the task but the acknowledgment was lost. Blind retries can create duplicate cases, charges, or conflicting recommendations. Reconciliation adds storage and latency but avoids assuming failure from missing acknowledgment. Fire-and-forget messaging is smaller and unsuitable for untracked consequential work. Simulate remote acceptance followed by a dropped response. Show how the client discovers or reuses the original task without submitting twice."}, {"id": 227, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "What does cancellation mean for a remote agent task?", "text": "What does cancellation mean for a remote agent task? Cancellation is a request to stop eligible work, not proof that every side effect has been undone. A task may have completed or crossed an irreversible boundary before the cancellation arrives. The host must interpret the remote result and reconcile its own state. The insurance application already distinguishes canceling a pending submission from withdrawing an existing claim. A remote-agent integration needs similarly precise lifecycle semantics. Closing the browser stream does not establish task cancellation. A user may otherwise believe a case was abandoned while remote work continues or a notification is delivered. Explicit cancellation acknowledgment improves user expectations and adds race handling. Letting tasks finish is operationally simpler for read-only work if clearly communicated. Draw cancellation before acceptance, during review, and after completion. Define the displayed state and permissible follow-up action for each race."}, {"id": 228, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How can A2A artifacts become a prompt-injection or disclosure channel?", "text": "How can A2A artifacts become a prompt-injection or disclosure channel? A remote result can contain untrusted text, URLs, attachments, or instructions. Treat it as data from a service with a specific trust level, validate its schema, and allowlist any subsequent action. Remote output must not inherit system-instruction authority. The existing prompt already classifies retrieved policy and tool text as data. A2A expands that principle to independently operated peers and their returned evidence. An artifact can ask the model to retrieve another customer's claim or fetch an internal URL. Authentication of the sender does not make every returned instruction safe or every referenced document authorized. Rich artifacts support useful collaboration and need validation, size limits, safe fetching, and provenance. Minimal structured references are easier to govern than entire conversations and attachments. Return a mock artifact containing a cross-tenant reference and an instruction to bypass confirmation. Verify neither is executed."}, {"id": 229, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "How should one trace span cross an A2A boundary?", "text": "How should one trace span cross an A2A boundary? Propagate an approved trace context and stable operation identifiers while keeping credentials and private content separate. Each service records its own spans and outcome; the host correlates the remote task with the originating operation. The current tracing adapter stitches local turn, tool, and model calls. Remote services add clock, sampling, retry, and ownership boundaries that need an explicit propagation policy. A trace ID is not authentication, and untrusted inbound trace metadata needs validation. Copying full claim text into span attributes can leak it to both organizations' telemetry systems. Distributed traces speed diagnosis but require shared conventions and retention policy. Correlation by sanitized operation IDs is a simpler fallback. Design a trace tree for one remote review with a retry and later artifact delivery. Show which spans represent waiting versus remote execution."}, {"id": 230, "chapter": "A2A and distributed agent collaboration", "slug": "a2a", "title": "When is a multi-agent architecture justified for insurance work?", "text": "When is a multi-agent architecture justified for insurance work? Use independently scoped agents when capabilities have distinct ownership, data access, deployment, or specialist review responsibilities. A local planner calling five functions does not need networked peers simply to look sophisticated. A proposed policy-analysis specialist and a human-review service might have different permissions and operating teams. The current local registry remains an appropriate baseline for coverage lookup and claim preparation. More peers increase routing errors, coordination cost, inconsistent evidence, and cascading failures. Agreement between agents is not independent verification if they share the same wrong source or model. Specialization and organizational boundaries can justify distributed collaboration; a single orchestrator with narrow tools is cheaper and easier to test. Write an A2A architecture decision record with a task lifecycle, delegation policy, data-minimization plan, failure budget, and a simpler alternative."}, {"id": 231, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "Why is answer evaluation insufficient for an agent?", "text": "Why is answer evaluation insufficient for an agent? An answer can sound correct after using the wrong tool, revealing unauthorized information, repeating an expensive action, or failing to commit the intended operation. Agent evaluation needs task success, tool choice and arguments, permissions, side effects, recovery, and final communication. OmniCare's tools include both read operations and preparation. A claim confirmation has database invariants that no similarity score over final prose can verify. A model judge can reward a statement that a claim was submitted even when no claim exists. Conversely, a safely rejected operation may sound less helpful while being the correct outcome. Multi-layer evaluation needs more fixtures and gives actionable diagnosis. One aggregate answer score is easier to display and weaker as a release gate. Create three cases with identical final text but different underlying tool and database histories. Define which pass and why."}, {"id": 232, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How do reference trajectories and invariant-based trajectories differ?", "text": "How do reference trajectories and invariant-based trajectories differ? A reference trajectory compares observed calls with an expected sequence. An invariant checks necessary properties such as owner scoping, no duplicate confirmation, and policy retrieval before a coverage determination. Different valid tool sequences may satisfy the same task. ADK provides trajectory-oriented evaluation, and OmniCare's prompt has concrete tool-routing rules. Use exact paths for deterministic protocols and allowed partial orders for flexible multi-intent turns. Exact sequence tests can fail harmless reordering; very loose rubrics can miss a missing critical step. Tool names alone also ignore whether arguments or results were correct. Reference paths are easy to inspect and brittle under valid variation. Invariants need careful definition and better express safety and business requirements. Evaluate a mixed coverage/status request with two valid call orders, then reject both if the claim lookup lacks the correct owner context."}, {"id": 233, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "What does the host's context evaluation mode actually measure?", "text": "What does the host's context evaluation mode actually measure? Context mode evaluates retrieved material as the answer-like input to answer metrics. It can diagnose whether retrieval contains useful content, but it is not a measurement of a live agent's generated response. High faithfulness here must not be presented as proof that the agent follows evidence. domain/evaluation.py distinguishes retrieval-only, context, and end-to-end modes and records answer_source. It also labels an offline retrieved-context substitute used when a live generator is unavailable. Comparing proxy scores with live-agent scores without checking labels can create a false regression or improvement. Availability fallbacks should never silently change the meaning of the reported metric. Offline proxies are cheap and useful for early diagnosis. Actual agent evaluation costs provider calls and measures a different layer. Run or inspect configurations for all modes and assert the reported answer_source. Separate proxy and live results in your report."}, {"id": 234, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How should a multi-turn claim-preparation test be structured?", "text": "How should a multi-turn claim-preparation test be structured? Model the user's progressive facts, corrections, missing fields, preparation, and explicit confirmation boundary. Assert the prepared payload and server effects at each step, not only whether the assistant eventually produces a token. The prompt instructs collection of policy number, claim type, amount, and description and special handling of corrections. The actual pending-token lifecycle must be checked against those promises. A user simulator may accidentally supply hints or accept a fabricated success. Test fixtures should inspect the database and use deterministic assertions for required fields and token state. Multi-turn tests take more execution and reveal memory and correction failures absent from isolated prompts. Keep a small high-value regression set alongside larger sampled evaluations. Create a scenario that changes the amount after preparation and attempts the old token. Report both current behavior and the desired contract."}, {"id": 235, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How should refusal and clarification be evaluated separately?", "text": "How should refusal and clarification be evaluated separately? Refusal is appropriate when the task is unsupported or evidence cannot justify an answer. Clarification is appropriate when obtaining missing facts could make the task answerable. Escalation is another outcome requiring truthful operational status. The host dataset includes expected-behavior labels, and the prompt has an uncertainty ladder and domain restrictions. Extend labels beyond answer/refuse when product outcomes require more nuance. Rewarding all refusals as safe can produce an unusable assistant. Rewarding direct answers to ambiguous coverage questions can produce unsupported decisions. Rich outcome labels cost annotation effort and better match product expectations. A binary correct/incorrect label is simpler and loses diagnostic detail. Label cases as answer, clarify, refuse, retry, or escalate. Require evidence for every final coverage claim and an actual handoff result before claiming transfer."}, {"id": 236, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How can evaluation verify that writes happened exactly as intended?", "text": "How can evaluation verify that writes happened exactly as intended? Assert durable rows, ownership, consumed-token state, lifecycle events, and outbox records after the operation. Repeat and race requests to verify logical uniqueness. Evaluate external deliveries separately from database acceptance. Confirmation combines several writes in one caller-owned transaction. Tests can inspect that one logical claim exists and that rollback removes related pending effects when a later write fails. A mocked submit function can return success without exercising SQL locks or commits. A judge of answer text cannot establish atomicity or idempotency. Real integration tests are slower and necessary for authoritative effects. Cheap fake-model tests remain useful for routing and error mapping around them. Build a failure-injection table for every write in confirmation. For each failure, assert the visible claim, submission, event, and job state."}, {"id": 237, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How should a judge be calibrated and protected from the answer being judged?", "text": "How should a judge be calibrated and protected from the answer being judged? Use a precise rubric, trusted references, known positive and negative cases, and human review to measure agreement. Treat candidate answer and retrieved text as untrusted data in the judge prompt. Record judge model, prompt, and sampling settings. Ragnor's answer evaluators provide useful score containers, while insurance-specific criteria must reflect unsupported limits, missing exclusions, authorization, and false submission claims. A candidate can contain instructions to the judge, and a judge can favor style over factual support. A second model is not independent truth when it shares the same missing evidence. Model judging scales nuanced review and costs money and uncertainty. Deterministic checks should handle exact citations, amounts, permissions, and state transitions where possible. Add a fluent unsupported answer and a judge-injection answer to a calibration set. Compare model verdicts with explicit human labels."}, {"id": 238, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How do uncertainty intervals and category slices improve evaluation?", "text": "How do uncertainty intervals and category slices improve evaluation? A score from a small sample is an estimate, not a universal property. Report sample counts, uncertainty, and important categories such as exclusions, limits, corrections, unanswerable questions, and authorization failures. Inspect severity as well as frequency. The host defines categories and difficulty metadata that can support stratified reports. A high aggregate score can hide a small but serious failure class. Repeatedly tuning on the same visible cases overfits the benchmark. Averaging a cross-user disclosure with several harmless formatting errors gives a misleading quality summary. Category reporting creates more numbers and reveals actionable regressions. Small categories need cautious interpretation rather than overconfident ranking. Split a benchmark by answerability and category, publish counts, and flag critical failures as release blockers instead of averaging them away."}, {"id": 239, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "How should evaluation control cost and side effects?", "text": "How should evaluation control cost and side effects? Separate deterministic unit tests, offline retrieval checks, fake-model trajectories, and opt-in live-agent runs. Use synthetic identities and a sandbox, forbid real irreversible actions, and bound tokens, calls, and total spend. Record which execution mode actually ran. OmniCare uses an explicit live-evaluation environment opt-in and a synthetic evaluation user identity. That helps isolate data, but test tools and external integrations still need sandbox behavior. A live agent can prepare records or call real services if its test capabilities are not constrained. Test traces may also export fixtures containing private data. Offline checks are fast and less realistic; live runs expose provider behavior and require stronger controls. Use both without conflating their scores. Define a CI budget and a nightly live-evaluation budget with safe tool replacements and explicit answer-source assertions."}, {"id": 240, "chapter": "Evaluating agents and insurance outcomes", "slug": "agent-evaluation", "title": "What should an agent release gate require?", "text": "What should an agent release gate require? Require critical authorization and write invariants, non-regression on held-out task outcomes, acceptable latency and cost, and a rollback path. Keep quality thresholds distinct from informational efficiency metrics. A release gate should reflect product harm and operational capacity. The backend has baseline and evaluation-gate tests. Extend their report with trajectory, correction, false-success, and privacy checks rather than only raising an answer-score average. A changing judge or proxy fallback can move metrics without a real behavior change. A passing average must never excuse a critical cross-user disclosure or duplicate write. Strict gates slow some releases and prevent expensive regressions. Narrow deterministic checks should fail quickly before costly live evaluation. Write a gate with hard denials, category quality thresholds, p95 latency, cost per successful task, and explicit metadata compatibility checks."}, {"id": 241, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "How do you choose an evaluation library without turning the stack into a collection of dashboards?", "text": "How do you choose an evaluation library without turning the stack into a collection of dashboards? Start from the question to measure: retrieval relevance, grounded answers, tool trajectories, task completion, adversarial behavior, or production drift. Choose one durable experiment record and connect additional metrics only where they add diagnostic value. OmniCare already has Ragnor-based evaluation and stored run records. Another library should add a missing measurement or workflow rather than replace labeled data with a brand name. Different tools may use the same metric name for different inputs, judges, and aggregation rules. Comparing their numeric outputs without recording definitions is invalid. One harness is easier to operate; specialized libraries can deepen particular checks. Prefer a small adapter around a shared dataset and result schema. Create a selection matrix for ADK evaluation, LangSmith, Ragas, DeepEval, TruLens, Phoenix, and Promptfoo. State one justified use and one limitation for each."}, {"id": 242, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "What does ADK evaluation add for this current ADK agent?", "text": "What does ADK evaluation add for this current ADK agent? ADK evaluation can test tool-use trajectories and final responses using reference or rubric-based criteria. Its fit is strongest when you want evaluation close to the runtime's sessions and events, including reviewed multi-turn cases. The project currently calls a Ragnor evaluation runner rather than proving every ADK criterion is enabled. A proposed ADK suite should exercise the same five-tool registry and inspect actual schema and version support. Exact trajectory matching can reject valid alternative orders, and judge-based criteria add model dependencies and cost. Some criteria require additional services; the dependency's broad lower bound does not guarantee every current documentation feature exists locally. Runtime-native evaluation reduces event translation and may couple fixtures to the framework. A framework-neutral contract suite survives migration more easily. Define one exact confirmation-preparation trajectory and one rubric for flexible coverage review. Pin versions before running the documented criterion configuration."}, {"id": 243, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "What is LangSmith evaluation useful for if the agent uses ADK?", "text": "What is LangSmith evaluation useful for if the agent uses ADK? LangSmith supports datasets, experiments, evaluators, and review workflows that need not require LangChain as the agent runtime. The application can submit its own target function and measured outputs while retaining ADK execution. OmniCare already integrates LangSmith tracing through an adapter. Evaluation is a separate integration decision; having traces does not prove a dataset or evaluator has been configured. Uploading examples, outputs, or traces can disclose claim data. Experiment identity must distinguish model output from an offline context proxy and preserve prompt, corpus, and evaluator revisions. A shared experiment service improves collaboration and adds data-governance and service-dependency considerations. Local JSON reports are easier to keep isolated and less convenient for team review. Design a target function returning answer, evidence IDs, tool trace, and answer_source for one synthetic case. Specify which fields may be exported."}, {"id": 244, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "How does Ragas help evaluate retrieval-augmented answers?", "text": "How does Ragas help evaluate retrieval-augmented answers? Ragas provides metrics for relationships among questions, retrieved contexts, answers, and references. Faithfulness asks whether answer claims are supported by context; other metrics address relevance and retrieval behavior. Each metric has required inputs and may use a model judge. A proposed Ragas adapter should convert OmniCare's actual outputs into the chosen metric schema, retaining source IDs and answer_source. It is not installed merely because Ragnor has an evaluation module. Faithfulness to the wrong policy revision can still score well. Missing references, synthetic contexts, and judge changes can alter meaning or cost. Specialized RAG diagnostics help isolate failure layers and do not establish authorization, exact-money calculations, or correct claim writes. Keep those deterministic tests separate. Compare an answer faithful to stale evidence with an answer correct for the active policy. Explain why both evidence applicability and faithfulness must be evaluated."}, {"id": 245, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "How does DeepEval fit into a pytest-oriented workflow?", "text": "How does DeepEval fit into a pytest-oriented workflow? DeepEval provides test-case and metric abstractions for language-model systems, including faithfulness evaluation over retrieval context. It can make selected behavioral quality checks part of an existing test workflow while still depending on judge configuration for relevant metrics. The insurance backend already uses pytest. The worked example shows a proposed LLMTestCase and FaithfulnessMetric adapter with an injected judge, not an installed or executed production evaluation. A unit-test appearance can conceal network calls, nondeterminism, and cost. A passing metric does not prove server-side authorization or transaction atomicity. Developer-friendly tests improve feedback locality; broad judge-heavy CI can become slow and flaky. Run exact contract tests frequently and sampled model judging under a controlled budget. Add one faithful and one unsupported answer to an optional evaluation suite, pin the judge configuration, and record raw verdicts alongside thresholds."}, {"id": 246, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "What does the TruLens RAG triad measure?", "text": "What does the TruLens RAG triad measure? The RAG triad distinguishes context relevance, groundedness of the response in context, and answer relevance to the question. Separating these relationships helps identify whether retrieval or generation deserves investigation. OmniCare's current context and end-to-end modes can be mapped to some of these questions, but a complete TruLens integration would need explicit feedback functions and recording. The host does not currently instantiate it. Good triad scores do not establish that the policy applies to this user or that a submitted claim exists. Domain and permission checks remain outside those semantic relationships. Structured feedback improves diagnosis while adding instrumentation and evaluator configuration. Use it when the team will act on the distinctions rather than only collect more scores. Create three failures: irrelevant context, unsupported answer, and grounded but off-topic answer. Map each to the relationship that should fail."}, {"id": 247, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "How can Phoenix combine tracing and evaluation?", "text": "How can Phoenix combine tracing and evaluation? Phoenix supports inspecting model-system traces and evaluating behavior using recorded inputs and outputs. It can help connect a quality failure to a particular retrieval, tool, or model span. It is a proposed observability/evaluation alternative here, not an existing backend integration. The current tracing facade localizes vendor-specific code. An experiment can export a reviewed trace schema to Phoenix while keeping application instrumentation and business tools unchanged. Automatic instrumentation may capture sensitive arguments or create duplicate spans alongside LangSmith. Local hosting alone does not guarantee safe access control or retention. Correlated traces and evaluation improve diagnosis but require export policy, span conventions, and service operation. One well-configured backend is often enough. Define the minimum trace fields needed to explain a wrong policy answer, then compare that allowlist with an automatic export's actual payload."}, {"id": 248, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "Where does Promptfoo fit compared with a Python evaluation harness?", "text": "Where does Promptfoo fit compared with a Python evaluation harness? Promptfoo provides configuration-driven evaluations and assertions over model or application outputs, useful for comparing prompts, providers, and adversarial cases. A Python harness is often more natural for inspecting internal service and database state. A proposed HTTP or custom-provider adapter can target the insurance assistant while existing pytest tests verify writes and ownership. Keep fixture credentials and execution modes explicit. A contains assertion can pass on quoted or negated text, and a model-graded assertion still depends on judge quality. End-to-end outputs alone cannot reveal every unsafe intermediate tool call. Declarative matrices make broad experiments convenient; custom Python gives finer control over stateful workflows and failure injection. Define deterministic assertions for an empty result and missing confirmation, then add a reviewed rubric for coverage faithfulness. Explain which checks need backend instrumentation."}, {"id": 249, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "How do you make evaluation results comparable across libraries?", "text": "How do you make evaluation results comparable across libraries? Preserve a shared case identity, corpus revision, policy applicability, model and prompt identity, exact evidence, trajectory, answer_source, judge configuration, and failure policy. Record raw results before converting them to a common report. OmniCare already persists evaluation runs; the expanded examples include a framework-neutral result record and deterministic invariant scorer. Library-specific adapters can write into that shape without pretending different semantic metrics are identical. Averaging one library's probability-like score with another's binary verdict produces an uninterpretable number. Silent skipping of failed cases can also inflate averages. Rich records cost storage and support reproducibility. A compact dashboard is useful only when links expose the underlying definitions and examples. Run the same two synthetic cases through two adapters and compare case coverage, inputs, judge settings, and failure handling before comparing scores."}, {"id": 250, "chapter": "Agent and RAG evaluation libraries", "slug": "evaluation-libraries", "title": "What evaluation stack is a sensible starting point for this project?", "text": "What evaluation stack is a sensible starting point for this project? Retain deterministic pytest contracts for security and writes, use the existing Ragnor harness for retrieval diagnosis, and add a small opt-in agent trajectory suite. Choose one experiment and tracing workflow, then add one specialized evaluator only for a demonstrated gap. This builds on implemented code instead of installing every named library. The comparison table and worked examples explain alternatives without declaring that all should run simultaneously. Tool proliferation fragments datasets, duplicates provider spend, and increases private-data copies. A simpler stack with reviewed gold cases can produce more reliable engineering decisions. Incremental adoption delays some advanced features and keeps failure attribution manageable. Expand when the team can name the question the current tools cannot answer. Write a one-page evaluation architecture with test tiers, dataset owner, judge budget, privacy policy, and release blockers. Limit the first implementation to three layers."}, {"id": 251, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "How do logs, metrics, traces, audit records, and evaluations differ?", "text": "How do logs, metrics, traces, audit records, and evaluations differ? Logs describe events, metrics aggregate measurements, traces connect causal execution spans, audit records document selected authoritative actions, and evaluations assess behavior against expectations. They can correlate through operation IDs but are not substitutes for one another. OmniCare has request-context logging, bounded metrics, LangSmith turn/tool/model tracing, an audit service, and stored evaluation runs. Each has its own data and failure policy. A successful trace export does not prove a claim committed, and an audit row does not explain a model's poor retrieval. Confusing these roles produces incomplete incident diagnosis. Multiple signals add operational work and answer different questions. Start with a small coherent schema rather than unrelated dashboards. For a failed confirmation after a successful model turn, state which signal proves the transaction result and which explains the preceding tool choice."}, {"id": 252, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "What should a useful agent trace tree contain?", "text": "What should a useful agent trace tree contain? One request or turn root should connect relevant model, tool, retrieval, and persistence activity with durations and sanitized outcomes. Include operation and version metadata needed to reproduce the failure, while excluding secrets and unnecessary private content. tracing.py exposes a vendor-neutral surface; LangSmith implementation decorates turns and tools and links LiteLLM completions beneath the current turn. Correct parentage makes one operation understandable end to end. Orphan model spans can hide which user-visible turn incurred cost. Copying entire state into attributes creates a privacy problem and a noisy trace. Rich trace structure improves diagnosis but adds instrumentation complexity. Keep high-value boundaries stable and avoid tracing every tiny helper. Draw the trace tree for coverage review with two tool calls, retrieval, one retry, and final persistence. Mark parallel versus sequential time honestly."}, {"id": 253, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "Why does the ADK-LiteLLM-LangSmith bridge need explicit context stitching?", "text": "Why does the ADK-LiteLLM-LangSmith bridge need explicit context stitching? Different instrumentation layers may not share the same active trace context. A parent turn recorded by one SDK and a completion recorded by another callback can otherwise appear as unrelated runs. Correlation metadata bridges that boundary. langsmith.py reads the current run tree and injects IDs and ordering metadata at ADK's LiteLLMClient seam. It installs the patch idempotently and registers the callback once to avoid duplicate spans. Patching a framework seam is sensitive to dependency changes. A successful import does not prove nesting remains correct after an upgrade, and duplicate callbacks can double recorded usage. A localized bridge preserves business-code independence and needs targeted compatibility tests. Provider-native tracing can be simpler but may not connect all application activity. Assert one root turn with nested tool and model spans before and after repeated startup. Verify parent IDs using synthetic data."}, {"id": 254, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "How do you distinguish tool exceptions from business failures in telemetry?", "text": "How do you distinguish tool exceptions from business failures in telemetry? Record transport/runtime success separately from domain outcome. A function that returns error='backend unavailable' has executed without raising but did not satisfy the user task. An empty successful search is another distinct outcome. instrument_tool records success when the handler returns normally. That is useful runtime information but does not automatically classify returned found=False or success=False payloads. The stream and prompt layers interpret some of those shapes separately. A dashboard based only on exceptions can report excellent tool reliability while users receive failed searches or validations. Mixing not-found with outages also obscures incident severity. Typed outcome categories require consistent tool schemas and provide actionable alerts. Raw exception counts are simple and incomplete. Add outcome dimensions for completed, empty, validation_rejected, unauthorized, backend_failed, and cancelled. Test each without logging private arguments."}, {"id": 255, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "How should token and cost telemetry handle unknown prices?", "text": "How should token and cost telemetry handle unknown prices? Record observed usage, estimated cost, pricing source, and whether pricing is known. Unknown should not be silently interpreted as free. Compare cost per successful task and per stage rather than only per model request. The LiteLLM callback prefers completion_cost and falls back to response metadata, returning zero if pricing cannot be resolved. That observed fallback needs an uncertainty label in operational interpretation. A zero-cost metric can hide a new provider or unsupported model price. Retries, embedding, and reranking may also be omitted from a narrow generation-only dashboard. Detailed accounting adds metadata and improves budget control. Provider invoices remain a reconciliation source rather than assuming local estimates are exact bills. Send a synthetic unknown-model usage record and verify it is reported as unpriced, not confidently free. Attribute all calls in one successful task."}, {"id": 256, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "Why are in-process metrics different from fleet-wide monitoring?", "text": "Why are in-process metrics different from fleet-wide monitoring? Process-local counters and bounded event buffers describe one process since its start. They do not automatically aggregate across replicas, survive restarts, or provide a durable long-term time series. Fleet monitoring requires collection and identity rules. The backend's metrics module provides useful local accounting and telemetry routes. Exporters or an external collection system are needed for deployment-wide retention and aggregation. Summing request snapshots repeatedly can double count, and restarting a process can look like a sudden drop in demand. High-cardinality labels such as raw claim IDs can overwhelm a metrics backend. Local metrics are lightweight and useful for debugging; shared systems add infrastructure and coherent operational visibility. Run two replicas and restart one. Specify how counters, gauges, and histograms should be exported and queried without inventing fleet totals."}, {"id": 257, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "How can trace collection disclose private insurance data?", "text": "How can trace collection disclose private insurance data? Tool arguments, results, prompts, completions, exception strings, and URLs can contain policyholder or claim information. Tracing a trusted tool does not make its payload safe to export. Data minimization must apply before or at the instrumentation/export boundary. The host's trace wrappers can record tool inputs and outputs, while the Ragnor event layer has different redaction defaults. Those are separate pipelines. A redacted metric does not sanitize a full LangSmith run automatically. Trace readers or vendors may gain access broader than the product's owner-scoped API permits. Confirmation tokens and source text should not appear by accident in support exports. Content-rich traces help reproduce model failures and increase privacy obligations. Prefer sanitized fixtures and reviewed sampled content when necessary. Inventory actual exported payloads for one synthetic claim preparation. Define an allowlist and prove a fake token and private description are absent."}, {"id": 258, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "Should tracing failures be allowed to fail business operations?", "text": "Should tracing failures be allowed to fail business operations? Optional telemetry often uses best-effort failure containment to preserve availability. Mandatory business audit may require a different policy. Decide per signal rather than declaring that every kind of observation may be dropped harmlessly. setup_tracing catches errors and the tracing integration degrades gracefully. audit/service.py also describes best-effort behavior, a separate choice whose suitability depends on the required business guarantees. Silent instrumentation failure can leave an incident undiagnosable. Conversely, blocking every request on a slow telemetry vendor can create an avoidable outage. Fail-open telemetry improves availability and needs health signals for lost coverage. Transactional audit or a durable audit outbox increases assurance and operational coupling. Simulate unavailable tracing and unavailable audit storage separately. Define the expected request, readiness, and alert behavior for each."}, {"id": 259, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "What SLOs and alerts are useful for an insurance assistant?", "text": "What SLOs and alerts are useful for an insurance assistant? Monitor successful authorized task completion, stream completion, backend failures, indexing lag, oldest pending job age, provider throttling, and cost per completed task. Use freshness and correctness signals alongside request latency. The application exposes health and telemetry surfaces, while evaluation runs provide sampled behavioral evidence. A fast answer that falsely claims submission should count as a failed task, not a latency success. Alerting only on average HTTP latency misses stuck jobs and quality failures. Sampling can also miss rare severe authorization incidents, which need deterministic denial monitoring and tests. More nuanced SLOs require definitions and ownership. A small set linked to concrete user impact is more maintainable than dozens of disconnected thresholds. Define a stream-success SLO and an indexing-lag SLO, including numerator, denominator, window, and corrective action."}, {"id": 260, "chapter": "Agent observability and trace privacy", "slug": "agent-observability", "title": "How should observability produce better evaluation cases?", "text": "How should observability produce better evaluation cases? Triage a failure trace, minimize and sanitize it, establish the correct outcome with a reviewer, and add a versioned regression case. Keep raw production data access restricted and preserve which failure the new case is intended to catch. The host's tracing facade and evaluation-run storage can support a feedback loop. A trace alone is an observation; a reviewed expectation turns it into a useful test. Automatically adding model answers as gold labels reinforces errors. Copying production conversations into broad datasets can violate data-minimization policy. Reviewed case creation costs human time and improves relevance. Fully synthetic tests scale cheaply and may miss real ambiguity. Turn one synthetic wrong-policy trace into a case with expected revision, required tool steps, allowed final outcomes, and a clear privacy review."}, {"id": 261, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "Why is coverage support different from adjudicating a claim?", "text": "Why is coverage support different from adjudicating a claim? Coverage support explains retrieved policy language and identifies missing facts. Adjudication applies an authorized business process to a specific claim and records a decision. A conversational assistant should not imply that an explanation is a binding approval or denial. The prompt asks for coverage procedures and source-grounded facts, while claim status transitions live in deterministic application code. Administrative transitions are currently denied by default through an empty actor allowlist. A fluent statement such as this is covered can be interpreted more strongly than the available evidence supports. The host must distinguish general sample-policy scope from verified personal contract applicability. Careful conditional explanations may feel less decisive and preserve the actual authority boundary. Automated adjudication is a separate product requiring substantially stronger domain and governance evidence. Rewrite three coverage answers to state the supporting clause, material conditions, unresolved facts, and whether an authorized decision has actually occurred."}, {"id": 262, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "How should incident date, filing date, and policy effective date be handled?", "text": "How should incident date, filing date, and policy effective date be handled? They describe different events. Incident date concerns the reported occurrence, filing date records submission, and policy effective dates identify versions or applicability intervals. None should be guessed from another simply to fill a required field. Claims have a nullable incident_date and database timestamps. Preparation can carry incident date through pending JSONB, while PolicyVersion has effective_from and effective_to. Their presence does not prove the retrieval path joins them correctly. Applying today's policy to an earlier incident can produce an inaccurate answer. Time zones and inclusive/exclusive boundary rules also matter for exact applicability. Explicit temporal modeling adds query and validation work. A current-only general FAQ is simpler if the UI honestly limits its scope. Create incidents before, at, and after a policy-version boundary. Specify the applicable interval rule and the evidence required for each answer."}, {"id": 263, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "Why must exclusions and conditions be retrieved alongside a positive coverage clause?", "text": "Why must exclusions and conditions be retrieved alongside a positive coverage clause? A positive clause can be limited by exclusions, definitions, sublimits, deductibles, and factual conditions elsewhere. Retrieving one semantically similar paragraph is not necessarily sufficient to explain applicability. The procedure-and-policy split is intended to guide a fuller review. Current query_policy returns five retrieved results and section-level citations; that implementation does not guarantee every related exception is included. A faithful summary of an isolated clause can still mislead by omitting a controlling exception. More top-k hits may not solve the problem if the corpus lacks structured relationships. Targeted follow-up retrieval or structured links improve completeness and add calls and complexity. Large indiscriminate context increases noise and cost. Build a case where the positive clause ranks highly but its exclusion is elsewhere. Evaluate whether a follow-up query or section expansion retrieves the missing condition."}, {"id": 264, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "Why is a section citation weaker than a revision-aware evidence reference?", "text": "Why is a section citation weaker than a revision-aware evidence reference? A section label identifies a human-readable location, but may not uniquely identify source, revision, or exact supporting span. A reproducible reference needs enough identity to retrieve what the user originally saw. query_policy deduplicates citations by section and returns source/section with optional distance. It does not currently expose the library's complete revision-aware EvidenceItem contract. Two sources with the same section name can therefore need richer identity. A later policy edit can make an old answer unverifiable, and section-only deduplication can collapse distinct sources. A fallback source filename is not evidence of the original document's identity. Rich citations add metadata and snapshot retention while enabling verification. Simple labels work for a small sample corpus and should not be overstated. Create two sources with an identically named section and different terms. Design a stable citation key and bounded source-opening API."}, {"id": 265, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "How should OCR, tables, photographs, and attachments extend the insurance pipeline?", "text": "How should OCR, tables, photographs, and attachments extend the insurance pipeline? Treat each modality as a separate extraction and evidence problem. OCR converts visible text, tables need row-column relationships, and photographs may require a vision model or human review. A file upload alone does not create reliable multimodal reasoning. The current backend demonstrates text policy/procedure ingestion and structured claim descriptions. The Ragnor extractor seam can support extensions, but the inspected host does not establish a complete attachment-processing workflow. OCR errors in amounts, dates, or negations can change meaning. Attachments can also contain malware, private third-party data, prompt injection, and oversized payloads. Richer inputs improve user access and require scanning, extraction confidence, provenance, storage policy, and review states. Text-only support is narrower and easier to validate. Design an attachment intake state machine with quarantine, extraction, review, indexing, and rejection. Keep source bytes separate from generated interpretations."}, {"id": 266, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "Why should fraud or risk signals not be treated as automatic truth?", "text": "Why should fraud or risk signals not be treated as automatic truth? A statistical risk score is evidence for investigation, not proof that a claimant is dishonest or that a claim should be denied. Training data, false positives, protected proxies, and changing behavior all affect interpretation. The current code does not implement a fraud model. A proposed extension belongs behind explicit review and evaluation boundaries, separate from coverage retrieval and status lookup. Presenting an opaque score as a decision can create harmful unsupported accusations and unreliable automation. It also introduces sensitive-data and model-governance obligations beyond this guide's engineering examples. Assisted triage can prioritize workload while requiring calibration, review, and appeal paths. Simple rule-based routing may be more transparent for a small system. Design a review-only output with supporting observable signals, uncertainty, missing information, and prohibited automatic actions. Do not invent a fraud threshold."}, {"id": 267, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "How should deterministic calculations complement generated explanations?", "text": "How should deterministic calculations complement generated explanations? Use reviewed deterministic code for exact arithmetic under explicitly selected policy rules, then let generation explain the result and assumptions. A model should not invent a deductible, round a monetary value arbitrarily, or apply an unverified formula. The backend stores Numeric amounts but does not implement a general payout calculator. The worked example demonstrates Decimal arithmetic as an educational calculation, not an actual coverage or payout rule. Correct arithmetic with the wrong rule or policy version is still wrong. Caps, sublimits, depreciation, multiple currencies, and exceptions require domain-approved logic. Deterministic calculators improve reproducibility and need versioned business specifications. Free-form arithmetic is convenient and unsuitable as authoritative financial logic. Write a hypothetical deductible calculation with explicit inputs and rounding, then state which real policy facts must be verified before using it."}, {"id": 268, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "What should a real human-escalation record contain?", "text": "What should a real human-escalation record contain? Store the originating operation, authorized user or case, reason, minimal evidence, destination, delivery state, and an acknowledgement or failure. Distinguish requested, accepted, in progress, and completed rather than claiming connection from generated prose alone. The current prompt contains escalation language, but no default registry handler demonstrates specialist assignment. Notification infrastructure is a useful building block, not proof that a human receives a case. Exporting a full conversation can disclose unnecessary personal data, and a failed handoff can leave a user waiting without an actual owner. Structured escalation adds integration work and truthful operational status. A clear support-contact instruction is a legitimate simpler fallback. Specify a proposed handoff service and acceptance tests for delivery failure, duplicate requests, revoked access, and a later human response."}, {"id": 269, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "How should fairness and accessibility be evaluated in insurance support?", "text": "How should fairness and accessibility be evaluated in insurance support? Evaluate whether the assistant handles equivalent requests consistently across language, writing style, disability-related interaction needs, and differing levels of domain knowledge. Focus on support outcomes and access, not unsupported automated judgments about protected traits. The current prompt and evaluation dataset provide a starting point, but fairness and accessibility require reviewed cases and a usable interface. A high English aggregate score does not establish equivalent performance for other users. Simplified wording can accidentally omit a condition, while multilingual retrieval can fail under ASCII lexical fallback. Voice or OCR inputs introduce another representation gap. Broader evaluation and interface support cost effort and improve real usability. Clearly bounded supported languages are preferable to an untested universal claim. Create semantically equivalent questions in plain language, formal wording, and a supported second language. Compare evidence, clarification, and escalation outcomes."}, {"id": 270, "chapter": "Insurance evidence and decision boundaries", "slug": "insurance-decisions", "title": "How should the application explain uncertainty without hiding system failure?", "text": "How should the application explain uncertainty without hiding system failure? Explain whether facts are missing, evidence is absent, the source is inapplicable, or a backend is unavailable. Give the strongest supported statement while preserving a distinct failure status. Uncertainty should guide the next action rather than be a vague disclaimer appended to everything. The prompt has an uncertainty ladder, but policy retrieval can convert errors to an empty result and the policy tool then returns a no-information message. That integration loses an important failure distinction from the library's richer types. An outage can be mistaken for proof that the policy says nothing. Reassuring language may also imply a specialist handoff that never happened. Explicit statuses require API and UI changes but make retries and support decisions honest. Text-only fallback is simple and ambiguous. Design separate responses for missing incident facts, no matching clause, source outage, stale policy, and completed escalation. Map each to a testable backend status."}, {"id": 271, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "What is the public SSE contract, and why should the browser not parse raw ADK internals?", "text": "What is the public SSE contract, and why should the browser not parse raw ADK internals? The API maps runtime events into text_delta, tool_call, tool_result, tool_error, error, and response_complete messages. A stable application contract lets the UI evolve independently of ADK and supports a future orchestrator adapter. chat_stream.py documents typed JSON frames and a terminal status. The route persists the turn before emitting message IDs in completion, linking the displayed answer to feedback authorization. Forwarding raw runtime objects can leak fields and break the UI after dependency changes. A stream ending at the network layer is not necessarily a successful response_complete event. An adapter adds code and preserves product stability. Direct runtime streaming is quicker for a demo and creates tighter coupling. Capture a synthetic successful and failed stream. Verify event discriminators, JSON validity, and exactly one meaningful terminal outcome for the actual route path."}, {"id": 272, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should a browser parse streaming chunks correctly?", "text": "How should a browser parse streaming chunks correctly? Buffer bytes across network reads, decode incrementally, split complete SSE frames, parse their JSON, and retain any unfinished suffix. A network chunk may contain half a JSON object or several events; it is not an event boundary. The backend emits data frames, but client transport segmentation is independent. The worked example supplies a framework-neutral frame buffer to teach this boundary without claiming to replace the project's frontend implementation. Naive JSON.parse on each read loses events or fails on multibyte characters. Error recovery must also distinguish a malformed event from a clean terminal completion. A small parser handles a narrow contract; a mature SSE client can cover more protocol details. Test the actual API framing and reconnection requirements. Feed the same stream split at every possible byte boundary, including inside Unicode text. Verify identical parsed events."}, {"id": 273, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "Why do persisted message IDs matter for feedback?", "text": "Why do persisted message IDs matter for feedback? Feedback should reference an actual assistant message owned by the user, not a temporary browser-generated ID. The backend can then authorize the record and connect a rating to its conversation, evidence, model configuration, and trace. response_complete carries assistant and user message IDs after save_conversation_turn. Feedback routes validate relationships instead of trusting that an arbitrary UUID represents a visible answer. Rating an optimistic local ID can fail or attach feedback to the wrong turn. A persistence failure may leave partial text visible but no authoritative message to rate. Waiting for server identity adds a final synchronization step and preserves referential integrity. Client-only messages are easy to render and weak as durable references. Simulate a stream that generates text but fails persistence. Define whether feedback is disabled and how retry behaves without duplicating the displayed answer."}, {"id": 274, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "What should the UI state machine distinguish during a turn?", "text": "What should the UI state machine distinguish during a turn? Distinguish draft, accepted, searching or tooling, generating, completed, failed, cancelled, and disconnected. Track evidence and persistence status separately where needed. A spinner stopping is not proof that the model completed successfully. The backend's error and terminal status fields provide signals for this state machine. The UI should retain partial output with an explicit incomplete marker when a stream fails. Treating every closed connection as completion can show truncated claims as final. Late responses from a prior conversation or signed-out user can also contaminate current state. Explicit states add frontend logic and improve recovery. A single loading boolean is compact and cannot represent the important distinctions. Create a reducer for stream events and test disconnect before completion, tool failure, persistence failure, cancellation, and a late event after tenant or conversation change."}, {"id": 275, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should claim confirmation be presented and protected in the UI?", "text": "How should claim confirmation be presented and protected in the UI? Display the exact prepared fields, identify that no claim is yet submitted, and send the token through the authenticated confirmation route only after the user's action. Disable duplicate controls for usability while retaining server-side race protection. The backend binds tokens to user and pending state and locks confirmation. The UI should not infer completion from the agent saying prepared or from a locally generated confirmation animation. Corrections can leave old pending tokens valid unless the server supersedes them. The UI must not promise revocation that only exists in prompt text. A review screen adds friction and supports consent to material fields. The authoritative check must remain on the server because browsers can retry or be bypassed. Test double click, refresh, expired token, cancellation race, and correction after preparation. Compare displayed state with the actual submission record."}, {"id": 276, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should browser caching respond to sign-out, role changes, and archived conversations?", "text": "How should browser caching respond to sign-out, role changes, and archived conversations? Partition cached results by trusted user and relevant resource identity, cancel or ignore obsolete in-flight responses, and clear private state at sign-out. Revalidate permissions for source opening and mutation even if a cached view once allowed access. Conversation and feedback routes are owner-scoped, while browser state is a separate implementation layer. A secure backend does not prevent a frontend from accidentally displaying another session's cached text. Reusing query keys across users can show private content without making a new unauthorized request. Hidden or archived messages can also remain in model context unless their lifecycle is mapped deliberately. Aggressive invalidation costs network requests and preserves clear session boundaries. Long-lived caches improve responsiveness and need stronger partitioning and freshness rules. Switch users during a slow stream and verify no old message, source, or confirmation token appears in the new user's interface."}, {"id": 277, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should citations and tool activity be rendered accessibly and safely?", "text": "How should citations and tool activity be rendered accessibly and safely? Render untrusted text safely, expose expandable source details by keyboard, use meaningful labels, and avoid announcing every token to assistive technology. Show user-relevant activity without exposing internal prompts, private tool arguments, or raw exception data. The backend can emit tool activity and section citations. The browser decides what is visible, navigable, and announced; receiving a field over the network is already disclosure even if the UI later hides it. Raw HTML or unsafe URLs can create XSS, and a busy live region can make streaming unusable. A similarity distance should not be displayed as percent certainty. Progressive disclosure reduces overload but must keep verification discoverable. Detailed internal traces belong in authorized operator tools, not the policyholder screen. Review a synthetic answer using only the keyboard and a screen reader. Include malicious markup and long source titles in the fixture."}, {"id": 278, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should client retries preserve operation identity?", "text": "How should client retries preserve operation identity? Reuse one idempotency key for retries of the same logical request, but create a new operation for a genuinely changed request. Record whether the server accepted or completed it. Claim confirmation should rely on its own token and durable state, not a generic chat response cache. core/idempotency.py scopes cached responses by user and verifies a body hash. Missing explicit keys can derive a deterministic key from user and message, affecting how repeated identical messages are interpreted. Reusing a key with a changed body should fail rather than replay another answer. Retrying a long-running request after a best-effort Redis wait can still duplicate model calls. Explicit operation identity requires lifecycle handling and supports predictable retries. Automatically retrying all failures is easy and can repeat expensive work. Test same key/same body, same key/changed body, new key/same body, and an ambiguous timeout. Document behavior for chat versus confirmation."}, {"id": 279, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "How should frontend, API, and agent contracts be tested together?", "text": "How should frontend, API, and agent contracts be tested together? Use a small set of full user journeys plus focused contract tests for events, ownership, persistence IDs, and confirmation effects. Fake providers make UI tests deterministic; separate live-provider tests assess actual model behavior. The backend has stream-contract and feedback-ID tests. The broader project has frontend end-to-end tests, but this handbook's conclusions about the backend do not certify all frontend behavior without executing those journeys. A mocked stream that always ends successfully cannot reveal cancellation or malformed-event behavior. Live model tests alone are too variable for every UI assertion. Layered tests provide fast diagnosis and credible integration coverage. Excessive end-to-end duplication raises cost without necessarily testing new behavior. Define five journeys: cited answer, corrected preparation, denied foreign claim, failed stream, and post-restart history. State the provider and database realism each needs."}, {"id": 280, "chapter": "Full-stack streaming and user workflows", "slug": "product-delivery", "title": "What should the completed portfolio demonstration show?", "text": "What should the completed portfolio demonstration show? Show one coherent user journey across authentication, evidence retrieval, streaming, source verification, claim preparation, confirmation, background indexing, and feedback. Then demonstrate a denial and a recovery case with supporting traces and tests. This connects frontend interaction with the concrete backend modules and the reusable Ragnor library. It demonstrates integration judgment rather than only the ability to call a model. A polished recording can hide unimplemented handoff, current-only policy scope, or in-memory deployment state. State those limitations and show the effective configuration. A narrow reliable application is more persuasive than many unverified agent features. Add remote agents or model adaptation only when the measured problem justifies them. Prepare a ten-minute demo with architecture, one successful task, one adversarial denial, one crash recovery, and a held-out quality/cost report."}, {"id": 281, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "What must change when scaling from one process to multiple replicas?", "text": "What must change when scaling from one process to multiple replicas? Shared sessions, idempotency, rate limits, jobs, migrations, and telemetry need explicit cross-process semantics. Process-local dictionaries, locks, and counters remain local even when replicas run identical code. The host offers database session storage and Redis-backed controls but defaults several settings to memory. Startup can degrade shared behavior rather than fail the service, so selected and effective backends must be visible. Adding replicas can multiply quotas, repeat model calls, and split conversational state. A load balancer does not make local state shared. Shared services improve continuity and introduce network dependencies. One process with honest limitations can be a simpler initial deployment. Test retries routed to another replica, session continuation, worker claiming, and global quota behavior during Redis failure."}, {"id": 282, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "Should migrations run on every application startup?", "text": "Should migrations run on every application startup? Startup migration is convenient for small deployments but couples readiness to schema changes and can make replicas compete to migrate. A controlled migration job followed by compatible application rollout is an alternative. _run_alembic_migrations runs alembic upgrade head through a worker thread and fails startup on errors. Centralized migration ownership is proposed hardening rather than the current policy. Concurrent DDL, long locks, and destructive changes can affect all replicas. A schema change can also prevent rollback to the prior image. Self-initializing services reduce manual steps; dedicated migration stages improve sequencing. Either requires tested failure behavior and appropriate database privileges. Design a rolling deployment with one migrator, compatible schema, application rollout, verification, and delayed cleanup."}, {"id": 283, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "Why does startup ingestion need separate operational treatment?", "text": "Why does startup ingestion need separate operational treatment? Ingestion performs provider calls and writes unrelated to accepting a web request. Running it during every replica startup can duplicate preparation, delay readiness, and couple scaling to source and model availability. The backend can ingest policies and procedures during lifespan and treats those ingests as best effort. Ragnor snapshots and locks reduce some duplicate mutation but do not eliminate all preparation cost. A service can start successfully with missing knowledge. Database connectivity does not prove usable policy evidence exists. Startup ingestion suits a sample corpus; a scheduled or operator-triggered worker gives clearer ownership, progress, and recovery. Start two replicas with an unchanged source, then with an unavailable provider. Report effective readiness, freshness, and duplicate preparation."}, {"id": 284, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "What does the current health endpoint prove?", "text": "What does the current health endpoint prove? It proves the API can execute a bounded PostgreSQL SELECT 1 check at that moment. It does not establish provider availability, current evidence, active workers, authorization correctness, shared state, or functioning trace export. health_check uses engine.connect with a one-second wait_for. Telemetry and evaluation provide different information and should not be inferred from that narrow probe. Calling this fully healthy can conceal an assistant unable to answer or index. Putting expensive evaluations in every probe can instead overload the service. Cheap probes suit frequent checks. Readiness and scheduled consistency checks should cover critical product dependencies separately. Define probe outcomes for database failure, provider failure, stale corpus, and a stopped embedding worker."}, {"id": 285, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "How should connection pools and worker concurrency be sized together?", "text": "How should connection pools and worker concurrency be sized together? Count API requests, background jobs, agent-session writes, migrations, and probes that need connections. Bound concurrency and pool waits so one workload cannot consume all capacity. Async I/O does not remove database connection limits. Each replica or worker may have its own pool. The NullPool option is test-gated to avoid cross-event-loop connection reuse; it is not the intended production throughput setting. Increasing replicas can exceed the total connection budget. Provider waits inside open transactions amplify occupancy even when CPU usage is low. Larger pools reduce local waiting until database saturation; smaller pools protect the database and need admission control. Calculate connections across replicas and workers, reserve operational headroom, and measure pool checkout latency under load."}, {"id": 286, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "How should container and dependency hardening be evaluated?", "text": "How should container and dependency hardening be evaluated? Review non-root execution, image contents, dependency resolution, vulnerability handling, secrets, and runtime privileges. A production filename or non-root USER is one control rather than complete supply-chain assurance. The Dockerfile uses a slim Python image, uv installation, copied application/test/migration files, and appuser. The manifest has exact pins and broad lower bounds, so clean builds can resolve different dependency versions. Without constrained resolution and compatibility checks, upgrades can break ADK events, tracing patches, or provider behavior. Build tools and unnecessary files also add maintenance surface. Strict locking improves repeatability and requires planned updates. Broad ranges support experimentation and weaken deployment reproducibility. Compare resolved dependencies from two clean builds and propose a tested update policy without changing the insurance repository."}, {"id": 287, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "How should worker shutdown and stale-job recovery be verified?", "text": "How should worker shutdown and stale-job recovery be verified? Stop accepting new work, allow bounded completion, release resources, and use durable job state for later recovery. A finally block helps graceful shutdown but cannot be guaranteed after abrupt process death. The drainer and host adapters support polling and stale reclamation. Safe resumption also needs ownership-aware completion and idempotent repeated processing. Reclaiming too early overlaps workers; never reclaiming strands jobs. A crash after external delivery but before completion marking can repeat delivery. Graceful draining improves continuity and slows replacement. Abrupt replacement is faster and demands stronger recovery invariants. Terminate a worker after claim, after embedding, and after delivery. Verify the next worker converges without duplicate logical effects."}, {"id": 288, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "How should an external provider outage affect the product?", "text": "How should an external provider outage affect the product? Distinguish unavailable generation, unavailable embeddings, failed retrieval, and degraded tracing. Preserve committed business state and expose retryable status. Do not fabricate policy answers or report unperformed actions as successful. Embedding jobs decouple claim creation from provider latency. The policy tool's empty-result fallback still needs richer error propagation to distinguish absence from infrastructure failure. Nested retries can exhaust pools and budgets. Switching providers can change tool and privacy behavior and must be an explicit evaluated policy. Structured status lookup can preserve useful service while generation is unavailable. Full-availability claims are inappropriate when essential evidence is missing. Define an outage matrix for PostgreSQL, Redis, embedding, generation, and tracing, with permitted operations and recovery behavior."}, {"id": 289, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "What is a meaningful disaster-recovery drill?", "text": "What is a meaningful disaster-recovery drill? Restore authoritative data, recover or rebuild derived indexes, reconcile jobs, validate ownership, and demonstrate post-restart application behavior. Measure actual recovery time and data loss against declared objectives. Restoring claims without matching vector and notification state can leave projections inconsistent. A known fixture should include completed and pending work and versioned policy material. Old jobs may replay and stale vectors may remain visible. Backups themselves also need secure access, retention, and credential-restoration procedures. Frequent drills cost effort and reveal dependencies that documents miss. Backup configuration alone is not proof of recoverability. Restore a fixture with a claim, pending job, policy revision, and archived conversation. Produce a reconciliation report."}, {"id": 290, "chapter": "Deployment, recovery, and production readiness", "slug": "deployment-reliability", "title": "What evidence supports a production-readiness claim?", "text": "What evidence supports a production-readiness claim? Show effective configuration, tested authorization and transaction boundaries, migration ownership, restore evidence, queue and latency measurements, quality limits, privacy policy, and incident ownership. State the workload and scope under which the claim holds. Static source review identifies mechanisms and gaps; it does not certify the insurance deployment. A readiness review executes the storage and user journeys that the product promises. A polished architecture or passing unit suite can conceal untested runtime behavior. Secure, scalable, and exactly once are claims requiring specific evidence. Readiness work is slower than a demo and establishes a defensible operational contract. A prototype may ship earlier with narrower commitments. Assemble a release packet with concurrency results, quality report, dependency inventory, privacy review, restore drill, and rollback plan."}, {"id": 291, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "What mathematical foundations should an AI full-stack engineer learn first?", "text": "What mathematical foundations should an AI full-stack engineer learn first? Learn vectors, norms, dot products, probability, conditional probability, cross-entropy, optimization, and basic statistics. Connect them to retrieval geometry, training, calibration, and evaluation rather than memorizing formulas without a system consequence. The hashing example, scoring helpers, profiles, and evaluation harness provide small observable experiments. Large-model training is not necessary to understand why mismatched vectors or misleading scores break a product. A number can look authoritative while measuring the wrong event. Similarity is not truth probability, and judge agreement is not calibrated decision confidence. API skills enable quick prototypes; foundations improve diagnosis. Alternate practical exercises with theory. Compute normalized similarity by hand and estimate uncertainty in a small benchmark. Explain what each number establishes."}, {"id": 292, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "How do calibration and selective answering differ from ranking?", "text": "How do calibration and selective answering differ from ranking? Ranking orders candidates; calibration relates predicted confidence to observed correctness; selective answering chooses when to abstain. Define correctness and harm before turning a score into a decision threshold. Ragnor exposes distance and ranking scores, while the host has an uncertainty ladder and refusal evaluation. Those mechanisms do not implement a universally calibrated coverage probability. A threshold tuned only on answerable questions can accept unsupported claims. Rare severe errors require separate treatment from average accuracy. More abstention can improve reliability among answered cases while reducing coverage. Evaluate the risk-coverage relationship with reviewed labels. Plot answered fraction against error rate across candidate thresholds. Separate outages, missing facts, and inapplicable policies."}, {"id": 293, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "What are hard negatives and why can domain retrieval need them?", "text": "What are hard negatives and why can domain retrieval need them? Hard negatives appear relevant but fail a material condition, such as gradual leakage versus a sudden burst. They test whether representation and reranking distinguish the condition the user's question depends on. A benchmark should include near-miss clauses and exclusions, not only unrelated distractors. Some exclusions are relevant evidence even when they do not support a positive answer. Binary labels can incorrectly mark a controlling exclusion irrelevant. Distinguish supporting, contradicting, conditional, and unrelated evidence when the task requires it. Rich labels cost review effort and reveal meaningful limitations. Easy random negatives produce cheaper and often flattering scores. Build a query with a positive clause, exclusion, unrelated clause, and ambiguous condition. Evaluate candidate recall and evidence completeness separately."}, {"id": 294, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "When should you adapt an embedding or reranker instead of changing the prompt?", "text": "When should you adapt an embedding or reranker instead of changing the prompt? Adapt representation when evidence consistently fails candidate retrieval or ranking after extraction, chunking, and labeling are correct. Change answer policy when useful evidence arrives but generation ignores it or overclaims. Separate stages let you locate the first failure. An embedding change needs compatible reindexing, query configuration, cache invalidation, and held-out evaluation. Tiny-domain tuning can overfit visible questions and worsen rare exclusions or other languages. Training leakage can conceal the regression. Adaptation may improve difficult matching and adds training and migration cost. Structured metadata or deterministic routing can be cheaper. Classify twenty failures by the first stage losing correctness and justify adaptation only after simpler repairs fail."}, {"id": 295, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "What do distillation and synthetic data add to a learning plan?", "text": "What do distillation and synthetic data add to a learning plan? Distillation trains a smaller system from a stronger teacher's signals. Synthetic data expands examples, paraphrases, or adversarial cases. Both require independent validation because confident teacher errors can be copied at scale. This backend does not train a distilled model. Its reviewed corpus and categories provide a sandbox for a separate experiment with an unchanged held-out benchmark. Generated questions can mirror source wording and overstate generalization. Generated answers should not become gold labels without review. Synthetic expansion reduces annotation effort and can reduce diversity or amplify bias. Keep a reviewed core as the anchor. Generate toy paraphrases, review them, and compare held-out quality before and after using the accepted data."}, {"id": 296, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "How do quantization, batching, and caching affect serving differently?", "text": "How do quantization, batching, and caching affect serving differently? Quantization changes numerical precision, batching groups execution, and caching reuses prior results under a valid identity. They optimize different costs and can change quality, waiting latency, privacy, and freshness differently. Ragnor provides batching and scoped embedding caches; quantization is an external serving choice. Chat idempotency caching has different semantics again. Cached answers may be stale after policy or permission changes. Aggressive batching can delay interactive requests, and quantization may change task quality. Optimizations reduce resources and add operational complexity. Measure successful task cost and tail latency rather than throughput alone. List what each optimization changes or reuses, its invalidation identity, and the quality tests it requires."}, {"id": 297, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "Why separate model quality from inference-system reliability?", "text": "Why separate model quality from inference-system reliability? A capable model can fail through timeouts, rate limits, broken schemas, streaming errors, or unsupported settings. A reliable serving path can consistently deliver poor reasoning. Measure execution availability and behavioral quality separately. LiteLLM, ADK, callbacks, retrieval, persistence, and SSE adaptation can fail independently. Their boundaries support targeted diagnosis. Replacing a model to fix a parser or transaction bug wastes effort and can introduce incompatibilities. More retries cannot repair consistently unsupported reasoning. Layered measurement requires instrumentation and identifies effective changes. One end-to-end score captures impact but does not locate cause. Create incidents in model, retrieval, tool, transport, database, and frontend categories. State which signal identifies each."}, {"id": 298, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "How should experimentation record lineage and reproducibility?", "text": "How should experimentation record lineage and reproducibility? Record code and dependency revisions, corpus, source versions, chunking, profiles, prompts, generation settings, judge configuration, and execution mode. Keep case identity stable without assuming temporary chunk UUIDs never change. This handbook pins two repositories and hashes sources. Application evaluation records should connect those identities with outputs and observed costs. Equal model names may hide provider changes. Broad dependency ranges or a context-proxy fallback can change metrics without an obvious application diff. Rich lineage costs metadata and supports meaningful comparison. A screenshot of one average is easier to share and cannot reproduce a result. Write a machine-readable experiment manifest distinguishing configured, observed, estimated, and unknown values."}, {"id": 299, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "What advanced topics should follow the core journey?", "text": "What advanced topics should follow the core journey? Study temporal retrieval, reranker training, calibration, multimodal extraction, secure workflow persistence, distributed agent tasks, serving economics, privacy-aware evaluation, and failure-oriented operations. Add each through a concrete benchmark or product need. Graph retrieval, A2A deployment, model training, attachments, and automated adjudication remain separate extensions. Their teaching examples do not establish production integration. Collecting techniques without mastering ownership, transactions, and evidence creates complexity with weak guarantees. Reading an API tutorial is not operational experience. Deep understanding of complete workflows builds judgment; broad exploration is useful when tied to falsifiable experiments. Pick one advanced track and define prerequisites, a toy experiment, a held-out metric, a failure test, and a stopping condition."}, {"id": 300, "chapter": "Model engineering and the advanced learning path", "slug": "model-engineering", "title": "What demonstrates completion of the full AI full-stack learning journey?", "text": "What demonstrates completion of the full AI full-stack learning journey? Build and explain the path from source bytes to authorized evidence, conversational intent to validated tools, reviewed action to atomic commit, and measured behavior to deployment and recovery. Defend alternatives with observations rather than slogans. The Ragnor chapters teach retrieval internals; the insurance chapters add host behavior, databases, agents, evaluation, observability, and product integration. Each roadmap stage has an artifact and acceptance criteria. Familiarity from reading can exceed practical skill. Production claims need real integration, security, load, and recovery evidence beyond static review. A sustained portfolio is slower than memorization and stronger evidence of competence. Finish one reliable product before adding orchestration layers. Deliver the capstone with a working assistant, held-out report, write tests, trace-privacy review, restore drill, and three defended architectural alternatives."}];
